        <p>PIES AND BRAWN</p>
        <p>CHINA TEAS.</p>
        <p>Tea d za Sir Andrew Clarke: For years, whenever my Cristomers have asked me for a Mild Tea, I have advised the Pure. old Fashioned China which I keep, and the advice has been appreciated. It is very satisfactory to them that such an erahrent authority as Sir Andrew Clarke, the London Physician, corroborates opinion. - Here is an extract from an Interesting lecture, delivered by Sir Andrew the other day, to the Students of the London Hospital "Tea is a blessed beverage. Ido not know what 7 should do without it. But there is tea and tea ania one of the teas I hare in my mind is the representation of all that is physiologically wicked. Igo about town a good 'deal holding consulta- tions here and there, and about five o'clock, when I get into a place, the lad, of the housc, will say to me : • Sir Andrew, you look tired, do let me give you a cup of tea.' I say ' themk ru very much'. But the tea has stood for half-an-hour, and she remarks, I know you do you not like it strong, Sir Andrew. and she then pours about a tablespoonful of the tea into a cup and fills theeup up with water, Now I call it positivearuelty to give tea like that to anybody and I hope you, gentlemen, will always set your face again t it. Tea, to be useful, should be first of au Black China Tea ; the Indian Tea which is being cultivated has become .' -so powertul in its effects upon the nervins system that a cup of it taken early in the morning, as many do. to disorders the nervous system that those who take it actually get into a state of tea intoxication, and it produces a form of nervous disturbance, which is most painful to witness. If you want to have, either for yourselves or for your Patients, tea which will not injure and which will refresh. get Black China Tea, putting in the right measure—the old fashioned teaspooxful for each person and one for the blessed pot. Then pour en briskly boiling water, and within five minutes you must pour at off again. or it will become wicked instead of good." Such is Sir Andrew Clarke 's opinion and advise. A few words of warning, however, are necessary to those who have f or- gotten what this old fashioned tea is like. The colour of the tea after it is brewed will be found tobe much paler than that now in use, and the taste may not be acceptable at first owing to its apparent insipidness ; but if its use is persisted in, it will very soon be found to be much more suitable for thekvstem, and if, in a week to two after its use, a trial is again made of the kind now in ordinary use, you will wonder however you tout,' have peen using it,and you will endorse Sir Andrew's statement. This old fashioned China Tea, as recommended by Sir Andrew Clarke, can be obtained now, as always or the last 35 years, at --</p>
        <p>I HOM AS rp A.IOLYNEUX HOUSEHOLD STORES, 37, 39, &amp; 41, MARKET-STREET, EARLESTOWN.</p>
