      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>'SELECTED READINGS.</head>
        <p>'SELECTED READINGS.</p>
        <p>CASE OF Svacranin ILLusioN.—Mr. Macnish, in the second edition of his amusing and instructive work on "The Philosophy of Sleep," relates a number of inteiesting cases of spectral illusion. The following, of which he himself was the subject, Is one of the most curious :—" In March, 1829," says he, "during an attack of fever, accompanied with violent action in the brain, I experienced Illusions of a very peculiar kind. They did not appear except when the eyes were shut or the room perfectly dark; and this was one of the most dis- tressing things connected with my illness; for it obliged me either to keep my eyes open, or to admit more light into the chamber time, they could well tolerate. I had the consciousness of shining and hideous faces grinning at me in the midst of pro- found darkeess, from whioh they glared forth In horrid and diabolical relief. They were never stationary, but kept moving in the gloomy background: sometimes they approached within an inch or two of my face : at other times, they receded several feet or yards from it. They would frequently break into fragments, which, after floating about, would unite—p:ntions of one face coalescing with those of another, and thus forming still more uncouth and abominable images. The only way I could get rid of those phantoms was by admitting mere light into the chamber, and opening the eyes, when they instantly vanished, but only to reappear when the room was darkened or the eyes closed. One night, when the fever was at its height, I had a splendid vision of a theatre, hi the arena of which Ducrow, the celebrated equestrian, was performing. On this occasion I had no;con- sciousness of a dark background like to that on *which the monstrous images floated; but everything was gay, bright, and beautilul. I wak broad awake, my eyes were closed, and yet I saw with perfect distinctness the whole scene going on in the theatre —Oucrow performing his wonders of horseman- ship, and the assembled multitude, among whom I recognised several intimate friends; in short, the whole process of the entertainment as clearly as if I were present at it. When I opened my eyes, the whole scene vanished like the enchanted palace of the necromancer; when I closed them, it as instantly returned. Bet though I could thus dis- sipate the spectacle, I found it impossible to get rid of the accosupanyieg music. This was the grand I march in the opeia of Aladdin, and was performed by the orchestra with more superb and imposing effect, and with greater loudness, than I ever beard It before; it was executed, indeed, with tremendous energy. This air I tried every etlort to dissipate, by forcibly endeavouring to call tither tunes te mind, buz, it was in vain. However completely the vision might be dispelled, the music remained in , spite of every effort to banish it. During the whole os this singular state, I was perfectly aware of the illusiveness of my feelings, and, though labouring under violent headache, could not help speculating upon them, and endeavouring to trace them to their proper cause. This theatrical vision continued for about five hours; the previous delusions for a couple of days." WHILST Admiral de Ruyter, in his expedition of 1664, was at anchor near the Castle of St. George, on the coast of Guinea, three of his sailors who were ashore went into a house where ardent spirits were sold, and, getting drunk, extinguished the lights, and began to fight with their knives. The landlord, who came in to separate them, received a wound of which he died; and there was no possibility of dis- covering the murderer, as all three denied it alike positively. General Valkenburg, the governor of the forts and magazines in Africa which belonged to the Dutch West India Company, warned the whole three to prepare for death, adding, that lots should be cast to determine which of them should be executed. He upon whom the lot fell was hanged. When it was thought he was dead, the rope was cut, and preparations were making for his burial, when signs of life were perceived. Means were used for his recovery, which proved suc- cessful; and a message was sent to Valkenburg to inquire what was to be done with him. The general answered that he must die, according to the tenor of his sentence; but De Ruyter was of a differ- ent opinion, alleging it was by no means certain that this unfortunate man was the perpetrator of the crime, as the lot might fall upon the innocent as readily as upon the guilty; that since it had pleased God to preserve his life as it were by a miracle, it would be wrong to deprive him of it; besides, that the punishment he had already suffered was suffioi- ent to expiate the crime, and to serve as an example. Valkenburg yielded to these reasons, and the sailor was spared. He was afterwards interrogated con- cerning the state of his feelings during the infliction of punishment, and declared ne remembered almost nothing that had happened that day, having, after sentence was pronounced, lost all powers of recollec- tion; except that, when he was thrown off the ladder, it seemed to him that he was falling into a deep well. THE severest example recorded in history of the punishment of a corrupt administrator of law, is that which Cambyses, king of Persia, caused to be inflicted on the person of Sisamnes, one of the royal judges. Having learnt that this magistrate allowed himself to be swayed by bribes, he ordered him to be flayed, and caused the tribunal where he used to sit to be covered with his skin. He then appointed Ostanes, the son of Sisamnes, as his successor, and made him take his place at the tribunal covered with his father's skin. It may be remarked, that however dreadful this example, it could have no permanent effect in the country where it was exhibited. During the reign of Cambyses, it might secure more impartial decisions; but avaricious judges would perceive that nothing beyond a temporary reformation was necessary on their parts. for such is the instability of the interests of the people under a despotic government, that the next prince might be as ready to go halves with his officers in their unjust exactions, as Cambyses was to visit the guilty with condign punishment. WHILE the Emperor Aurelian was besieging the town of Thyana, he swore that "he would not leave a dog alive in the city;" which caused the inhabi- tants to believe that they should all be put to the sword. When the place was reduced, however, he took a less sanguinary method of fulfilling his oath, , Issuing orders to kill all the dogs that could be i found. The young reader must not fail to observe, that, if Aurelian intended from the beginning to perform his vow literally, it was unworthy of a personage to use so paltry a quibble; and that, if, in making the vow, he employed the expression in its ordinary acceptation, as the people of Thyana understood it, it was still more unworthy of him to form and avow so bloody a purpose: in this latter case, the only praise due to him is, that he did not carry into execution his bad designs. FLOWERS and the perfumes distilled from them have a salutary influence on the constitution, and constitute a therapeutic agency of high value. Resi- dence in perfumed atmosphere forms a protection from pulmonary affeel ions and arrests the develop- ment of phthisis. In the town of La Grasse, France where the making of perfumes is largely carried on, phthisis is almost unknown, owing to the odorous vapours inhaled from the numerous distilleries. IN Iceland a large ring, variously formed of bone, stone, jet, gold, and silver, is used as a ratification of engagements. It is large enough to admit the groom to pass his four lingers and palm through it, In which hand he receives the bride's hand during the betrothal. Occasionally these ceremonial rings are placed upon the altar, and them o used. This may be a modification of an old custom prevalent In the Orkneys, where the contracting parties joined hands through a perforation or ring in a stone pillar. THE wdf arc of a society, and the justice of its arrang,erneats, are at bottom dependent on the character of its members. . . There is no political alchemy, by which you can get golden conduct out of leaden instincts.—llerbert Spencer. IT is by no means necessary for an engagement ring to have only one stone in it, nor are you con- fined to ariy particulftr stone, although diamonds are the greatest favourites. Sapphires, pearls, rubies, and turquoises are used, bu', n emeralds, as they signify " forsaken." The ring 14 frequent without stones at all, or if there are any they are deeply set, so that they need not be taken off the finger for washing, Sec. WHOEVER is conscious of faithful, earnest effort should regard his temporary failures with a calm and untroubled spirit. Regret them he must, learn lessons from them he may, but to sink into despair because of them is both unreasonable acti unmanly. A LOVING wife, rosy-cheeked chiluren, and a bright fireside are the quickest kind of assets to realise on in distressing emergencies.</p>
        <p>ADVICE TO 1110,rffEne I—Are you brokeu in your rest by a sick child suffering with the pain of cutting teeth Go at once to a chemist and get a bottle of MINI. WINSLOW'M SOOTHING SYRUP. It st ill relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It Is Perfectly harmless and pleasant to taste. it ['mutters natural, quiet sleep, by relieving the child trotr pain. and the little cherub awakes '' as bright as a button." It soot bee the child, If softens the gums, allays all pain, relieves wind, rogulates the bowels, and is the best known remedy for dyeentry diar- rhcea, whether.arising front teething or other Mrs. Winslow's soothing Syrup is sold by Aledinine deers every- where at Is. lid. ner bottle.—"lda.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Johnson, the late widow of the proprietor of the" Standard," who died at Brighton, unlike Miss Ingram, of the " Illustrated London News," or Mrs. Leslie, of New York, exercised no control whatever in Shoe-lane. Mr. Mudford was appointed per- manent editor of the paper by the will of the late proprietor. Mr. Alfred E. T. Watson, the dramatic and musical critic of the " Standard," is married to the daughter of the late Mr. and Mrs. Johnson.</p>
        <p>The grant of £lOO from the Royal Bounty Fund to Mr. James Dryden-I!oaken, the Cornwall post- man poet, is another evidence of Mr. Gladstone's interest in literary promise. When Mr. Hosken'sl volume of plays appeared six months ago,the it is said, was one of the first to read and praise them. Both Mi. Andrew Lang end Mr. Quiller- Couch have taken an interest in Mr. Hosken' ' and the latter is writing a preface for kis new volume of sonnets. "Q" spends a great deal of his time at Fowey, where the temperature in generally genial, and Mr. Hosken's home, lielston. is not ver4 far from Fowey. Of course, Fowey is the origin' lof "Q's" "Truy Town," and no doubt in time Mr. Hosken will embalm Helston in poetry. The Baroness Bolsoier, whose name has cro ped up so prominently in the Howard de Walden nese, and whose protecting legis has advantaged Lady Howard not a little is the daughter of the Bon. and Very Rev. Montague Browne, Dean of Listnore by the Hon., Catherine Penelope, daughter og the first Viscount de Montmorency. She was born on 8 Nov. 1834, and in June 1862, she married Lieut.- General Arthur Cavendish Bentinck, next in succession to the dukedom of Portland, but she missed the strawberry leaves by two years,l3er husband predeceasing the old duke by that period. As a solatium " Dizzy " created her Baroness Bol- sover, with remainder not to her own children but to her stepson the Duke of Portland, The Duke, however, is as her own ion, and no family is more closely united. The Baroness is a gentlewoman in the truest sense of the word, and she made herself greatly beloved by her neighbours of all degree, during the year she presided at Welbeck Abbey.</p>
        <p>Madame Venturi, who will be best remembered in England as the biographer and translator of Mazzini, is, we regret to learn, so seriously ill that her life is despaired of. Her husband, who died some twenty years ago,was prominent in the move- ment for the liberation of Italy and an intimate friend of Mazzini and Garibaldi. During Maziini's stay in England the family of Madame Venturi, the Asbursts, were very kind to the exile, so that he wrote," I cannot refrain from consigning to these pages the name of the dear, good, sacred family of Ash urst, who surrounded me with loving cares that —but for the memory of my own dear ones who died without me by their side— might have made me at times forget even exile." Madame Venturi has recently been occupied in preparing an edi- tion of Mazzini's letters, but her illness will pro- bably indefinitely delay their publication.</p>
        <p>Apart from her connection with the Italian movement, Madame Venturi was also a strong in- dividualist Radical, and took an ac:ive part in the agitation for the repeal of the Contagious Diseases Acts. She has also been intimately connected with the Personal Rights movement. Although not so good a speaker as a writer, she can address an audience with equal ease in English, French, or Italian. Her sister married the Right Hon. J. Stanstield, M.P. for Halifax. As she is now seventy 3 ears of age, Madame Venturi belonged to the generation which is passing sway. She was an in- timate friend of Thomas- Carlyle, alter whom her cottage in Chelsea is named, and she also numbers Mr. Whistler among her personal friends. Those who were associated with her in her work will read with borrow the news of her probably fatal ill- ness, and:will loin with us in hoping she:may yet recover.</p>
        <p>Members af Parliament occasionally receive queer commissions from their constituents. Lately an eminently respectable baronet was handed a rather peculiar box by his butler, and on opening it found that it contained, oral! things, a corset. With it was a letter asking him to " use pressure "to get it accepted and adopted by the Princess Beatrice, as it was to be sent to the Chicago Exhibition, and this would ensure its sale by thousands amongst the " democratic" ladies of America. The right bon.recipient (for 80 he is); who had just been read- ing the De Walden case, was afraid to keep the ' very compromising article of apwirel in his posses- sion, and sent it down to the serya2ts' hall, where he heard shouts of laughter as he closed his house door behind him on leaving for St. Stephen's. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Holmes, F.S.A., the Queens Librarian, is editing a comprehensive work on the historic bind- ings in the library at Windsor Castle. It will con- tain nearly one hundred and fifty facsimiles of the rarest bindings in the royal library, from a set of specially executed photographs. Many of the bindings represented are exceedingly choice, and almost unique. Others have a special historical interest attached to them. The introduction and the letterpress accompanying the plates are from the pen of Mr. Holmes, who is admirably qualified to deal with the subject. The volume (which will appear next month) will be dedicated to her Majesty. The frontispiece will be a photograph of a corner of the library in which an ancient mantle- Piece of the time of Queen Elizabeth stands sur- rounded by books. Messrs. Griggs are bringing out the book, which will be royal a quarto, and limited to an issue of tw- ' —dred and,fitty copies. ftProfessor Rudolf is an interesting representative of the older generation of scientists, who are unhappily fast passing away. He has just passed the limit of threescore years and ten. Had Owen lived, he would have been eighteen years his senior. Bunsen the famous inventor of the burner which bears his name, is still with us, and was eighty-two years of age on Wednesday. Sir Joseph Hooker is seventy-five, Sir George Stokes seventy- four, while Professor Tyndall and Mr. Ilerbert Spencer are seventy-two. M. Pasteur, the French pathologist, whose fame is even greater than that of his German confrere, is a year younger than Virchow ; Max Muller, his own compatriot, two years younger; Professor Huxley four years younger ; and Dr. Ogle a year younger still, he having only just p...ssed the maximum age limit of sixty-five permissible in the public service. Vir- chow may be briefly characterised as the English Huxley, having, like him, written not only techni- cal treatises, but also philosophical studies:and pamublets on educational science. Dean Letroy is making good progress in the reforms he is endeavouring to effect in the use of Norwich Cathedral. Since his advent to the city he has done much to popularise the services. Dean Lefroy is now an earnest believer in the old adage, "Early to bed and early to rise ' " but he was not always so. In his early days, as a journalist in Dublin, he was a most nototious student of the lamp, and it is worth while recording that in his busiest sub-editorial days be, was reading bard in preparation for holy orders. His successful career in Liverpool, where he gained the esteem and friendship of Dr. Bardsley, who is now Bishop of Carlisle, led to his preferment to the Deanery of Norwich. and there are many who regard him as marked out for still further preferment' He is a staunch defender of Evangelical interests, an elo- quent preacher, and a powerful platform speaker.</p>
        <p>CANADA, 1893.—we nave Just received a copyof a most interesting and well-illustrated official handbook of the Dominion of Canada, published by the Department of the Interior, of which the Hon. T. M. Daly, Q.C., is the Minister. Those who are looking for opportunities to invest capital, or those who are thinking of emigration are strongly advised to obtain a copy of this most inter- esting book. It can be had free on application to the Canadian Government agent at Liverpool, Mr. John Dyke, who has recently returned from an extended tour through Manitoba and the North-West territories. We understand that Mr. Dyke will give enquirers the result of his personal observations, anti will furnish introduc- tions in cases of need. The Dominion of Canada is just now attracting large numbers of settlers of the best classes, besides what may be called the professional agriculturists, there are numbers of people going out whose fixed incomes are not sufficiently large to enable them to live in the manner they wish in this country ; they will as a rule follow farming as an occupation, but what appeals to them is the opportunity that is afforded in Canada for indulging their sporting propensities. There are several packs of foxhounds in the Canadian North West, and the whole country is famed for the magnificent shooting and fishing it affords. To those wishing jor a new holiday ground no more profitable or or interesting tour can be suggested than that through the Dominion as far as British Columbia ; it can be done in a very short time, and compared with tours on the Continent and elsewhere at comparatively small expense.</p>
        <p>Lisimx C.ITHARTICUM PILLS. agreeably aperient, 9A., 1313. 2. 93. Of all Chemists.—Advt. KAVEI COKPOUND. a demulcent anodyne expectorant for Coughs and Colds, 90., 130. Of all Chemist!.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1893.</p>
        <p>practically she was a good musician. Her Majesty en- joys nothing more, than either Performing herself, or lis- tening to the performances of others. A ladies' cricket eleven lately played against a boys' school, and won easily. The vanquished boys did not take their beating with very good grace, and maintained that it was far easier to cricket in girls' than in boys' clothes, and the next game was played on a footing of equality as to dress. It resulted disastrously to the boys. In Germany every servant girl is obliged to own a little blank book for stamps. Once a week the mistress pastes in the book a twopence-halfpenny stamp, which is purchased from the Government. When the girl gets old, or should she fall ill, the stamps are redeemed by Government, so that the girl has a small sickness or old age fund. This custom was ordered by the Em- peror about two years no. Ginger Pudding. • - Half a pound of flour, a quarter of a pound of finely- cbopped suet, a quarter of a pound of brown sugar, two teaspoonfuls of ginger; mix all the ingredients together, dry, put into a buttered basin • and boil for three hours.</p>
        <p>Poor Man's Soup. Take one pound of scraps of meat, cut into small pieces, add to it two ounces of pearl barley (or rice), onions, carrots, turnips, and potatoes. Cut small, put Into a jar, and cover with three quarts of water' pepper and salt. Cover it down closely with greased paper or a plate, and bake in the.oven for four hours. This is a very inexpensive and nourishing dinner for a family. Any bones that one has by one should be added to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEMI NBLur,</p>
        <p>Some women will, given a certain air of style, and even elegance, to a simple toilette, while ethers look dowdy and commonplace in costly, but W-sesorted clothes. Materials should always be of as good quality as the means will permit, but if you cannot afford both, better have a cheaper nuLterial well made than a richer quality made by soffieone who does not really understand the first principles of the art of dressmaking: The woman -who always looks well-dressed is the one who chooses her toilettes with good taste, and with reference to other articles of her wardrobe. As a rule, she will choose quiet colours, knowing that they are not only the most economical but the prettiest, for nearly if not all occasions. For this reason black is a favomite colour with persons of good taste and limited means, but we cannot always go in such &lt;l. sombre garb, however elegant, and spring and summer call imperatively for lighter ones; they need not necessarily be of blue, pink or other bright lines, which are conspicuous in the extreme for street wear, as there are many ethers, all shades of tan grey, any pale mauve, which make up exceedingly well.</p>
        <p>Although the weather is anything but spring-like, one has only to pay a visit to the milliners' shops to see all varieties.of the "flowers that bloom in the Spring," reproduced with snob absolute fidelity to Nature, that it is sometimes a little diffieult to distiguish between art and reality. Spring hats will be trimmed, not with sprays or clusters of flowers but with tight little bunches, for all the world like those sold in the streets at a pinny—dotted about at intervals. Is dosen't so nnd very pretty, does it? But I can assure you that on the hats the effect is exceedingly good. One of the newest hats (a Paris Model), • if the .new mixed straw, which will be nitt4 worn this season, the crown being green, and ifie brim black, with two lines of green an4erneasli; it is trimmed with twists of green velvet, and at the left side with three black ostrich tips, while little bunches of long-stalked violets are placed here and there, one of them resting coquet- tishly on the hair at the side. Skirts are apparently getting wider and wider and they are not widcliin7 without a cause, but whether that cause will be the dreaded crinoline remains as yet a mystery. It is very certain as M. Worth says that women cannot wear a slrirt measuring nine or ten yards round without some support for it. It would be unhealthy for them to do so' as the weight upon the hips would neces- sarilybe very great. The crinoline, or some of it, would of course make a kind of frame upon which the bulky gown would rest, and if the skirts con- tinue to widen according to the dictates of inexor- able Fashion,.women must take to the crinoline in self-defence. The fashion-mon,zers love to throw us off the scent and lure us into the wrong track, fcCr the purpose of putting us right again. Just as we have all wasted our substance in buying yards and yards of material of which to make balloon skirts, and just as we have talked ourselves into the crinoline, it is quite within the bounds of pro- bability that all will be ohanged, and a move in the opposite direction will immediately take place.</p>
        <p>Amongst the new Pins Fashions is a very pretty visiting costume of orchid coloured satin brocade hi purple and gold, plain skirt with Watteau pleats escaping from a small corselet of purple velvet draped and diced under a large rosette. The sleevr are of velvet opening over a second sleeve of orchid coloured satin and the capole should be of velvet In shades to match.</p>
        <p>A great deal of progress in various directions has been made by women during the past year, and it is interesting, if not very convincing, to learn, from an annual review of women's work Just published, that the sex are showing a decided disposition to abjure matrimonial fetters and live in single blessedness. For every hundred matrons in England there are now one hundred and nine spinsters; so it is evident that some of us must perforce be content, whether we like it or not, to play the role of "old maid." The only thing for us to do in these circumstances is to make the beat of our opportunities and make as good a race for the matrimonial goal as possible. To say that girls are practically turning their backs on what, in wedding reports, is called the "hymeneal altar," is, I venture to think, a dream of the " emancipated " rather than a reality. I wonder now how many girls would vote for single blessed- ness if the question had to be decided by ballot. I can fancy I hear a unanimous cry of "I wouldn't," though I am not at all of the opinion that women ought to regard spinsterhood with absolute contempt. As I say, some must remain In it, and the best thing possible in the circum- stances is to he as jolly as possible, and be "dear auntie" to everybody else's children.</p>
        <p>Another very pretty feature o! the new Paris fashions is a five.o'clock-tea costume of reddish. brown silk and shot velvet. On the seams and Mtn la a rich embroidery of sills, and the corsage, Which is embroidered to match, opens over folds of otoom-coloured mous,eline de r,ie, folded waist- band, and large sleeves of velvet, with deep embroidered silk cuffs. Bonnet of brown and pettuoe•coloured velvet with marabout aigrette, 04 small tntts of flowers, match this dress exceed. heir well. Yours lovingly, Jams.</p>
        <p>Whilst the postal and telegraphic departments in Sweden have, at least for a time, discontinued the ap- pointment of female clerks, the Swedish State railways are increasing the number of female officials. On one day in January, six ladies obtained appointments under the State railway department. One of the favourite diversions of our Queen has always been music. In her youth Queen Victoria was noted for the sweetness of her voice. Her touch of the ano, too, was very fine, and both theoretically and</p>
