      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>Cauliflower Soup.</head>
        <p>Cauliflower Soup.</p>
        <p>Two caulifiowers, one small onion, a little nutmeg, a little carbonate of soda,four ounces of butter,three nints of stock, a teacupful of cream, a teaspoonful of sugar, pepper and salt to taste. Wash the cauliflowers' put them In plenty of boiling water, to which add a table- spoonful of salt, and half a teaspoonful of carbonate of soda. When nearly done, strain them, cut them up in rather small pieces, and put them in a stewpan with four ounces of butter, a small onion sliced very thinly, a little grated nutmeg, pepper, salt, and the stock. Simmer for half an hour with the lid off, rub through a hair sieve, and return to the stewpan. Add the cream and sugar, give one boil,and serve with croutons of fried bread. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>SUSPICIOUS DEATH AT THE WARRINGTON WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>SUSPICIOUS DEATH AT THE WARRING- TON WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>SUSPECTED STAYCHNINE POISONING.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an in- quest in the Boart,l room of the Workhouse, on Monday, on the body of Emily Passey, aged 28 years, who died on the previous Fridty in the ' Workhouse. After the jury were sworn, Mr. Brig- house said that they would hear some evidence, and then they would have to adjourn for further inquiries to be made. On Wednesday deceased came into Daly's lodging-house in Scotland-road, with an aged an who professed to be her father. She seemed to be in perfectly good health. The man paid for two beds. Next morning, deceased had fits and vomited very much and appeared to be very ill. It was also seen that she was pregnant. At about 9.0 a.m. the same morning, the man went out of the house, and had never been seen since. , Deceased was taken into the Workhouse and died on Friday. From the post-mortem examination by Dr. Spinks, assiited by his son and Dr. Braide, there were suspicions of poisoning by strychnine, and the stomach had been sent to the County Analyst. The police were also on the look out for the missing man.— James Da'y, lodging-house keeper, 37, Scotland-road, bore out the first part of the coroner's statement,and said deceased had a very wild look on Thursday morning. The man who came with her seemed to be between 50 and 60 years of age, and was grey. He had whiskers but had no moustache. Deceased was asked to have some tea on Thursday morning but she could take nothing.— Margaret Brennan corroborated Daley's statement.— Thomas James Garnett, relieving officer, said that the deceased was brought to his waiting room on Thursday. She vomited a: great deal, and also worked her mouth face. She fell on the floor and had to be lifted up again. Dr. Peaeocke was brought in, but he thought that she was suffering from the effects of drink. She had to be taken to the Workhouse in a cab.—Alice Cavanagh,nurse in the Workhouse, said that she was present whec de- ceased was admitted to the Workhouse. Deceased was suffering from fits, and Dr. Spinks was sent for at 1.50 p.m., and arrived at 4.10 p.m. The Coroner thought ,that two hours was a long time to get the doctor to come. Witness proceeding, said that deceased had fits during the night until 1.30 a.m., and was convulsive, and she died at 4.55 a.m., on Friday. The Coroner asked why witness did not send for the doctor again, but witness said that she thought ho had done all be could for her. From 1.30 a.m. on Friday to the time of her death de- ceased was only partly conscious.—Dr. Spinks said he ' was engaged at the time he was sent for, and could not go into the Workhouse immediately. On getting there, he found the deceased insensible. He attributed the fits, etc. to her condition at the time. He brdered her some medicine but she could not take it.—The Coroner : Did you consider her case a serious one. —Witness: I did not expect her to die so soon, and did not intend to come again unless I was sent for. She seemed to have become worse afterwards.—The Coroner (sarcastically) : Yes, because she died !—Witness with his; son and Dr. Braid% made a post-mortem examination,and they found that thebody was healthy, with no marks of external violence. The brain and vessels of the brain were healthy, and the lungs fairly healthy. The heart was full of blood, but otherwise healthy, as also was the liver. The stomach contained four ounces of liquid, but no food. The membrane was covered with a black pig- ment which could not be rubbed off. They preserved schste of this as they strongly suspected strychnine poi- Boning._ Witness said that he did not think deceased was suffering from poisoning when he saw her alive. The , body was rigid, and the skin seemed to be contracted. The symptoms which led him to suspect strychii ne poison- ing were contractionVot the .feet, and the head was also drawn back upon her shoulder. The inquest was adjourned to that day month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>IN DEFENCE OF MEDIEVAL EUROPE.</head>
        <p>IN DEFENCE OF MEDIEVAL EUROPE.</p>
        <p>1 In his inaugural Oxford lecture, Mr. Froude speaks as follows of the condition of the masses of the people in medireval Europe. I do not believe that the condition of the people in mediteval Europe was as miserable as is pretended. Ido not believe that the distribution of the necessaries of life was unequal as it is at present. Of liberty, no doubt, there is a great deal more going now than there used to be. In the 'Middle Ages there was little liberty for any one. Kings and peers, knights and vassals, villians and serfs, were held together , under strict bonds of , obligation. But the one thing certain is that between the lords and their feudatories there werte links of genuine loyalty which drew high and low together as they have not been drawn since the so-called chains have been broken. If the tenant gave service, the lord gave pro- tection. If the tenant lived hard, the lord had little luxury. Earls and countesses breakfasted at five in the morning on salt beef and herring, a slice of bread, and a draught of ale from a black jack. Lords and servants dined in the same hall and shared the same meal. As to dress, plain leather and woollen served for all ranks except on splendid ceremonials. Examine the figures of the knights on the floor of the ante chapel in the Temple Church in London. The originals of those forms were net brothers of the order or bound to poverty. They were the proudest' and most powerful of the English peers. Yet their armour is without ornament save the plain device on the shield. The cloak is the lightest and simplest. The heavy sword hangs from a leather belt, fastened with an ordinary harness buckle. All those knights lie there, so they moved when they were alive, and when hard blows were going they had an ample share of them. No fact of history is more certain than the peasants born on the great baronies looked up to those lords of theirs with real and reverent affection— very strange, Hone party in the contract had nothing but hardship and the other was an arbitrary tyrant. Custom dies hard, and this feeling of feudal loyalty has lingered into our times with very little to support it. Carlyle told me once of a lawsuit pending in Scotland affecting the Succession to a great ettate of which he had known something. The case depended on a family secret known only to One old servant, who refused to reveal it. A kirk minister was sent to tell her that she must speak on peril of her soul. "Peril of my soul," she said. "And would ye put the honour of an auld Scottish family in competition with the saul of a poor creature like me ?"—From Froude's In.augurat Oxford Lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CONORBOATIONALISM</head>
        <p>CONORBOATIONALISM</p>
        <p>Mr. Yonge said that the essential and fundamental idea of the congregational policy, briefly stated, was that a Christian Church was an assembly or brotherho,d of disciples of Jesus Christ and no others. Just as,according to the teaching of New Testaineiat,so a Christian Church might begin anywhere at any time,under any conditions when two or three personal disciples of the Lord agreed to associate in acts of Christia,s worship, and for the promotion of Christ's kingdom among men. The essen- tial truth of -their Congregational polity was that a new Testament Christian Church was a society of faithful disciples of the Lord Jesus Christ and 90 others. The Rector had given a very confused idea of the Congrega- tional doctrine upon this matter. Mr. Yonge criticised the Rector's views of their church membership, and contended that such a church membership as they had was the only One that had the slightest Scriptural war- rant. They confidently affirmed that the Now Testa- ment recognised and described no church except ;that which consisted of personal disciples of the Lord Jesus Christ. Another fundamental idea of Congregationalism was that each society of believers im Christ, was intend- ed to take the complete management of its own affairs. Congregationalism gave complete recognition to the dignity of the individual man. Congregationalism repudiated the idea of any State Establishment of a church. Entire freedom of all external control was the very vital air of Congregationalism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CURIOUS CLAIM AGAINST A HUSBAND.</head>
        <p>CURIOUS CLAIM AGAINST A HUSBAND.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens County Court, on Wednesday, before Judge Shand, Mary Baldwin an elderly woman, living , in Sutton-street, sued her husband, William Baldwin, of I Alma-street, for £5 2s. 6d., twenty and a half weeks' payments for the maintenance of her son, Percy Bald- win. Mr. Riley appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. Swift deiended. The parties were married in 1860, but, according to plaintiff, there had been frequent disagree- ments on account of defendant's intemperance, disgust- ing habits, and chronic laziness. The separated about three years ago, and during that time plaintiff had sup- ported herself by first being in service at the Isle of Man and by charin... In January, 1891, defendant told his son Percy, that he was not going to keep him any longer, and that he must go to his mother and bring word for how much she would keep him. A sum of 55. per week was fixed, and in March, 1891, a judgement for six weekly payments was obtained. In August the lad commenced working, and was earning is. 10d. per day. The arrears accumulated between March and August of 1891.—Mr. Swift submitted that a husband and wife could not enter into any contract for the maintenance of their children.. He cited the case of M'Gregory v. M'Gregory, in which it was held that a contract between husband and wife for the maintenance of children was invalid.—His Honour, after , perusing the case, said he did not think it bore on this case. An arrangement was ultimately come to, the plaintiff agreeing to accept a judgment for £2 10s. at 10s. per month.</p>
        <p>THE HOME RULE BILL AGITATION.</p>
        <p>LADIES PRACTISING TO SHOOT.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. S. Green, rector of Killigordon, Donegal, speaking at a Unionist meeting at Castlefin nn Monday night, said that last week he was in a house in a district where a young woman was practising with a revolver. Two other ladies said they were now able to shoot crows with rifles. If they could shoot crows they could shoot a village ruffian if he attacked them. They trusted in providence, but kept their powder dry. The remarks were cheered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>COTTON STRIKE.</head>
        <p>THE COTTON STRIKE.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING DISTRESS AT BURNLEY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting convened by the Mayor of Burnley on Monday to consider the acute distress in the town caused by the stoppage in the cotton trade several appal- ling cases of distress were given. One family had been entirely out of work for 18 weeks, a daughter had lost her reason, and the father had attempted to drown himself. There are several thousand operatives out of work. Several local committes offered to hand over their funds to the Central Committee, and it was ar- ranged that the Mayor should convene a town's meeting to organise a relief fund for the whole town.</p>
        <p>KAY'S no PILLS, specific in Neuralgia, Face-ache. Stid andl3W. ; postage ld. Of all Chcmists.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA is absolutety pure.—The Pubita AnA lysts bave submitted Catmour's COCOA to close tests Rad the result has always proved its great value as a svotata lug and nourishing beverage... Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CARD.</head>
        <p>A CARD.</p>
        <p>EVEREND JOSP-11--.HOLMES' improved In Mexican Prescription, as discovered in the hot table- lands of Old Mexico, is a guaranteed cure to all those wh) tmffer from the errors and indiscretions of youth, neivous weakness, physical exhaustion, and early decay. To intro- duce it into England, the Mexican Prescription will be sent free of charge, upon receipt of a self addressed stamped envelope. Only address: JOSEPH HOLMES. Remedy Co.. BlOOmsbury Mansion, Bloomsbury-square, London, W.C. Mention this paper.</p>
        <p>THE REV. JOHN YONOE'S REPLY TO THE RECTOR.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, the Rev. John Yonge gave an address in the Wycliffe Church, on Congregational- ism, being a respectful reply to instructions' thereon by the Rector of Warrington. There was a crowded attendance. Mr. Yonge said to deal with the history of congregationalism would need vastly more time than was at his disposal, and he must there- fore content himself with the general statement that it was not precisely as had been set forth in the Parish Church. In reply to the Rector's remarks, throwing the blame for the death of Henry Barrowe, John Greenwood and John Penry on the State, the preacher said next month Congregationalists would be asked to celebrate the 300th anniversary of the martyrdom of these men, they having a very definite conviction as to the causes which led to their deaths. The Rector held them up as traitors, while history had enshrined them on her page as confessors and martyrs for faith and principle. Tha Rector had been acting clearly within his unquestion- able right in speaking to his congregation and, by help ot reports in the press to all the people of Warrington, upon the distinctive facts and doctrines of the Christian denominations which held aloof from the Anglican Church, but they might in turn claim the liberty to dis- cuss the doctrines and assumptions of the church which he represented.</p>
        <p>THE RECTOR AND NONCONFORMISTS GENERALLY. The Hector was evidently and naturally of the opinion that apart from the particular errors and defects of any particular Nonconformist view, it was a serious evil for people to separate themselves irom the Church of Eng- land for any reason whatsoever. Holding this opinion, he wag endeavouring to convict all who differed from the constitution and claims of his church of being in the wrong. There was yet in him and in all who held his view, the old belief and idea of 300 years ago, that not to conform was a most grievous wrong doing. It was to imperil salvation, to dishonour Christ, and to retard religion. So intensely does he believe this that he was not sure but that if their amiable and courteous Rector had lived 300 years ago, he would have been—like Saul was in the cast of Stephen—" consenting unto the death S' of Barrowe, Greenwood, and Peary. And if so he would have done it in the honest belief that he was "doing God's service." It was noticeable that he spoke now in deprecation of persecution, and said that happily the mode of treatment of Nonconformists after these exectations of 1593 amelior- ated from capital punishment to banishment. But he clearly seemed to think that the ruling powers of these past days, were fully justified in strongly dealing with those who tefused to conform. Now he said, that the old spirit was by no means dead in :these _days. Did they not have aa illustration of it in the case of Mr. Knowles, Yew Tree Farm, in Cheshire, the other day ? He believed that persecution for religion as such could never become possible in this country again. But there are cases, not a few, similar to that just named. Aud- it was a prevalent idea among many churchmen, and especially among many of the clergy, that Nonconform- ists were very doubtful and questionable people, either tightly bound in the bonds of ignorance, or mischiev- ously inspired with the spirit of mere .contentiousness and discord.</p>
        <p>NONCONFORMISTS AND THEIR CONVICTIONS. It might, therefore, be well to take this opportunity to say that both them Nonconformist forefathers and the Nonconformists Of to-day claimed to be Credited with as cionscientious convictions for Noriamfformity as the adherents of the Established Anglican Church could claim for Conformity. Protestant Noncon- formity was founded upon certain great and definite principles, such, for example, as these : the right of private judgment and liberty of conscience, in opposition " to all human authority in matters of religion ; the supremacy of Christ,as the only head of ills Church,and the sufficiency of Holy Scripture as the Rule of Faith and Practice. Nonconformists preferred an infallible religion in the New Testament to a fallible one in the Articles and Prayer Book. They regarded Scripture as the only guide into the truths of Salvation, and the enlightened individual conscience as the best interpreter of Scripture It might quite probably be, that,in the days of the past, Nonconformists were a great difficulty to the ruling powers. But were not the ruling powers a great diffi- culty to Nonconfoirmists ? It was a conflict between tyranny and liberty. Men who were roused to fight for freedom were always difficulties to men who wanted to uphold it. And it might be, also that men freshly , escaping oppression, and coming suddenly themselves to power, became in turn oppressive to those from whom they had suffered. But the Rector said all that was "the temper of the times." We live,in days when all this should be completely altered. Churchmen ought to be perfectly well informed about Nonconformity. Its prin- ciples were now clear, and intelligible, and widely pub- lished. Nonconformity was not a synonym for ignorance. Nor was it a disguise for faction and cantankeroy,s- ness. Doubtless there had also been Churchmen of a very similar order. But the early separatist leaders were men of high scholarship and University training— men of great mental calibre, and of unquestioned chars: ter. Nonconformity sprang from intelligence, and from learning, and from conscience, and, from loyalty to truth and Christ. It survived to-day because it was be- ing maintained from the same sources! And when it was remembered that Nonconformitl, in all its various denominations comprised the majority of the British peo- ple, surely it savoured not a little of ,what looked like arrogance when Anglican clergy and ,their followers could so confidently assume the monopoly of truth and right.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>I DARKIATOOD.</head>
        <p>I DARKIATOOD.</p>
        <p>A TALE OF LONEMOOR.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. HARRIETT LEWIS, author of "The Sundered Hearts," "The Bailiff" Scheme," "The Double Life," &amp;o</p>
        <p>Caen.= XVIII. DRIVEN FORTH.</p>
        <p>It seemed indeed as if Ronald Chilton was a false lover, and that he had wooed and "rode away," like him of the ballad.</p>
        <p>Gwen waited for him that evening, watched for him the next day, and the day thereafter, but he did not come.</p>
        <p>'No message came from him. He seemed to have dropped completely out of her life, leaving an awful yawning void. She had told him to give due reflection to his proposal, fearing that he was acting upon impulse, and out of a chivalric pity for her. He seemed to have taken her at her word, to have reconsidered the matter calmly, and to have repented his haste. When a week had passed and no tidings had come from Ronald Chilton, GM en gave him up in her own heart, but she did not blame him. Nor did she deem him false or inconstant. With her history she knew herself, no fitting match for him, and submitted with proud humility to be thrown aside as valueless. •</p>
        <p>The butler secretly believed that the story he had told Mr. Chilton had put an end to the young gentleman's visits. "It's just as well, Maria," he said, when alone with his wife. "He is of a great family in Berk- shire—the Chiltons of Chiltom. His father is a vis- count. His mother was a duke's daughter. If Miss Gwen were the lawful and acknowledged grand- daughter of Squire Max kham she would be no match for him. Being what she is, of course, he could not marry her on any terms. I blame myself that I did not tell him at the outset ; but I sup- posed that he would hear something of all this gossip about Miss Gwen. It is fortunate that she was not interested in him !"</p>
        <p>"How do you know that she was not ?" inquired the housekeeper. "Miss Gwen doesn't wear her heart upon her sleeve. Her hurt may be deeper than we can guess. We have been to blame in allowing his visits, John. It seems as if everything, we do is a mistake."</p>
        <p>"Why don't we hear from the squire ?" muttered Mr. Quillet. "Can he be angry with us, and refuse even to answer our letter? Is he coming home Has he written to his solicitor to turn us out ? His silence may mean. harm to us." _ _ _</p>
        <p>The old house at Lonemoor seemed full of troubles jn these clays. Even the servants shared the general gloom. The Quillets were wretched with appre- hensions, but Gwen's burdens were hardest of all to bear, and still she bore them in a proud silence. The housekeeper resentfully called her insensible. She little dreamed of the sorrowful nigho s, the anguished thoughts, the horrible torture that young sold endured in a pitiful patience. A few days after tbe disappearance of Ronald Chilton, Gwen sat one morning busy at her em- broidery in the sitting room in which Squire Mark- ham was first introduced to the reader. She had abandoned the drawing-room of late. This gloomy apartment, with its dusky oak wainscoting, its sombre furniture, its dreary, dingy, family pictures suited her present mood better. ,</p>
        <p>The dom.: opened abruptly, and Claxton Orkney appeared on the threshold.</p>
        <p>wen started up with an impulse to retreat. Mere was no other door than that in which he stood, and she moved towards the bellpull. "Don't ring," said young Orkney advancing into the room. "I must apologise for entering unan- nounced, but the maids are not visible, and Mr. and Mrs. Quillet are in the kitchen garden. I beg you to grant me a brief interview, Miss Winter. I have something of importance to say to you." His manner was respectful. Gwen halted, re- garding him quietly and expectantly. "I behaved badly to you, Miss Gwen, that day upon the moor," observed young Orkney, fluently, as if repeating a lesson he had well learned before- hand. I desire to apologise to you for my con- duct.''</p>
        <p>The girl bowed. "You look pale and thin," continued young Orkney, advancing a little nearer. "All this trouble is telling heavily upon you. Let me take fou away from this neighbourhood. I love you, Miss Gwen, and I offer you honourable marriage. Will you be my wife ?" It is impossible." "Why impossible ?" and her suitor's face red- dened. "You will not be obliged to meet my family. I will take you directly to London. I am respectable, and can earn a good support for you.,, "It is impossible," repeated Gwen. "I do not like you, Mr. Orkney, and I could never marry 3' °it • But, you do 'like' your recent visitor, Mr. Chilton, cried Orkney, sneeringly. "I suppose you'd marry him if you had a chance ! But, after visiting you nearly every day for a month, and giving rise to no end of gossip, he has abandoned the field to me. He was only amusing himself in this quarter. And, having amused himself suffi- ciently, he left Yorkshire last Tuesday night—a week ago." Gwen s hand dropped to her side. Her face grew white; her heart seemed for an instant to stand still.</p>
        <p>Ronald had left Yorkshire; she had not thought that he had gone. "Why, you didn't really expect to win him l" ex- claimed Orkney in amazement. "Do you know who he is? He is the Honourable Ronald Chilton, son of Viscount Chilton—a regular nob—heir to one of the richest properties in England. It can't be possible that you have ever for a moment dreamed that he would marry you !"</p>
        <p>Gwen did n'ot even resent this insult. She was stunned by the announcement of Mr. Chilton's departure. Yet what had she expected after his long awl significant silence of the past week? And Ronald had gone away on Tuesday night— and it was on that very Tuesday that he had told her that he loved her, and asked her to become his wife! He had departed in haste, without a word to her of his intention It was only too clear that he had repented his folly and given her up. "You have made a great mistake, Miss Gwen," said Orkney "Mr. Chilton was a sportsman, and you were fair game, that's all. He will forget you in a week and next year he'll marry a highborn lady and laugh at all his boyish follies. But you need not wear the willow for him."</p>
        <p>Gwen had sunk into a chair, a great blackness obscuring her vision. She did not faint, but her pallor was like that of one long dead. Orkney's latest words were to her but an unintelligible mur- mur, and much that followed she did not hear, but finally his speech again became intelligible, and she listened with nerves that quivered as under a sur- geon's knife.</p>
        <p>•11. was returning from Penistone on horseback; Orkney was saying, "when I met Mr. Chilton on his way there. He was mounted also and seemed in haste. I had heard his name and rank, and con- cluded to apologise to him for that scene on the moor the day he knocked me down. He is the son of a viscount, and will be a lord himself some clay, and I've respect for titles," acknowledged Orkney, frankly. "I stopped him and apologised. I noticed that he seemed agitated. He accepted the apology, I suppose, for he bowed coldly, and was about to pass on, when I said that I must speak a few words in justification of myself. And then I told him, YOU know "—just here the young man beeame embarrassed—" all about your history, and what people said,and so forth, and that you were not looked upon exactly as other young women were, and that sort of thing, and that I considered myself justified." "What did he say ?" asked the girl in a strained voice, scarcely above a whisper. Orkney rubbed his cheek instinctively and uncon- sciously, as if to relieve the blow of a riding-whip which Mr. Chilton had planted there; but Gwen did not observe the movement.</p>
        <p>"What could he say 7" demanded Orkney, with a forced laugh. "It was true, and he knew it. He answered me that 'Miss Winter was nothing to him, and that if I chose to make amends to her for my insult the field was open to me.' That is why I am here this moment. Mr. Chilton has withdro.wu from the field. Is there not a chance for me ?"</p>
        <p>The speech attributed to our hero by the bailiff's son was false in every word, and had been inven- ted by young Orkney; but Gwen was in no condi- tion of mind to analyse it and discover its falsity. Besides, the fact that Ronald had gone, and with- out a word to her, seemed to guarantee its truth. "Will you not let me offer you a home 7" con- tinued Orkney, as she remained silent. "Will you not become my wife 7" "No, no," cried the girl, with a shudder. "Leave me. I never want to look upon your face again." Orkney bit his lips in a sudden anger. ," Do you know that no answer has been received from Squire Markham to the letters my father and the Quillets sent him ' he demanded. "lily father thinks the packet has miscarried, add he has resol- tO laws you expelled from this bens°. _The</p>
        <p>mlicitor will. probably co-operate with him. .They intend to visit you this very day. You will he turned out of Lonemoor. And, by Jove! my father and the lawyer are coming now. I see our waggon- ette approaching. Miss Gwen, I offer you a last chance Take it, and I'll defend you from these enemies of yours." The girl commanded herself by a strong effort- In the approach of this new danger she regained her coolness and courage. She seemed brought to bay. All the world appeared arrayed against her, but she would defend herself to the last. _ _</p>
        <p>"Cease to persecute me with your offers of mar- riage," she exclaimed. "I have said that Ido not like you. Shall I speak yet plainer? I fairly loath the sight of you. I would die sooner than become your wife. Now you have my answer." This passionate outburst served to inflame Ork- ney's rage, but before he had time to respond the waggonette rolled into the yard. He was in the midst of an appeal to her to recon- sider her answers and to allow him to defend her from the attack about to be made upon her, when the bailiff, the lawyer, and tne Quillets burst int° the room. The girl was standing like a statue, white and immovable. Young Orkney was flushed and eager- His father's glances fell upon him. "You here l" the bailiff exclaimed. "As you see," replied the son. "I have just mad° Miss Winter an offer of marriage, and I am waiting in the hope of a favourable answer." This cool statement inflamed the anger of the bailiff to an unreasoning rage. He bad come to Lonemoor with the lawyer, bent upon the expulsion of Gwen without any further delay, being incited thereto by the female members of his family; an his purpose knew no faltering from this moment nt his sons defiance. _</p>
        <p>"You hear 7' he exclaimed, turning to the law yer and Mr. aud Mrs. Quillet. "You can all see now that lam right-in my demand. This girl should not remain here another day. What right has she here ?—a pauper—the (la:lighter of a waua- ering outcast—what place has she in a respectable country house like Lonemoor ?'' The lawyer, a grace, elderly gentleman, with o kindly face, interposed to stem this torrent of words. But the bailiff, usually cold and cautious, believing his family honour at stake, was fierce in the maintenance of his cause. " We have come here, have we not, to settle this matter ?" inquired Mr. Orkney. "If we have, We must let no foolish scruples stand in our way. We have failed to receive any answer to our letters to Squire Markham, dispatched nearly six meeks ago. My opinion is the packet went astray. Shall I wait another month, until we can write again eo° receive an answer?"</p>
        <p>"I think it would be best," 'said the lawYer: "Another month of waiting cannot do any berm: "But I think it can,' exclaimed the bailid quickly. "This girl has no right here, but she is rioting on the squire's property, and making Lone' moor a scandal to the whole country side. he hes had a young gentleman to visit her every day for is mon,th. She receives my son. She dresses sod conaucts herself as a young lady—as mistress of this house. I refuse to be a party to this waste of our employer's property. I demand that the girl be sent away this very day." The butler and the housekeeper made protest, but they were growing weary of the contest; were filled with doubts as to the wisdom of their course; and wished vainly that they had never encumberees themselves with the girl. If they had followed the example of the squire, and abandoned her at the outset, sent her to an asylum, or something of thil sort, it might have been better for themselves an for her—so they thought now in their perturbation- And Gwen, preternaturall3 sharpsighted, reed their thoughts. "Jam more to blame than anyone else in alloW ing the visits of Mr. Chilton," said the housekeePer' " Miss Gwen would not have received him but for my counsel. I have generally been in the N°lll, during his visits. Miss Gwen has alway been ver) gentle and tractable.</p>
        <p>' - The bailiff sneered. "If you choose to set up a pauper as your idol, and worship and serve her, you can do so, ' he - "but you mustn't expect the rest of the world t° worship her also. I repeat, her presence here 1014 scandal, Lonemoor is become a byword. The very, Hodges sneer as they pass it. Let her go away she has a common sense of decency. I wonder Os° she can stay here a day or an hour under the cir- cumstances, knowing who and what she is. Gwen had beard all unmoved. Now she broke her silence, speaking haughtily, with head uplifted, sna her dark eyes glowing with scorn at her enemies. "I have remained at Lonemoor until now bowie" Mr. aild Mrs, Quillet desired it," she said, in a cle&amp;rt cold voice. "They brought me up. I owed a oer- , tam n amount of obedience. I have been obedient. I have rcinair:ed with them when a stay here WI been worse than torture; bu t now I must think an" act for myself. I refuse to allow Mr. and Mrs' Quilletto ho persecuted any longer u pon my account. They will be glad to bend of me. Mr. OrkneYp you need have no further fears or anxieties upplirl account. I shall leave Lonernoor immediately. A Young Orkney prate ted. His father express his satisfaction. The lawyer's face showed ll° approval: Even the Quillets, while dismayed egoi' hibitecl a certain relief at the announcement Gwen's resolve. "Ent where will you go ?" cried the housekeeper' "Stay, Miss Gwen, until we can hear from the' squire. Or, if you must go, we will go with Y°' We cannot let you go alone." "I will go as any mother, did," said Gwen, srit„ii unavoidable bitterness; " and I would to Hest- that I might go to iny death, as she did to 101 Mr. Quillet, will you order the chaise for Ina? must go at once—within the hour l" This was her decisicn, and nothing could ektt't It. The lawyer approved It. &amp;could not see tu3d, her further stay at Lonemoor would do' anY Gwen went up to her rooms to prepare for her parture, and Mrs. Quillet attended her. An hour later the chaise drew up at the Gwen's small trunk was put in, Gwen herse travelling costume, took her seat, and Mr. Quiha placed himself beside her and drove away direction of Penistone. And so the girl in storm and bitterness, with tesbe burden of her mother's disgrace upon her, Paesba. out of Squire Markham's house like her mother 0 fore her, to begin her battle with the worid. W°ll her battle end as her mother's had ended! (To be continued.) ANIMAL PARADOXES. th° Perhaps no 'birds spend more of their lives 04iag wing than parrots and pigeons, the latter via also among the most graceful and rapid ,04 Of Inhabitants of the air. In New Zealand a sPer": tbs parrot is found that, finding its food entiralY 00altbe ground, has lost the power of flight. It differs frebeieg rest, of its family only in this particular and in otbog almost voiceless. Among recent breeds of pigernter 0,1 parlour tumbler, which has not only lost the P°:',oll' flight, but has very nearly lost that of walking 6',„iveh Its queer motions when it attempts to walk hatl°oll!' it its name—the tumbler. "As thick as the "''NteSl' dog's back" expresses nothing in Mexico, for the, sni can dog is utterly devoid of hair on his back where else. The hot climate having rendered it litmus, Mother Nature kiaelly divested him of it;,our does "the little busy bee improve each shining " thatt in that country. On the contrary, it soon learnetc for as there is no winter there, there is no necessl4jto r' laying i,n a store of honey, and degenerates.At be thoroughbred loafer. "As big as a whale rather small, as there is a species of tne cctecea,? hardly three feet long. "As cunning as a fox initita• have sounded idiotic to the discoverers of ICarhscstoPl6l They P.aincl foxes in large numbers, but sc ober, because they had never before seen an enemy, tn3ther could be killed with clubs. The " birds of a re%auiii that "flock together" do not belong to the l';';;;iag family, as they are entirely destitute of feather, for a covering a kind of stiff down. Another Pe_lter, peculiarity is that it swims not on, but wider:liar never keeping more than its head out, and wherpf-rolii coining to the surface at such brief and rare rr,,, 00' that an ordinary observer would almost cert3ioy,ooo take it fore fish. Ducks swim the w.,rld over, bir7,ol3a do not. In South America a domestic species that cannot excel an ordinary hen in aquatic acc°,7“:,,ter ments. It has lived so long in a country vghere ste-• an. only found in wells that it has lose its aquatic ta._,,s not, abilities entirely. "As awkward as a crab ' ccerrab is apply on some of the South Sea Islands, for a_ „e ma° found there that not only runs as fast as an avers&amp; but climbs trees with the ease of a schoolboy.</p>
