        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>r r Sseeretaries O f Football Chubs are requested to note that heePa"cirt of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the Wet ?face. Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than ten °Q. on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>--- Coemi/lerETDRS should procure the 1893 Pocket Diary and Ipf,,Laariton published by Geo. G. BUSSEY &amp; CO. It is full of , IgA.Vation and has the requirements of an ordinary Di,rv. bound gilt edges, post free 6d , from the Sports u ml r4et°l7, Peckham Rye, London.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON v. ST. HELENS RECREATION. A record " gate " has to be chronicled as the result ofthe first meeting of the above clubs, which took place at Wilderspool on Saturday. Long befnre the adver- tised time to kick-off-3-30—the route to the popular enclosure was lined with enthusiasts, and it was 'estlin- ted_, that when operations were colnmenced there would ' - nee on 9,000 people present. The day was beauti- fallFflee, and the heat was tempered by a refieshipg wA,...i'ul• Originally the teams should have inch last Nov. but the dreadful scourge of smallpox which the„- reigned in this district compelled the St. Helens authorities to postpone the match untinthe last month °t the s'ason. Both teams were strongly represented, a all • ... Were received with hearty cheers by their different 13Ittit509 on entering the arena, the Saints having brought with them nearly 1,200. ' aha b, ~. Ashton won the toss, haeir-.-oe, advantage of the breeze and the sun at his th.. oeddon kicked off, and Heesom piloting back, L besi_arst-named and Webster next exchanged, the result 1 ht. r2g that half-way was the scene of operations. The BeS' eeriOlolage formed, saw the homesters packing and liatae..w..leg ia workmanlike manner, and Jolley dribbling ' , t. utifully reached Singleton, who received safe atten- , reea at the hands of Ashton. The Saints' quarters were and maintained, and although Pilkington tried ..0 0 Fair. Turner successfully grassed him at the ; tr afen,d time of asking The two Bates now demons- ztaeheir smartness,-. and thrice over one or the other b, svithie an ace ef scoring. James Bate next threw 1- iegebster, who failed at the rather high pass, and the i baci,t' u,. ere afforded a slight relief. Jahn Bate kept the ."tilsilv employed, and some useful forward work befo-pl„enail• l, Rands and Nevins saw Seddon well floored could respond. Ellison got the oval out of ; ft,,-,Ser, but the next minute saw Evans take a chance 01,;,'" a long throw-out. He defied the attempts of the cliv'nkairion until near the line, when he was challenged ' hitet.'ingleton, but the Warrington centre was ably --ed up by Bandies, who took the pass finely and Fiv " iiiieeckineg 11 Scored near the posts. , m 'flutes had been played, and, with Heeso : goal,Warrington had a majority of five points, ,1 Sieirscill Made, his mark from the re-start, and a lofty ' sat, again placed the visitors back on their line: James ' saViished to the line, but Singleton luckily saved, a relief by Seddon was ably met by Evans. I th 'n the Bates, aided by Donohue and Thorniley,forced 4 e c,a te " roe and John Bate eventually passed across to esete who at th Scored a rattling try cmner, and loud cheers met Fair. Turner when : gtelrpiaced the magnificent goal from touch. The home- id not relax fheir energies, and playing in an irres- i aftere planner, Singleton was only just in time to save, ' 'well' °011e5", Ashton, and John Bate had dribbled ' l'sbi The drop-out was charged, and Ashton forced , Vngton into touch well within his own 25. The lets now displayed some smart passing, and looked and breaking through, but Webster raced across , Sehi succeeded the Saints rushed the oval past Boscow, and in accounting for Ellison. Not to be !. vtor,lebeesdte,r raced up and kicked dead. A scrimmage was 1 holte.e.d on the line, and Evans upsetting Seddon, the I ia a ~,"' ierwards responded to the calls made upon them ! oheesallant manner, and successfully placed the visitors ! Ititeere on the defensive. John Bate put in a neat (' Capsid Singleton apparently under-estimated the , gs3ss2 ities of Webster, who raced up at top speed and h,zt ed the Saints' custodian in fine style. Higham was Bohafi:t fault and Warrington were penalized,but Nevins, ' WhieV(', and Fair. Turner followed with a long dribble, ' hatt a inost led up to James Bate scoring. Boscow next V YeesomtT at goal, and a second minor was set up. the,,,-;and Singleton exchanged kicks with advantage south svarringtonian,and Webser next put the finishing tlaot„,!°, a clinking display by the two Bates, and al- the 13507flote1d on the line he succeeded in grounding, but the lead. Was overruled. Success, however, attended %sal `r3 a minute later, for James Bate getting pos- '3ll. he with rerea y. Bluffed his Opponents but Ilef,,e'uom cleverness,and got over near the posts, j°ll.la, BaTerlthia time was at fault with the kick at goal. 43`ang dar,--xt Passed to Heesom, who was upset when illatetrbeeacith --ber°tla, and following this let-off the visitors ; °vat to Boscow, whose reply caused a hasty only f, air. Turner ied next dropped at goal, and Webster zulitleaTt. to get up by about a yard. Evans was pro- two f' "oe more, whilst a rush, headed by Ashton, the Baipt.urners, and Jolley, almost ended in disaster to the ttea i";, Heesom next dribbled along the popular touch, Pa- avving a splendid though futile shot at goal by tr. Turner, the with u, ; Interval was Reached Warrington leading by 2-1-4 to nil. On resuming, eary:.aints at once opened with attacking, and Seddon on narrowly missed registering edropped goal. te-,Wind had increased, and the homesters relied chiefly eee'refensive tactics to bring them out of the fray sue- Webster saved magnificently one rush of the bs,-,:ra forwaids, and then came a unique bit of passing tett`n,o Bates, Evans and Heesom, the latter finding half-way. A free kick against Boscow wastnet hi, „amesßate,and Ashton succeeded in getting away,but Vass to Jolley was a shade forward.Seddon next inter- -1)a71, ,a Pass and reached Boscow, who tackled the oehauecs.' back when looking all over a scorer. 'Jolley, clap e, and Frank Turner rushed the oval out Of tapigdeir, and from a series of long kicks minors were 3' sent UP. Seddon end th :Again Dropped at Goal, It 1,-e •ball struck the upright, whilst a few minutes a'n!ro "„er again made a huge bid for a try, but failed to sviieu - for Fair. Turner. Following the fourth minor s'aseda uSefel dribble by Thorniley, and Warrington in- aai• lr !or the first time this half, but the effort was 'l,,Doileg`;,t,°ed off by Singleton and Ellison. John Bate c"* ":• ,ilson repeatedly, and then Boscow a ppeared to bsette. - ,oilemma, and the Saints attacked in strong :clan 4nother minor followed, and from the drop-out ,;4topedilarl splendidly, and the home goal narrowly , Isiah, This was the last exciting incident, and the oaiht Sr of the play was of a give-and-take nature,the gt,s,,Ting the best of matters. Final score : War- -2.1.4 ; St. Helens Recreation, 0-0-5. Teams : 4a3,40,-Zail."..on : J. Boscow, back ; J. W. Evans, F. Hee- -411 tsa.s..s. Webster, three-quarters ; James Bate and vria,r ,1-e, half backs ; 11. Ashton (captain), Fair. k. Ita'n',P•ulc Turner, W. Nevins, J. Jolley, T.Donohue, J. 'l'. Thorniley, and J. Higham, forwards. dens It n ecreatio • J Singleton, back •J. Ellison,J. 13'411 °W-i'l and Molynetir;' three-quarters; J. Pilkin,gtou it%a ,e 4, j 8,41. , half hacks ; J. Mo,tisd,ale E. Potts, T. J. t0,,8, and 1 -4'aPrnan, R. Sidles, P. bildi: T s, J. Briers, R. 'll, oft si • Briers, forwards. Mr. Hugh Hughes, Run- si, -iciated as referee. iskßaeut th - lilte,,, It- 40N A. V. ST. HELENS RECS. A.—This match 1 on the City-road ground, in beauntitfuel Virs.e.-_ I (Vine, a'-',,er,e. was a strong wind blowingedwol the choice heretla h,;"'"; the side that had the luck t tl'-' a. big advantage. Unfortunately for. War- 11‘;51'e disn7 lost the toss, and in conjunction with the qt, giarsiovantages had the sun, with almost tropical 114""rried, ng in their faces. Although the kick off 44enDSon tSerne smart play by Massey, Monks, and bat o fly ~;;°ll,tlcl the game being contested at midfield. ir 11' -cabin' b the Rees recovered the lost ground set e l'iSit 'y • •• 41 4Res ors, notwithstanding the immense disad- taPses. s^oPosed ne them showed up promisingly, and illbe to %nu Potts by some capital play carried the war. qt:",tht4 hOrne 25. A fly kick then travelled right Is e arrington quarter, but Tease made i! grand_ 41:atdsn,_(1 receiving assistance from Dakin, Bailey and cie"tecl ': Telt the leather to midfield. Lawless supple- effort by forcing the Rees, into their trs' adv ' hut kicking by the hoinesters quickly nullified cltanfta„ge• Warrington, who had started well, ie4k'rl by° Lau away, lacking their usual dash, and from rep,„a st,o Saints Pickering scored for Crossley to Ishfrciera4l goal off touch. The game degenerated hilt-e be ,Y, football being conspicuous t by its absence, hut ".„,enie team showed an utter disregard for he festiot le -narnes on one occasion saved finely. Then th:11 tetedi and Crosby kicked another grandgoal sat gtot,O, and with this lead half-time arrived. With ci- biltM ~..and elements in an their favour it was antic-i- -t hie v",'`varrinzton would show up more prominently ds'tt, an',orts of the first half had had an exhausting 14 t°4l' d the team' playing la in much below form, could In' likitr Ingtosn Wltr 1 the chances that came in wasted w ti__ pressed a lot, but the Bees. fully Ilti' Idexlre by kicking into an adjoining bowling green tla 04 anOson and Potts had hard lines in not scoring.... hs_lae,ts ?ther occasion Davies made a grand rush to 've , Vel, being floored a yard off the line. The play, talltier,"l. Warrington was weak, and with the - 4teer'ati: ° a smart try by Potts nothing was scored, pnw'fining by 2-0-3 to 0-1-4. , Thi WARRINGToN V. WIDNES. atii44, el/seP:iiistPnned match was played at Widnes on Mon- gers a g, before a: large number of spectators. The erived with a good team. atid hilltarbet iti Frank Turner kieked off, 11. t„ „ ."'e. mg, Jolley almost scored in the first attele swedlef (')orl came by the aid of Druannond, and • rhorniley took the oval well in a line out Th: frorn nle headway, but Clarke neutralizing froma Planner taVord., Barber looked dangerous until Fair tc, ‘43 bet_ ,ed him. Some exchange kicking next took Daa,ttaeit.weenthe backs, eventually causing the visitors Wking h Widnes slightly relieved, but another spell of h 'rise tte t3' the Warrington backs saw Webster at fault, olits4erng the visitor grandly. Scrimmages and aimed attention for a lengthened period, and</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Clarke eventually got the oval to Boscow, who reached half way, but Donohue dribbled to the quarter flag. A free kick against the visitors was met by Bandies, and Fair Turner and Thorniley dribbled away, Brennan being collared. A fine pass travelled from James Bate to Webster, who was held en the line. Drummond punted out of danger, but Fair Turner and Ashton responded gallantly, and Widnes were again penned. A penalty kick against the homesters was taken by Fair Turner and from a rush which immediately followed the Bates passed well, but the final essey saw Heesom failing to hold. Another free kick fell to the credit of Warrington, and the visitors displayed an over-anxiousness which lost them a certain try. Boscow responded to many calls !rade upon him, but Brennan elicted cheers with a capital save. Another fine return by Boscow, in response to a kick by Alford, was the next item, but no mistake was made by the Warrington back a minute later. Evans staved off an invasion of the hornesters, and then came splendid passing by the Bates, Evans, and Heesom, play being taken direct to Widnes twenty- five. Evans was now hurt and Heesom was drifted to centre, and Holmes placed on the wing. Alford put in a good run and kick, which Webster saved, and Half Time Arrived</p>
        <p>with the score sheet blank. Barber restarted, and play opened at half way. The Bates and Heesoin passed well, but some fine kicking by Webster caused Warring- ton to assume the aggressive. James Bate and Frail Turner dribbled to Clarke, and the game was waged on the home line once more. Shaw and Johnson dribbled out of danger, whilst Barber supplemented the effort with a good run and kick, taking play to neutral quar- ters. Heesom upset a vigorous rush of the homesters and then came a spell of passing, in which the Widnes backs excelled, and Clarke taking up the running Scored a Brilliant Try near the corner, Barber failing at the place. Turner's drop out saw Alford returning well, and Drommond upset a movement of the backs, but Heesom regained some lost ground, with an excellent kick to Brennan,and from a good bit of play by Alford, Boscow was com- pelled to touch down. Stimulated by their latest successes, the Widnesians displayed good form, the for- wards especially, and another rush saw Drummond having a drop at goal; Webster touching down. Jolley was foremost in a couple of rushes, and Holmes met a kick by Clarke, and Warrington new had the best of it. The Widnes forwards rushed the ball out of danger, and Widnes were penalised, Fair Turner making a good bid for goal. Forster dribbled well on the drop out, and a penalty kick against Warrington saw Barber failing,and Boscow running and punting .out of danger. James Bate upset Alford, and then Wright and Clarke passed, and Warrington again were hard pressed, Webster and Heesorn beat ,back the homesters once more, and although Jolley got going and looked dangerous, the tackling of the Widnes backs allowed of little progress, and the _ . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Conclusion of Hostilities</head>
        <p>Conclusion of Hostilities</p>
        <p>saw Widnes deservedly winners by a try and two minors to Warrington a minor. The visitors were afterwards entertained to a capital repast, served in Host Law's, best style. and the numerous good things provided were done ample justice to. Warrington played the same team as on Saturday, with the exception that Holmes took the place of Nevins, whilst the Widnes team was made up as follows :—Brennan, back •, Clark, Alford, and Barber, three-quarters ; Drummodd and Wright,. half-backs ; Johnson, Fraser, Wynne, Shaw, Gordon, Hogan, Forster, Waylin, and Ridgway, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Slevin, Wigan. RuNconn V. NEW BRIGHTON.—PIayed at Runcorn on Saturday. Kingston was absent from the home ranks, and on the other side Drinkwater was compelled to stand out owing to injury received when playing against Hud- dersfield. Bfettargh was also away, MacDonald filling the position of full. back. Faulkner opened the play, but the ball was quickly at the visitors 25, whaT Luya put his feet to the ball and executed a grand dribbling movement, which resulted in Bleasdalo getting aver near the corner, Ellis failing at goal. The home forwards, nettled by this reverse, led on a spirited rush, and the visitors' tackling powers were frequently tested. Gradu- ally the play assumed an even character, when Cromp- ton got possession, and, brushing aside all opposition, scored behind the posts, Warder placing the goal. From the restart Houghton was called upon, but cleared effectively, and shortly following Warder got well down, a feat' which was followed by Faulkner, who scored a second try, but the kick at goal failed. These were all the points scored in the first portion, and on restarting the Brightonians executed some neat passing move- ments, but were unable to elude the vigilance of the home backs. Cannell put in some good work, and Crompton, on the other side, kept his forwards well ahead. The play toned down somewhat, and towards the close H. Williams scored an unimproved try. Final result—Runcorn, 1 goal and 2 tries ; New Brighton, 1 try. Mnmom v. WIDNISS.—At Millom. Each team played substitutes. In the first half Millom had the wind, and led at half time by a goal and a try to nothing. Arm- strong and Hodgson scoring. In the second half much better play ,was shown by the home team, whose for- wards played grandly, and Hodgson, Armstrong, and Kitchen scored. Widnes got a goal. Result :—Millom, 1 goal 5 tries (15 points) ; Widnes, 1 goal (5 points.)</p>
        <p>_ Sr. HELENS V. Ronal:ALE HORNETS.—These well-known Lancashire clubs met at Rochdale on Saturday, in the presence of 5,000 spectators. dross started for St. Helens, with a strong sun in their faces, and a poor return by Midgeley gave the Saints a footing in the Hornet's quarters. Little obtained possession from the scrimmage, and ran an easy try. Foulkes converted. Succeeding play went in favour of the Hornets, but the Saints defended finely by good forward play. The Hor- nets continued to press, and Wightman got over, but the point was not allowed. Final result :—St. Helens 1 goal, Rochdale Hornets nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET A.F.C. T. MANCEISTIM WAN- DERERS.—PIayed on the Cricket Ground on Saturday. The Wanderers kicked off, having the wind and sun at their backs. Play for a few minutes was even, neither side having any advantage. The Cricket Club right wing and centre, working very hard, managed at length to work the ball up the field, and about ten minutes from the start Connor with a good shot scored the first goal for them. Soon after this the Wanderers made a combined rush and Mays scored. From the kick off Connor worked the ball down, and had very hard lines in not scoring, a magnificent shot hitting the cross bar. A minute or two later he was rewarded by scoring a second goal. Half time score 2-1 in favour of the Cricket Club. After the interval the Cricket Club had the best of the game. Fennelly had bard lines in not scoring, a good shot being just stopped. The Wan- derers' goal keeper, did net get it far enough away, and Connor, was at once on to it and both put it through. G. Williamson and Hoare both shot well at this point, but were unsuccessful. Afterwards Connor played in grand form, put on a couple more goals and the Cricket Club won a most enjoyable game by 5 goals to 1.</p>
        <p>-- -- WARRINGTON IDISERVE V. ORFORD CRESCENT.—The above match was played on the Crescent ground on Saturday, the home team being assisted by several of the Oxford Barracks players. The visitors arrived With a weak team, being three men short, and both teams agreed to play ten on each side. The Crescent kicked oil with the wind and sun in their face. Five minutes from the start Whiteman scored for the visitors with a good shot. The home team with a rush equalized. The visitors again took up the running, and pressed hard, causing the full-back to kick through his own goal. Half-time arrived with the score—Warrington, two goals; Crescent, one goal. In the second half the game was very even. The Crescent managed to score once, and the game ended in a draw, two all. •</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON NORTH END V.PADGATE INSTITUTE. —This match was played at Padgate on Saturday. The first match between these teams resulted in a win for the North End by four goals to one. North End winning the toss the homesters kicked off and assumed the aggresive only to be repulsed by W. Rostron who transferred play to the other end. North End were now beginning to have the best of it and from a combined rush of the forwards Edwards opened the scoring, and he was soon after credited with another. After some give and take play both goals being visited in turn, Pendlebury received the ball and passed to Edwards who again sent through. Matters were now beginning to look lively for the homesters, but they could not break through the defence of the visitors, half-time araiving with the score 3-0. After the change of ends, the ball was again taken down by a rush of the forwards and this time Bennett scored, Padgate now played up and Ilovvard scored. North End then put on pressure and the ball was taken to the other end T. Mather this time scoring, the same player scoring again after a grand passing movement. A minute later Bradshaw hitting the goalpost from a grand shot. From a free kick W.Rostron put on another. Padgate strove hard to stem the tide but all to no pur- pose, as Bennett again sent through. From the restart however, the homesters got another goal from Sherwood Bennett afterwards getting another for the North End who left the field easy winners by 9 goals to 2. Warring- ton North End team : goal, T. Davenport ; backs T. Brown, and W. Rostron ; half-backs, J. Maguire, W. 0•Niell, and J. Dakin ; forwards J. Bradshaw, T. Mather, J. Bennett, J. Edwards, and G. Pendlebury. WINNINGTON PARK V. RUNCORN.— Played OR the ground of the latter, before a fair number of spectators. Run- , corn were without their centre forward, H. Pye, but his place was well filled by Arthur Limb. Runcorn kicked off, and the game became very fast, both teams attack- ing. Then Winningtoti having the best of the game, scored. Ttie homesters exerted themselves. and Arthur Limb sent in a tine shot, which was finely saved. Later Winnington added another goal, so that at half time the score stood :—Winnington, 2 ; Runcorn, nil. On changing ends the visitors were put on the defensive. During a tough scrimmage in front of the visitors' goal, Arthur Limb rushed the ball through, and scored the first goal for Runcorn. The Runcorn backs played a good game all through—Mainwaring and Kidd deserv- ing especial mention. Runcorn had several times hard lines in not scoring, but erratic shooting among the forwards helped to lose them the game. From a kick off the ball came in contact with one of the players, and bonneed back through the visitors' goal, a second point for Runcorn, which, however, the referee not seeing refused to allow. A very exciting and fast game ended in a win for Winnington by two goals to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>•4r. r 1.4 11 Lil !I .1 i `11`1.;': ,11,44 f 14 A 7::1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY;</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY;</p>
        <p>I resolutions of this Authority mid of the Runcorn Corn- missioners had been duly presented and the matter was fully discussed, but no decision was come to, the con- . ference being again adjourned sine die. He also reported that an opinion was expressed by the Representatives of the County Councils that the amounts proposed to be contributed by the Local Authoritlei of Widnes and Run- corn were not sufficient, and that the proposal that these Authorities should have a first charge upon the tolls was not regarded favourably, but that it appeared probable that all other questions being settled, provision might be made for such a toll on condition that it formed the security for the entire cost, and not merely for the ' local contributions. He also stated that if any further proposal were made, there would be another conference, but if not, the County Councils would proceed to deal with the matter upon its present footing, at their next meeting in May. Resolved—that the further considera- tion of the matter be adjourned until Wednesday,lsth Inst. The Committee resolved that the salary of the Town Hall keeper, Thomas Whittle, be increased by lls, per week. —Alderman Wareing moved •itud . Councillor Shores seconded the adoption of the minutes.—Councillor Quinn said he understood that the attempt to arrange for a new post office in Victoria-road had fallen through, and ho suggested that the Committee when dealing with the extension of the Town Hall should consider the advisability of providing for the post office in the build- ing. (Hear, hear.)—Alderman Wareing said the Com- mittee would bear Councillor Quinn's suggestion in , mind.—Councillor Smith protested against the increase ' in the hallkeeper's salary and Alderman Wareing said the services of five people were involved.—Tne minutes Were passed.</p>
        <p>THE TOWN CLERK'S PETTY EXPENSES. On the Finance Committee's minutes, Alderman Wil- liamson said he observed that there was an item of £4l for the Town Clerk's petty cash, and he desired to ask the Chairman of the Finance Committee whether an ac- count of the petty cash had been laid before him, and whether he was aware that the full amount paid in that way during the past four months was £lO6. This would make a large item in the course of twelve months ; it would amount altogether, with salaries, to a rate of 3d. in the £ for the 'Town Clerk only,—Counclllor Owen re- plied that Alderman Williamson was a memker of the Committee, and he could have had the matter explained to his satisfaction in Committee. He was personally satisfied that the disbursements had been properly made. It was out of pocket expenses.—The,Town Clerk said the Corporation only re-paid him such sums as he properly paid as disbursements on their behalf.—Alderman Wi- liamson said he was quite satisfied with the replies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall, on Tuesday afternoon. The Mayor (Alderman Gos- sage) presided, and there were present Aldermen Sadler, Wareing, Tilley,and Williamson, Councillors Longton, Gough, Quinn, H. Singleton, Carney, W.W. Gossage, Hodgkinson, Owens, Davies, F. Niel, Chris- tie, G. J. Niel, Barlow, Bullough, Shuttleworth, Smith, and Shores, Messrs,Oppenheim (town clerk), Craik (assistant clerk), Hoyle (accountant), Dr. O'Keeffe (mediaar officer, Carr (engineer), Sinclair (surveyor). THE CEMETERY QUESTION. The Town Clerk hoped to have received information on this question before next Council meeting. '</p>
        <p>MAYORAL APPOINTMENTS. The Town Clerk announced that Messrs. J. H. Yank- burst and R. Whiteside had been appointed elective auditors, and Councillor Shores Mayor's auditor.</p>
        <p>DISEASE AND DEATH.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee submitted the Hospital reports as follows :—Accident Hospital—Number of patients at last meeting, • admitted since, 6 ; discharged, 5; died, 0 ; patients still in, 8. Infectious Diseases Hospital— Number of patients at last meeting, 1; admitted since, 3 ; discharged since, 0 ; died since, 1 ; patients still in, 3. The following report of the Medical Officer of Health was submitted :—Births, 84, equal to 33.6 per 1,000 ; deaths (total), 35, equal to 14.0 per 1,000, including children under 1 year, 9 ; children , between 1 and 5 years, 3; zymotic diseases, 8 ; inquests, 2. The death rate is unusually low in this borough, in fact, almost the lowest in England. Three cases of smallpox have been notified, which are now in hospital,and are doing well. He regretted to state that one case of smallpox terminated fatally after 2i days' residence in the smallpox hospital, which was sent to hospital on 10th February. The Infectious Diseases (Fever) Hospital has been without a patient for the last three weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee submitted the following minutes : Report of tests made by Mr. C. E. Bellamy, of Liverpool, on the 22nd and 27th inst., showed : Illuminating power, 1965 and 17.74 candles, respectively, free from sulphuretted hydrogen. Re- solved+-Tha t a 12-inch gag main be laid from Parr's Bank corner to the top of Hutchinson-st.: at an esti- mated cost of £220, for the purpose of increasing the pressure in Waterloo-road, West Bank, and the Marsh districts, and that tenders be invited for the supply of the necessary pipes. Also that tenders be invited for the supply of water pipes for an extension of the water main at Halebank.—The Engineer reported that in view of the proposed stoppage of miners the stock of coals at the Gas Works had been increased to a 25 days supply. —The Town Clerk reported the result of his negotiations with the Widnes and Ditton Land Co. and the United Alkali Co. Ltd. for the acquirement of the 635 yards of land required in connection with the erection of the pro- posed new gas holder, and stated the terms upon which the land could be acquired. Resolved—That such terms be accepted and that the Town Clerk be instructed to complete the purchase. The Engineer submitted the plans for a tank and triple lift gasholder, having a capacity of 605,000 cubic feet and estimated to cost £7,000, are again submitted. Resolved— That the plans and estimate of the proposed new gas holder be approved and adopted, and that tenders be invited for the carrying out of the work. Resolved—That the reward of 10s., at present offered by the Corporation for information as to damage to public lamps, be increased to £l. Resolved—That from and after the 25th March instant the price of gas to con- sumers within the Borough be as follows, viz :—To consumers below 3,000,000 cubic feet per annum 2s less 2d per 1,000 cubic feet discount. To consumers above 3,000,000 cubic feet per annum is 10d less 2d per 1,000 cubic feet discount. Resolved—That the present rates for the supply of water for other than domestic purposes within the Bcrough, be, as and from the 25th day of March instant, altered and varied as follows, viz :—For quantities over 50,000,000 gallons 2d. for each 1,000 gallons, in excess of such 50,000,000 gallons, nett cash. Provided that this resolution shall not come into force unless and until the United Alkali Co., Limited, shall enter into an agreement with the Corporation that they shall not be entitled to be supplied with water at the , above mentioned rate, except in respect of water supplied to them by the Corporation in excess of 500,000,000 gallons in the aggregate in any one year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NEW GASHOLDER.</head>
        <p>A NEW GASHOLDER.</p>
        <p>Alderman Timm-is-in in-OTI-Ing the adoption of the minutes, said the present gasholder was not sufficient for the requirements of the town. It only had a stor- ing caoacity for 15 hours supply and in the case of a break -down the town would soon be in darkness. It was estimated that the proposed gasholder would meet the requirements of the town for a period of twelve years. The little strip of land they had purchased would be of great value in the future for extending their works, as locomotives would be able to enter the yard and discharge the coal at the retorts, should new works be constructed there. As to the proposed reduction irt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>PRICE OF GAS</head>
        <p>THE PRICE OF GAS</p>
        <p>the profit for the last year was £2,800, and the estimated profit for the present year despite the loss on coke and residuals was £2,240. The reduction of 2d. would ab- sorb £lOOO of the profits, leaving still £1,200 in the hands of the Finance Committee. Through the ability of their engineer they were able to use the Dinsmore pro- cess of carbonizing and by this process saved 100 per cent, on their coal bill. The Committee expected that the reduction in the price of gas would increase the number of consumers and they did not anticipate that the profit would be less than it was at the present time. With regard to the reduction in</p>
        <p>TILE PRICE OF WATER,</p>
        <p>the Committee could pump 5,000,000 gallons per diem, but at the present time they were only pumping 21 millions per diem and if they could by any means dispose of this surplus water it would be to the advantage of the Corporation and also to the traders. He dwelt upon the great importance of , giving such advantages to the Manufacturers to stay, and of offering inducements to others to come and settle in the district by reason of the cheapness ot water and gas.—ln replying to questions from members of the Council, Alderman Timmis said the price paid for the land at Ditten was £230 ; they expected to reap far more than that value from being able to utilize their own land in which there was a quicksand. As to Alderman Sadler's question they were making no reduction in the price of water at all ; they were simply trying to sell their surplus. There had been no charge on the rates for the gas and water undertak- ings ; additions to the property had been made out of the profits. As to the minimum charge it was fifty per themg vcent,aavtneec roTnlsosei tr:st ispcmopeftil shi oepafednuommnweot plitascosnuwngi owing wgne re.—Councillor Christie: I will regardiTsnahgied t toh ettr2oaddt. decrease ehwa eoti xunmi gdtaehtnet e amounter amplys —. Acsl oodvme oer Some works were doing more ; some less ; and they wished to increase the trade by offering inducements to use water. —The minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>THE 74iimicuLAR BRIDGE. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on the Bth inst., the Town Clerk reported the result of the conference between the representatives of this Corpora- tion and the Runcorn Commissioners with the Joint Com- mittee of the Lancashire and Cheshire Ceunty Councils, held at Liverpool on the 7th inst., and stated that the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>MARCH 18, 1893.</head>
        <p>MARCH 18, 1893.</p>
        <p>THE TECHNICAL SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Technicall-Insc—tiOt;--67nTirifttee recorded that they had received a letter. dated 21st February ult., from Mr. J. A. Bennion, director of technical instruction intimating that the Technical Instruction Committee of the County Council had decided to recommend the Lan- cashire County Council to make a payment of £360 for the purchase of apparatus and appliances, such payment to be contingent upon the completion of the proposed new Technical School, and to be made at such time as the Technical Instruction Committee should deem advis- able.—The minutes were passed on the motion of Coun- cillor F. Neil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>BRANCH READING ROOM.</head>
        <p>BRANCH READING ROOM.</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the Free Library Committee coming up for consideration ,Councillor Davies asked if the Com- mittee could establish a branch reading room at West Bank, and another at Simms Cross out of their present funds.—Cquncillor Shuttleworth And one at Farn:</p>
        <p>worth. (Laughter.)—Alderman Wareing: Might I suggest Halton View. (Laughter.)—Councillor Christie : We have one Alderman Wareing.—The Mayor said there was no doubt that if they could establish reading rooms it would be for the advantage of the place, but how were they going to pay for them.—Councillor F. Neil said at present the Committee had no funds to do it, but if the Council thought it wise to establish branch reading rooms, the Committee would take the matter in- to serious consideration. THE MAYOR'S CHAIN.</p>
        <p>The Mayor said it been thought that in the fitness of things there should be a Mayor's chain, and he had been devoting considerable time towards obtaining a chain that he thought was suitable.—As they were aware, Widnes started in a very different position from a great many boroughs. That was to say they had no history, and consequently they had no deeds of arms or other murders (laughter) that they could display upon their coat of arms. He now submitted three links in copper showing the design of the gold chain he proposed to give to the borough. These links were copper, they were repousee but not worked with a hammer like repousse gold would be.lt appeared to him to be particu- larly artistic and effective.—The links were then examined by the Council and the opinion appeared to be favourable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLAR? AT MAKERFIELD.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLAR? AT MAKERFIELD.</p>
        <p>No. I CONVENTION.</p>
        <p>This body held its monthly session in the Con- gregational School, Ashton-in-Makerfielel, on Satur- day. Bro. Roberts (Warrington), the C.0.T., called to order at 4 p.m., there being present sixty repre- sentatives and visitors from eleven lodges and four Juvenile temples. The officers' reports, though brief, were very interesting, and showed that the work of the Order was in a favourable condition all through the area. Arrangements were ordered to be made to help the Firs-lane Lodge to a better position. Bro. Vint stated that steps had been taken to revive the Ashton Lodge. It was hoped that all lodges of the Con- vention would in their turn come and give them an en- tertainment. Hindley was unanimously chosen for the next place of meeting, when Bro. R. Scaife (St. Helens) will read a paper, to be followed by a discussion. Bro. Mantle (Warrington) read a paper, entitled "Some of the weak points in our lodges, and how to remedy them." A lively discussion followed, and a vote of I thanks was tendered to the reader. After the session an excellent tea was provided, which was well attended. At half-past seven a public meeting was held, Bro. Hume (Ashton) presiding. Bro. Roberts delivered a very good address on the present aspect of the temperance move- ment. Bro. Clarke (Earlestown) moved, and Bro. Vint (Ashton) seconded, a resolution expressing approval generally with the Liquor Traffic Local Control Bill, which was carried with acclamation. In the interval some excellent songs and recitations were rendered by Various members, the meeting being brought to a close shortly after nine o'clock. During the session a resolu- tion was carried uraniniously thanking the various newspaper proprietors and editors for their courtesy in publishing from time to time such full reports of the meetings.</p>
        <p>THROAT IRRITATION IND COUGH.—SOreness and dryness tickling end irritation. intilucing cough and affecting the voice. fi`or these symptoms use Epps Glycerine Jujubes. In contact with the glands at the moment they are excited by the act of sucking, the Glycerine in these agreeable con- fections becomes actively healing. Sold only la boxes. 70.. tins is. 10. labelled "JAMES EPPS &amp; Co., Homceopathic Chemists, Lndon." Dr. Moore, in his work on "Nose and Throat Diseases," says: "The Glycerine Jujubes prepared by James Epps and Co., are of undoubted service as a curative 3r isolliative agent," while Dr. Gordon Holmes, Senior Phyfildan to the Municipal Throat and Ear Infirmary, writes : 'After an extended trial, I have found your Gly- cerine Jujilbes ot considerable benefit in almost all forms of throat of sease."—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>PRINCELY POMP IN LI7SHAILA.ND.</head>
        <p>PRINCELY POMP IN LI7SHAILA.ND.</p>
        <p>The correspondent of an Indian journal desctibes the manner in which Lallnova, the Lushai chief who has re- cently been an ally of ours, moved his residence from one village to another recently. The procession moved out in Indian file, led by the chief, a heir, a boy 12 years ' of age, wearing a quaint gray tall hat, the symbol of his father's rank, and a loin cloth. In one hand he carried a bottle of the liquor of the country, and with the other led a yellow dog. Next came "a maid of honour, bear- ing a huge basket of household goods and chattels, and smoking the everlasting pipe of peace the while." She was followed by the Prime Minister, leading the chief's pig, to whom succeeded the chief's wife, clad in a divided skirt of kuki blue cloth, and wearing the Royal amber necklace.. The chief himself came next. Ile wore a red helmet, two flannel shirts, an evening dress coat and waistcoat—the latter being arranged outside the coat—and a garment of Lushai cloth. The trousers , belonging to the dress suit were gracefully thrown over the left shoulder, a pair of grey socks, unlaced ammuni- tion boots, and a flagon of liquor completed the chief's travelling costume. Last of all came a gentleman-in- waiting, who carried the Royal bedding in a bundle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>TENNYSON AS A TALKER.</head>
        <p>TENNYSON AS A TALKER.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>In the "Contemporary Review "Agnes Grace Weld, a niece of the late Lord Tennyson by marriage, reproduces some of the things which she heard him say during the cross-country walks that they used to take together when she was young. On one occasion he remarked: "I cannot imagine why people are so curious about all the petty incidents of my life. I never cared to know about the daily " minutke " of great men's lives, which never interested me in the least." Says Miss (or Mrs.) Weld</p>
        <p>"There was one incident in his life that Tennyson knew had often beesi misunderstood, and he most solemnly laid upon me the charge that I, who, he said, could best undertake the task, should let all the world know how great a sacrifice' (these were his very words uttered in a tone of intense earnestness) he had practised in yielding to Mr. Gladstone's pressing entreaties that he should take the peerage." Tennyson used to say "The freedom of the human will and the starry heavens are the two greatest marvels that come under our observation. . . • Ido not like such a word as design to be applied to the Creator of all these worlds, it makes Him seem a mere artificer. A certain amount of anthropomorphism must, however, necessarily enter into our conception of God, because, though there may be infinitely higher beings than ourselves in the worlds beyond ours, yet to our conception man is the highest form of being. Matter, time and space are all illusions, but above and beyond &amp;ern all is God, who is no illusion. Tisne has no absolute existence, and we can as little conceive of space being finite as of its beinginfinite. We can really understand the existence of spirit much better than that of matter, which is to me far more incomprehensible than spirit. lire see nothing as it really is, not even our fellow-creatures, and perhaps when we see each other as we really are, we shall no more know each other than dogs do their masters in the path or on the snow." Miss Weld further ascribes to Tennyson the follow- ing "I believe that besides our material body we possess an immaterial body. . . Ido not care to make dis- tinctions between the soul and the spirit, as men did in days of old, though perhaps the spirit is the best Nord to use of our higher nature, that nature which I believe in Christ to have been truly divine, the very presence of the Father, the one only God, dwelling in the perfect man. . . We shall have much to learn in a future world, and I think we shall all be children to begin with when we get to heaven, whatever our age when we die, and shall grow on there from childhood to the prime of life, at which we shall remain for ever. My idea of heaven is to be engaged in perpetual ministry to souls in this and other worlds."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>YON PUBLIC HALL TOWER!</head>
        <p>YON PUBLIC HALL TOWER!</p>
        <p>Poor owd Nyowton ! it's no wunder That theaurt gettin' in a rage, Wen theau sees yon o'ergrown Tower Runnin' off wi' haul thi wage! It's enuff to set a-cossin', Even one that's thowt a saint; An' wotever else they caw it, It's a beauty, lads, beawt paint! But th' Commissioners fro' Earlestown Reilly are a plucky crew; Not afeaed o' vexin' Heaven, Wi' ther imitation flue! Why, it's eaut o' aw proportion, Even ez a foo may gee; An' fer thoose wo live foreneenst it, Sum fine neet they'll want to flee! Like a little dwarf er bullyed, It's to heyvy up at th' top; An' before so lung met clutter, Wi a devil of a flop! Wots it for l' aw hear sum arm', But the dule hissel caunt say; • Beaut its wot aw'm shrewdly gyoussin'— Hevin' bigger rates to pay! P'rhaps its maybe meant to fricken Thoose wo live abeaut i'th' street Er to give um fits o' stennin', Ev'ry time they get a seet Wen its finish'd, thay shud top it,— If ther brass 'ull go so far,— Wi' a blazin' lamp er launtern, Meant fer Folly's Guidin' Star 0 my poor owd native village, Theau's snuff beaut this to lose; Just to think, fer this abortion, Themes to pay feet" throo thi nose!" Et fer thoose wo live on wagging, Thay'n not quite such need to croak; Fey, yo see, i° their short lifetime, Thayil ne'er want their Stondin' Joke I WM 0' PEGS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Rose Bonk, March 14th, 1893.</head>
        <p>Rose Bonk, March 14th, 1893.</p>
        <p>KAI'S COMPOUND, for Coughs and: Colds. Asthma and Bronchitis are relieved by it, 91d. 131 d. - - - - - -</p>
        <p>Some excite ment has beentcaused in London by the discovery that " Mr. Pearce," co-respondent in a divorce case which was heard under reinarkable circumstances in Sir F. Jeune's court last week, was really Sir W. G. Pearce, one of the Conservative members for Plymouth. The Divorce Court reporters were suspected, in conse- quence of the brevity of the report and the suppression of the real title of the co-respondent, of having been " influenced ;" but they declare that Sir William was described as "Mr. Pearce" throughout the case by counsel a rid witnesses. CoecimaNs.—Cement for Broken Articles, 6d. and is. ; postage, 2d. Sold everywhere, home and abroad.-- Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>/(h6A</head>
        <p>/(h6A</p>
        <p>1 ST. ALBAN'S ROMAN 'CATHOLIC CHURCH. 1 AN IMPOSING DEDICATION SERVICE.</p>
        <p>With the congregation of St. Alban's Roman Catholic Church, and indeed, with the Roman Catholics of Warrington, Sunday was a great day. After extensive renovations, and the erection of a new grand altar, the church in Bewsey-st., was opened, ned and the alter dedicated with imposing religious ceremonies which constituted a spectacle grander and more sublime than probably any religious spectacle ever witnessed in Witt-- ri n gton before. At both morning and evening service Roman Catholics, Protestants and Nonconform- ists alike hastened to the church long before service, to secure seats, and to meet with a surprise that few had anticipated, for although it was well-known the altar would be unveiled then, only those who had been for- tunate enough to get a glimpse of it before, were aware of- the exquisite beauty of the work. The Church ot St. Albans was the first Roman Catho- lic Church Warrington possessed. Owing to the conse- quent character of certain portions of the building, the architect, Mr. Pugin, of London, found some difficulty in harmonizing his great work with the lesser conspi- cuous surroundings. The result, however, has been most satisfactory, and the apparently difficult task has been overcome by the architect, sculptor, and builder. The old elliptical arch remains, and the wall behind has been pierced, and a new stone arch put in supported on granite shafts ; on each side, on the springing line is the cornice, which rests on the cap of the arch on one side and on a respond on the other. The apse which has been thrown out beyond this consists of five sides. In the centre bay is a large panel sunk into the wall, with a semi-arch moulded and carved, resting on caps sup- ported by marble columns. The spandrils of the arch are richly carved, and above is the following inscrip- tion :—`• Venite exultemus Dornine. Jubilemus Deo salutari nostro," from the 94th Psalm. In each of the panels, which are of Oregon pine are painted subjects richly gilt and embossed, taken from the life of St. Alban, the patron of the church, and the proto-martyr of England. These panels have all been designed and painted by the well- known Warwickshire artist, Mr. Joseph A. Pippet. The two bays in the dexter and sinister side are each divided into two panels with mouldings running round the arch resting on caps supported by marble columns. At the angles and in the centre of each bay are marble columns supported on corbels, and terminating in caps supporting a cornice, which runs round the apse, on which an inscription has been carvA, representing St. Albans victory and final crowning. The apse is lit with lantern light, which cast a soft radiance on the painted panels. In the dado on the epistle side is the piscina and the ambry for the holy oils on the gospel side. The high altar, which is approached by three marble steps, stands in the middle of the apse. Thealtar stone is sup- ported on four marble columns with caps and bases, while beneath the altar are three panels in deeply re- cessed mouldings, which are exquisitely painted by Mr. Joseph A. Pippet, representing the distribution of the precious blood. The two super altars are covered with marble slabs, the face of the gradus is inlaid with marble. .</p>
        <p>The shrine forms the the central panel, and is a most magnificent piece of ecclesiastical work. It represents the Manses tomb, and pilgrims, including kings, queens, courtiers, monks, and nuns, the infirm and the lame coming up to the shrine, begging the intercession of the saint in their different needs. The great metal tabernacle rests on a marble slab in the centre, under which is carved " Sanctus." The canopy, which is massive and simple in character, is supported on the marble shafts, beneath which is the throne with angels adoring on each side. The old pilasters of the chancel have been carved and restored with Levant marble, which adds very much to the appearance of the chancel. The new altar rail is of alabaster, richly wrought, with marble step, and a dark marble moulded capping en top. The following are the firms engaged in the restoration of Si. Albans :—Messrs. Hardman, Powell and Co., Birmingham, have supplied all the brass work and altar furniture ; Messrs. Boulton and Sons, sculptors, Chelten- ham, have erected the altar, reredos, and have executed all the carving ; Messrs. Norbury and Pallison,Myrtle-st., Liverpool, built and erected the altar rails ; Mr. James M. Greenhalgh, Warrington, supplied the sanctuary oak step ; Messrs. Hardman, Powell and Co., Birmingham, assisted by Messrs. J. Richardson and Son, Bridge-st., Warrington, carried out the church decorations ;Messrs. Williams and Clay, of Warrington, fixed the marble altar steps, as also the marble pilasters ; Mr. Charles Walker, builder, Preston, was the contractor for all the building, stone work, new arches, &amp;c. ; Mr. J. Mason was en- trusted with all the plumbing, gas fitting, &amp;c. • Mr. Osborne, Church-st., Warrington, did the plastering. At the service on Sunday morning, the bright summer sun shone through the east windows and glinted upon the altar, with its beautiful slab of marble, and its , precious metal, with an effect that very much enhanced the beauty of the work. Pontifical High Mass was cele- brated by Archbishop Scarisbrick, the assistant priests being :—Father Cummings, Father Cody, and Father Craven. There were also on the altar—the Right Rev. Dr. O'Reilly, bishop of the diocese, the Very Rev. T. Anselm Burge, Prior of. Ampleforth College, Father Larkin, Father Roach, and Mr. John Flshwick, of Liver- pool, master of ceremonies; and in their gorgeous vestments they presented a very strikiv. scene. Of the music, " Gunods last Mass of the Sacred Heart" which was rendered by the choir under the baton of Mr. E. Whitehead, we cannot say sufficiently in admira- tion of the manner in which it was rendered. It was grandly expressive and given with such precision and taste, and attack that few have ever heard in Warring- ton before, and we are sure that the congregation en- joyed a treat that will be long remembered". The offer- tory piece, Gunods "Ave Maria," a beautiful gem of a piece, was plaintively and excellently sung with great feeling by Miss J. Unsworth. To hear the grand old " Gloria ' form Mozarts 12 Mass stirred the hearts of the people and fittingly brought the morning service to an end. Dr. Hedley preached a most impressive sermon, deep and interesting, fully worthy of his great reputa- tion. In the course of this sermon, the preacher said : "What is the life that the Saviour promises in the Kingdom which is to come P For a " life "it is to be. It is not to be mere existence—a changeless state, with- out movement or emotion. There are pictures in the poets of fairy lands, where the sun always shines and the winds are hushed, where the blue sky never alters, and where mortals sleep, or dream day-dreams, through ages of time unmeasured and unmarked. Heaven is not to be like that. Life means movement ; life means the activity of the whole being with all its powers. Life means the shock of impressions, the re-action of compre- hension and of fruition, the stir andthe swift succession of scenes, of events, of experiences. If Heaven is " life," Heaven must be this." At the evening service, the effect of the reflected light on the altar was simply charming and the people were enhanced and long alter the service was concluded, many remained to gaze on the altar and the church was with difficulty cleared. The evening service opened with vespers by about 20 priests in the chancel. This was very pretty and when the Bishop at there in full pontifical robes surrounded by his clergy and the whole brilliantly lighted up with the altar as a background, the scene was indeed grand. Father Burge of Ample- forth preached an excellent sermon..</p>
        <p>The4choir was augmented at this service hy a band under the leadership of Herr Praeger and Tugrginers ' grand Benediction Service, Mooratz " Exultet " were given with great effect. Mr. Joseph Bate sang the solo in Tugginers " Tantum Ergo" with great taste. The nice effects which the cornet produced in this service were much admired. Handers Hallelujah Chorus brought the service to an end. Mr. J. Caldwell presided at the organ with ability.. . .</p>
        <p>'A. contributor writes :—" Altogether it was a memor- able day for St. Albans, and marks a great epoch in history of this the oldest Roman Catholic Church in the town. It would have delighted the heart of its former pastor, the whll-known Father Hall, could he have lived to see this day. Re took great interest in St. Albans, and it may be stated that the beautiful new altar rails were put down in remembrance of him,and will sevre to remind his old children and the future worshippers of St. Albans of the great and loved priest who did so much for the church, and had such a great hand in erecting the noble edifice of St. Mary's in Buttermarket-st. We must congratulate Father Feeney upon the completion of this great work. It must have been a cause of anxiety to him and the whole work reflects on him the greatest credit."—We might mention that Father Turner who is here alluded Lo died a short time ago When he was collecting for the erection of St. Mary's Church, he gathered £lO,OOO in penny pieces.</p>
