      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ODD FELLOWS' ANNIVERSARY WARRINGTON„ PRESENTATION OF AN EMBLEM AND MEDAL..</head>
        <p>ODD FELLOWS' ANNIVERSARY WARRINGTON„ PRESENTATION OF AN EMBLEM AND MEDAL..</p>
        <p>The Loyal Friendship Lodge (1087) of the National Order of Oddfellows held their eighth anniversary at the Old Crow Inn, Bridge-st., on. Saturday evening. Bro. Henry King, the host, pro- vided an excellent repast. Bro. John Johnson, , N.G., occupied the chair, and Bro. Thomas Appleby. N.G., the vice chair. Ths proceedings were inter- spersed with songs. The toast of the Queen and Royal family was duly honoured, after which Bro. J. Crosby, P.P.G.M., proposed "The Loyal Friend- ship Lodge." Bro. Thomas Knight, P. 0., responded and took the opportunity to present Bro. James Dramhall, P.P.G.M., on behalf of the lodge, with an emblem el the order, handsomely framed, as a token of appreciation of the services rendered by him to the lodge —Bro. Bramliall briefly responded. Bro. J. Newton, D.S., presented Bro. Bramhall in the name of the lodge with a" Past Provincial" medal in recognition of his services for the past two years during which he had been P.P.G.M.—Bro. J. Burgess, PG., the lodge secretary, presented the annual financial report showing that £l4O 10s. 10d. bad been disbursed for sick pay; £135 19s. Bd. for Noma' expenses; £9l 14s. 9d. for medical aid, total £368 53. Balance in hand £260. There wore 114 mem- bers on the register, the average age being 29i years. (Applause) Bro. W. Woods, district sec., gave the health oh ti4e Warrington District to which Bro. Tyser, D.G.M. responded. Bro. Bramhall proposed the "National Independent* Order of Oddfellows." Bro. G. Smith, G.M., Eccles, in responding said that the order *as in a prosperousanstdatteh.ey had most gratifying reports from were 59,927 adults and 9,000 juveniles, '9 B93tus:t lookedeathat hen various parts of the country with regard to the increase of juvenile members. The percentage. too, of juveniles drafted into the parent society was almost as great as the number of new members. It was very gratifying to the officers and the Executive Board to hear of the good work that the juvenile branches were doing through- out the country. Referring to a remark made by Mr Woods about the societies giving their support to the central office, he said they should - Ibe loyal not only to their districts, but to those who were on the governing body of the Order. (Applause.) , Sir°. J. E. Cleveland, cot. secretary, said that he noticed that in Warrington in 1883 they had 158 members in two lodges, and he found that alter ten years they had to- day only 270 members in four lodges. It was often a wonder to him that the society in Warrington did not extend in a greater proportion than it did. There could not be much general collective satisfaction out of the small number of members that they had in Warrington, and he would rather sea the whole of them in one lodge soecsulaiduottidndoti shfn:gs72j 5,208 Bvs eaandiwml e whole, t t,o teed withbla last but than in four. (Bear, hear.) The Order had made rapid progress in the right direction for some years past. There were 63 capital of £209,000. These figures were very satisfac- n owthki which wwtahsatsotinhee; year,ttt l i ri e some- thing g mustwltihkheeerlcis alsot wereph per ryc o cent.theyn 4s ,i od 22e r need be particularly apprehensive on that l scare for other societies suffered nearly in the same ratio and, "t was impossible to avoid it. Bro. Emson Pi:osiv:. C.S. of the Manchester Unity of tlpiWndarrerdingstoocnit icetiesia.t,pctot, herres- ponded to the toast of the .it toasts followed. The musical Programme was follows song, " Boys of the Old Brigade," B Iti.gb7 P.G., and song "Bells of ! Mem* 0r17," Bro.J. Bro. 'Pa: There was also a recitation by. Mr. John Knight.</p>
