      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>o</p>
        <p>TIIE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1893.</p>
        <p>4 judge on A fortnight ago, in commenting on coßrewers the Local Option Bill we contended , rnl3ensetien. that the Government were dealing the the publicans much more considerately than 4." brewers sometimes treated the so-called 'etiants of the public-houses they owned. This Bhe,enis to be also the opinion of Mr. JUSTICE CAVE, is opinion being based upon certain evidence liven in a case that came before him at Chester tieBizes on Wednesday. In this case (fuller par- teulare of which are given in another column) Mr. o'll'l'vit) a wine and spirit dealer, sued Mr. irtron,the present tenant of the Wynnstay Arms °tel' 1th371, for a sum which included £lOO for egoodwill. The Wynnstay Arms Hotel, we may livlTil.,aira, belongs to Messrs. GREENALL and Co., of of 11 ''ers,,Pc'el Brewery, Warrington. In February ter: n t - ear, when Mr. BALDWIN entered upon the but iveY the hotel was not doing a good business, suee ..r. 1/41.1:4v1N appears to have been very Ai_ --sslui the eno in working up the connection. 0 .. . . t t July he disposed of the lease Mr. CLoton, who, among other things, agreed to Pay £lOO for goodwill. The action at the Ches- ter Assizes was brought to recover part of this 41°ney which had remained unpaid. In the course of the ease so..iae interesting evidence was given, Vhich should be of value at a time like the pre- sent when the cry for compensation to brewers is being so loudly raised. Mr. JOSEPH HARTLEY,thO North Wales agent for the Wilderspool Brewery, said that when CLOUGH signed the agreement he • 918 distinctly told that he could not get back the 00 goodwill. The following conversation then Place, according to the published reports, between Mr. Justice CAVE and Mr. HARTLEY, the ent of Messrs. GREENALL and Co. "The JUDGE: When the tenant gets up the value of a house do 14°U confiscate the additional value of the property ? 0:--4Witness :We do.—The Judge : Well, it is about 1 - Iwie Coolest thing I have heard in my life. Ido litiet wendar landlords are not popular in Wales if i."4iB is the way they conduct themselves. Are Sure you are authorised to say this for your ncipals P—Witness : Yes ; there is no such as goodwill recognised in our firm.—The C'ge : Then I repeat it is a most disgraceful ruing to confiscate a man's labour and put the , ee°eY into your own pocket.—Witness; We h ; h Quld never take the house from him so long as ' 0 behaved himself. He could keep the house for ' ex • In his summing up Mr. Justice CA4E o Di'essed himself pretty freely. He said "it was jltrageous and monstrous for brewers to confiscate bue value of a tenant's labour, especially as the , ewers themselves were at present going up and ouo the country complaining about their own ' ellni.elertY being confiscated. If there was any * i;.ll4Beation at all, he would sooner see their pro- iZtY confiscated by the Government than their ti,;arlt'B property confiscated by them. Confisca- , rti-11 Which benefited the public was manifestly, ; Thll.ell better than that which benefited the brewer.' tir„ie ease conies very opportunely at the present °. It exposes with a clearness that cannot be nais- viih(l.e,stood the hollowness and hypocrisy of the cry ' eat!ell iS raised by some people as to the " confis- , tililcln " involved in the Government's Local Veto 1' When local opponents of the proposed tern- -oco legiLlation—whether they meet as publi- ) taii3,,°r politicians—protest against what they , for Confiscation "it will certainly be necessary 1 4 them to define beforehand which class of con, , vtheation it is that they protest against—that , th leh is made for the benefit of the public, or which is simply and solely for the advantage , 4 the brewer.</p>
        <p>Atiother A ANOTHER crew of daring naviga- %editierne"C tors are about to risk their lives in • the quest for the North Pole. The nof this the latest Arctic Expedition will tMr.FREDERICK JACKSON, who is described as a wall Powerfully-built man of thirty years of age, trilth y t 4n special qualifications for the hazardous 13e,:erlDrise." The expedition of Dr. NANSEN IS —.'ng fitted out with little regard to expense. He embark in a vessel which has been specially eotis4. 'rneted to withstand the buffetings of Polar b ergS, and it will be equipped with every suit- e aPPliance that modern invention and science is rid.devisc• On the other hand Captain JACKSON 1813°8ed to plume himself on the fact that his 111.8 .pbeuclaidti4 will be the "cheapest on record." Ho no ship and he is taking only ten men. 4:„vessel will simply be a specially chartered ".1° 'tumor. Dr. DLISSEx's plan of operations</p>
        <p>is unique for its daring dependence upon a theory. He has come to the conclusion that there is an ocean current which, if his vessel gets fixed in the ice-drift, will gradually, in the course of five or six years, carry it across the Pole, and he is laying in a stock of provisions to serve him during that period. CAPTAIN JACKSON, how- ,ever, believes in the military maxim, " Keep your line of retreat open," and he will establish depots as he advances. With regard to these Arctic .expeditions some people are inclined to say, azi bone? What is the good of brave men hs-zard- ing their lives for the edapty honour of finding the Pole F We may certainly feel pretty sure that when it is found it will he a barren region, and not fit for colonization. Mr. 4-Acx.sotes answer will not he satisfactory to the utilitarians. He anticip- ates sorue accession to our knowledge of geography, of botany,and of zoology, and ho ventures to hope that he may be so fortunate as to bring back a live specimen of the musk ox,which has never yet been 56611 in this country. But, after all, the lives of a eozen good seamen are of more value to England than a musk ox.</p>
        <p>The rule of the majority is suppos- Obstruction. .ed to prevail in the House of Com- mons, but that rule is not of universal application. When, for instance, a , whole party resolves upon obstruction the major- ity is, within certain limits, helpless. The Tories and their allies were determined that the Home Rule Bill should not be read a second time before the Easter Holidays, and as they could not accomplish their object by legitimate means they have not scrupled to resort to obstruction of the most undisguised character. The result is that the Government have had to yield this point, and that debate on the second reading in which, according to their own often-repeated declaration, the Opposition were to mercilessly dissect and expose the provisions of the Home Rule Bill, has been postponed till after the holidays. The progress of the Home Rule Bill has revealed how little are the protestations of some politicians to be relied on. First of all, we had the Conservative and Liberal Unionist orators insisting with every kind of emphasis that the details of the Home Rule Bill should be revealed, and then when the time for a full explanation came they spun out the debate on the Address to a tedious length in order to cause delay. After the bill had been introduced and they had failed even to challenge a division on the first reading, we were assured that on the second reading a change would " come o'er the scene." But now rather than face the second reading these valiant politicians who are always going to do 1 great things but never do them show that they 1 are prepared to abuse the forms of the House of Commons to a scandalous and unprecedented extent in order to stave off the evil day. We are now told that the much-talked-of agitation that is to be conducted during the Easter recess is to demolish the Bill, if not the Government along with it. We, however, venture to predict that 1 after the last spark of the agitation has flickered I out the Government and its Bill will be pretty much where they were before, and that the Oppo- sition will still have to encounter the second read- ing and the inevitable defeat in the division Lobbies at the end of it.</p>
        <p>HYPNOTISM is on its trial. The steamer Naronic is considerably, overdue, and nothing has been heard of her for many days. It was expected that if she had been damaged in any way she would make for the Azores, but the latest mails from those islands give no account of her. Some vessels have gone out of their course with a view to rendering assistance if the ship had become ' helpless, but they have not been able to come across the object of their search. Under these circumstances the fate of the Naronic, is, to say the least, problematical. Here was an opportu- nity for the hypnotist to peer into the unknown, and it has been taken. According to a correspon- dent of a Liverpool newspaper there was an enter- tainment at Leeds on Monday evening, at which questions were put to a lady in a hypnotic trance. A question written on a piece of paper and held in the hands of the questioner was, "Is the s.s. Naronic safe ?" The answer was, " Yes ; she is safe, but is about two hundred miles out of her course; tidings will arrive shortly." After this definite pronouncement, those interested in hyp- notism will look for further tidings of the mis- sing Naronic with much anxiety.</p>
        <p>The influenza is amongst us once again, but, fortunately, not in the virulent form that it took a couple of years ago. Pohticians seem to be pecu- liarly susceptible to this malady. During the epidemic of May, 1891, many members of Parlia- ment were attacked, and several of them did not recover. During the last week or two quite a respectable number of gentlemen who sit on both sides of the House of Commons have suffered from the visitation, though, in these instanco, there have been no fatal results. When it was announced at the beginning of the week that Mr. GLADSTONE was laid up with a severe cold, it was naturally feared that he was another victim of the debilitating disorder, and much anxiety was felt throughout the country on his behalf. But once again it has been demonstrated that Mr. GIADSTONE'S vigorous, not to say phenomenal, constitution can repel attacks which many younger men are unable to resist. The latest report is that he has now practically re- covered, and is already fully engaged in transact- ing the onerous official business which falls to the lot of a Prime Minister. The leader of the Oppo- sition has not been so fortunate. It is authorita- tively announced that Lord SALISBURY has been prostrated by the influenza, though it is satis- factory to learn that it is not of a severe type, and that there is, as yet, nothing about it to cause alarm. Another eminent politician who is on the " sick list" is Mr. VILLIERS, M.P. He has reached the extraordinarily ripe age of ninety-two years, and the unequalled length of his Parliamentary service has earned him the title of the " Father of the House of Commons."</p>
        <p>• BILLIARD EANDIDA_P AT THE LIBERAL CLUB. On Thursday evening, at the Warrington Liberal Club, a billiard handicap was played between War- rington and Widnes -Liberal Clubs. The game re- sulted in a victory for Warrington by 330 points. After the match a tea was partaken of, served by Mr. Mitchell, the manager of the club, and it gave every satisfaction. A return match will be played at Widnes on the 30th March. The following are the individual scores: GTON.</p>
        <p>WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LRRINI</p>
