      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>ST. PAULS PAROCHIAL ROOM WIDNES. In this building on Monday evening the Rev. J. 1 Willink M.A., Vicar of St. Helens. delivered a lecture on "A tour through the Holy Land "illustrated by lantern I slides. There was a large audience. The Rev. G. H. Garrett presided. 11.0 LY TRINITY INFANT'S SCHOOL, RUNCORN. A concert of a very enjoyable nature took place on Wednesday evening in Holy Trinity Schools, Runcorn. The chair was occupied by Mr. Joseph Pritchard. The programme consisted of several part songs, all of which were very successfully rendered. Go to PRITCHARD'S for the best five guinea watch a work- ingman can have.—Advt. A Tot:Mutt, OROANIST AT RUNCORN. Master Arthur C. Timmins, son of Mr. Charles Tim- mins, of Rockfield, on Sunday morning presided at the organ in St. Paul's Wesleyan Chapel. The young organist is only 13 years of age. He carried Gat his part of the service with great credit, and he bide fair to achieve fame as a musician. RUNCORN LIBERAL ASSOCIATION, Under the auspices of this Association a lecture will be delivered in Union Hall, Regent-et,, on Wednesday evening next. The Rev. James llonby will deal with the subject " Gia4tone—patriot, orator, statesman." This association gives promise of doing some valuable work, and it is hoped that as many as possible Will join. PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for 50 years, aadouality and 11r:t-class workmanship are still guaranteed.—Advt. BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, RI7NCORN. Another of the " Pleasant Sunday evenings" took place on Sunday, when solos were rendered by Miss Johnson, Messrs. IL Pilgrim and J. Weedall. and Mr. R. W. Pierpoint gave a cornet solo. Mr. C. LeCouteur played a selection on the organ. The pastor, Rev. J. Siinpson delivered an address in which he took the negative view of the question " Is Christianity played josApArraromp ;;,:ons spect,cle, M snit all sights from a shilling a pain—Advt. DRoWNINO irr—HUti&amp;l. Rl4. 1 , About halt-Ost eight on Thursday esening„ a dead j body wee It2covered from the Albert Dock, Runcorn, by I several Seamen. It was afterwards identified as that of HUgli Rooney, labourer of Runcorn, VG ho had been miss- ing from home for live weeks. On Saturday evening the 4th February, the deceased went to Westron Point with some food for a workman there, and it is surmised that when returning in the dark he walked into toe dock and was drowned. A LUCILY WIFE DESERTER AT RUNCORN. At Runcorn Police Court, on Thursday morning, be- fore Mr. James Handley, George Dunbavin was charged under a warrant with leaving his wife chargeable to the Runcorn Union, on April 9th, 1890. Superintendent Atherton intimated that the man was apprehended under a very old warrant, and his wife was no longer , chargeable. He therefore asked for his discharge. This request was granted. JOSEPE PEITCHLEE, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 61, Higit- L at., and 4, Bridge-st., Runcera.—Advt. PRINCESS-STREET SUNDAY SCHOOL, WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting in connection with Princess street Chapel was held on Thursday evening. An excel- lent entertainment was subsequently given by the choir and friends, and during the evening the prizes for regular attendance. Ac., were distributed to the scholars by Mrs. W. Hodgkinson. The tea tables were under the care of Mesdames Roberts' Blannin Adams, Misses Wiles, Owens, and Rollason. Mr. D. Lewis pre- sided at the entertainment. JOSEPH PRITCHARD'S sale shop is 51,Hign-it., ms workshop 4, Bridge-st.—Advt. WANTON DAAILON AT DITTON. At Whines Petty Sessions on Thursday, Patrick Cross- laivl (18), James Butler (18), and Henry Barnett (19), pleaded guilty to having wilfully damaged a brick prets belonging to William Smith of Ditton. The defendants entered the brick yard and placed three fog signals into the press, which exploding blew the tap of the press off. It would cost £3 to repair the damage. Each defend- ant was ordered to pay £ll2. 81 and costs each, or, in default, 14 days' imprisonment. Both PRITCHARD'S Shops are under personal supervislen.and sustomers may rely upon receivingevery attention.—Advt. HALTON WESLEYAN TEMPERANCE SOCIETY. A meeting of this Society was held on Mondny even- ing, in the Wesleyan Schoolroam. Mr. John Bagulay, of Halewood, presided, and there was a large audience. Several songs and duets were given, and speeches made by the Rev. J. Flanby, and others. A resq,lution was unanimously carried, approving of the Direct Veto Bill introduced into the House of Commons, and urging the member for the Division of NorthWich to continue his support to the measure. When in want of a chaste and elegant wedding or birthday present go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-st.—Advt. SERIOUS CELARGS AGAINST A RUNCORN TRADESMAN. At the Chester Assizes held at Chester Castle, on Tues- day, before Mr. Justice Vaughan Williams, Thomas Fleesom, pork butcher, 47, Devonshire•place, Runcorn, surrendered to his bail upcn a charge of having at- tempted to criminally assault Anne Geary, a girl 15 years of age, upon January 30th and other dates. The jury after a hearing which occupied five hours returned a verdict of " Not guilty," and the prisoner was dis-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>charged.</head>
        <p>charged.</p>
        <p>CONFIRMATION SERVICE AT RUNCORN. On .Tuesday evening the Bishop of Chester held a con- firmation at the Runcorn Parish Church. There was a large congregation. The clergy present were the Revs. A. Maitland Wood, J. Holden, C. F. Montgomery, A. J. Jameson, G. H. Kingston, J. 11. Wilcockson, (Weston Point), A. Riley, (Holy Trinity), G. D. Wray, (Halton), and the Hon. A. Grimstom The candidates who presented themselves from the various churches num- bered—Parish Church, males, 54; females, 55 ; Holy Trinity, 1 male and 12 females; Weston Point, 3 males, and 4 females; Halton, 10 males and 16 females; Navvy's Mission, I male and 5 females. If you want a lucky wedding ring go to 51, Ilittn-sz.,whers you will get a present with it.—Advt. THE FATAL SCAFFOLD ACCIDENT AT Riniconsf. On Saturday at the North-Western Hotel, Runcorn, Mr. Worsley, the district coroner, held an inquest touch- ing the death of a painter named William White, aged 50. The deceased was engaged in painting a house in Bentwick-st. A strong wind was blo wing, and the de- ceased who was on a scaffolding thirty test high lost his balance and fell to the ground, bringing some of the planks with him. His head struck the kerbstone, and he died shortly afterwards. A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned. Joerviv PRITCHARD has the beet stock of ladles' silver and gold watches In the town.—Adet. MESSRS. EEENSZER TimstlNS AND SONS LIMITED, .RUNCORN. This is the title of a company registered with a capital of £20,000 in £2O shareS. The object is to carry into effect an agreement referred to in the articles of Asso- ciation (Clause 3) for the conversion of the business of ironfounders, engineers, &amp;c., hitherto carried on by the firm of Ebenezer Timmins at Runcorn and to carry on and extend the same. There shall not be less than three nor more than seven directors. The first are W., T. D., C. A., and A. TiMMIHS. The qualification is 200 shares and the remuneration according to profits. Jos EPEE PRITCHARD is prepared to wind and keep in repair. private clocks from 10s, per annum.--Advt. THE ALLEGED WOUNDING ET A POLE AT WIDNES. At the Widnes Police Court, on Monday, a Polish labourer named John Babouka was charged on remand with unlawfully wounding another Pole named Joseph Yackhan, on Sunday, the sth inst. Both men were em- ployed at Gaskell, Deacon's chemical works, and cn the afternoon in question they quarrelled. During the dis- pute the prisoner, it is alleged, picked up a large iron screw key and struck Yackham on the head with iton- flicting a serious wound.—The Bench, considering it a tight netween the men, dismissed the case. If you wantyour watch repaired by skilful workmen. take It to PRITCHARD'S.—Advt. SALE OF WOES AT WIDNES. In aid of the funds of Albert-road Wesleyan Chapel, a sale oe work was held in the schoolroom, on Thursday attc )n. The room was neatly decorated, and the 're well laden with miscellaneous articles. ISI!. chrhond, of Liverpool, was unable to fulfil his o perform the opening ceremony, and the duty amed by Mr. R. Cook. Mr. S. Robinson ft tat the debt on the chapel amounted to £425, ished that day to raise £l5O. Mr. Cook then brief speech, and declared the sale open. The ised was £3B 165., including £5 from Mr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATUBDAY, MARCH. 18, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>W/ ONES COUNTY *COURT. His Honour Judge Shand held a sitting of this court yesterday, but the list included no cases of public interest. WIDNES CONSERVATIVES AND Home Betz. A petition ts being signed in Widnes Conservative Club, which declares that if a separate legislature for Ireland were created. the lives and property of the loyal people of that country would be jeopardised, would cause oppression and crime and in all probability result in civil war, and would be a constant cause of grave danger to the welfare and stability of Great Britain. PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL, RUNCORN. A service of song, entitled the " Basket of Flowers," was rendered on Wednesday evening, in the above chapel. The reader was Mr. -A. R. Norman. The solos were given by Miss Hillidge, Miss Stubbs, Miss Wilson, Miss Davies, Miss Broadhurst, and Mr. A Shingler. The choruses were interpreted by the choir in a very credit- able manner. Mr. W. Gittens, jun., presided at the organ, and Mr.Hillidge acted as conductor. The proceeds were for the building fund of the new school.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY. M will be seen from our announcement columns, this society will give their third and last concert on Thurs- day evening next. Mendelssohn's great work " St. Paul "will be produced and the principals will include our clever local contralto, Miss Clara Taylor. The choruses have been admirably trained by Mr. F. H. Crossley, and they are expected to give a highly credit- able rendering of the oratorio. It is the first time " St. Paul " has been produced in Runcorn.</p>
        <p>WITH DESERTION AT WIDNES. Before Mr, T. S. Timmis and Mr. J. Brock, at Widnes Petty Sessions, on Thursday, William McNamara, a middle aged man, was charged with deserting his wife and two children in May, 1800.—Mr. Wood, relieving officer, said the family were left destitute and they had since been chargeable to the common fund of the Pres- cot Union. Relief tis the amount of £BB had been paid. The prisoner was arrested at Farnworth near Bolton, where he had gone under the assumed name of Egan.— The prisoner excused his conduct by the statement that his wife was a prostitute and that she had been con- victed of that offence in the Widnes court.—The Bench sentenced him to a month's hard labour, and Mr. Timmis advised him if there was any truth in his statement to apply for a judicial separation on his release from gaol. FUNERAL OF A FREEMASON AT RUNCORN. The mortal remains of Mr. Joseph Torkington' of 28, Greenway-road, who had died on the previous Friday, were interred in Runcorn Cemetery on Monday evening. The deeeased gentleman, who had attained the age of 63 years, came to Runcorn 30 years ago. He acted as business manager for the late Mr. James Rigby, builder and contractor for some time, and for more than 20 years filled positions of trust in the firm of Messrs. Brun- drit and Hayes. Latterly he had been in the employ of Messrs. Derbyshire, Lmited,quarry owners. Mr. Tork- ineton had taken a deep interest in church work. He was a Past Master of the Ellesmere Lodge of FreS ASI4 Accepted Masons, and was also essatiated with the Briegewater Chapter of Archmasous, and he was fol- lowed to his last resting place, by a number of the Masonic brelitelts, YOUTHFUL PICKPOCKETS AT WiDNES. On Saturday, at the Widnes Police Court, before Mr. ,H. W. Deacon, two boys, named Thomas O'Neil, ten years of age, living in Nelson-st., and Henry Murphy, aged twelve, also residing in Nelson-St., were charged with stealing a purse containing 6s. and a pocket hand- kerchief from the person of Mrs. Rathbone, 76, Churchs st. The prosecutrix stated that she was in the Co-opera- tpirvi oSnteorrsesa,bMo uttrse oa her. y-r sdh,od:reity,e.:efene ri rWie 3e slid missed s ee al t itohre purse and handkerchief from her dress pocket. She afterward welat tfn tiweeb shop opposite, and found •L • •• two prisoners there with the purse. Police Con- stable Worsfold, who received information of the robbery, deposed on Tuesday last he had the younger prisoner in custody for five hours on a charge of stealing 10s., which he admitted, but no prosecutor could be found, and he was liberated.—Superintendent Keighley also spoke to the bad character O'Neil bore. Mr. Deacon remanded the prisoners in custody. The prisoners were again brought up on Monday, and each received six strokes with the birch rod. RUNCORN ASSEMBLY BALL.</p>
        <p>The annual Assembly Ball was held in Runcorn Pub- lic Hall on Tuesday night, and the success which has attended this social function in the past was eclipsed on the present occasion. The Hall had been very effectively decorated by Mr. Steele, of Church-street, who also pro- , vided an admirable " stretcher," which was highly ap- preciated by the numerous Terpsichorean devotees who assembled. There were about fifty couples present, and the duties of M.C. were efficiently carried out by Messrs. H. Wright and T. Whinyates. Music was supplied by Massa's Band from Chester, and the programme was as follows :—Waltz, Waltz, Lancers, Waltz, Schottische, Quadrille, Waltz, Lancers, Waltz, Schottische, Lancers, Waltz, Waltz, Quadrille, Schottische, Waltz, Lancers, W alto, Lancers, Schottische, Lancers, Waltz. The catering was entrusted to Mr. Arthur Hough, of the Rifleman's Arms Hotel, which gave every satisfaction. The entire arrangements were in the hands of a Com- mittee consisting of Messrs. T. Clegg. T. Kirkham, G. Davies, 'l'. Haynes, C. Johnson, Dr. McKenzie, J. Ray- ner, E. Sherratt, J. 0. Sherratt, with Mr. J. I). Widders, hon. sec., and Mr. G. Shone hon. treasurer. THE LITE MIL ISAAC SFEAKMAN, RUNCORN. . . . .</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning, at the Parish Church, the Rev. Canon A. Maitland Wood, after the third of a series of sermons on " True Penitence," made a reference to the death of Mr. Isaac Speakman. He said they had lost one, an officer of that congregation, sue for whom they had the very greatest regard, one who had been very greatly respected, one in his character very gentle and unselfish, one to be relied upon thoroughly by his colleagues and neighbours, one whom he believed to have been sincere in his piety, one who was loyal, had passed away from them in peace with God and his fellowmen. There was no doubt his loss would be long and sorely felt ; and it was not too much to say that it would be difficult to fill his place. How wonderfully God watched over their lives, watching them at all times, taking them just at the right motjaent ; not when •they thought, but when He knew best, calling them unto Himself. Was it not better that it should be so ? It was far better than that they should have any idea when they should be called—that they should be in God's hands. Could anyone wish anything better than that when their turn came to pass away, that while the world should be sorrowing, heaven should be glad ?—At the close of the service the organist, Mr. Johnson, played the Dead March in " Saul." RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above Association on Saturday evening, under the presidency of Mr. Thomas J. Holt, a paper was read by Mr. Ssmuel M. Tetnperley on "Re- creation and amusement." In the course of his essay it was laid down that athletes were muck better without alcohol. It was shown that amusements were absolutely necessary and in this respect it was pleasant to find employers of labour encouraging their employes. The latest proof of this was the recommendation of the direc- tors ef the United Alkali Company who had that day issued a circular to the managers of their works, asking them to encourage the formation of football clubs, and offering geld and silver medals for competition. Mr. Temperley strongly depreciated gambling among young men, spoke of the duty of the church, which in the past had failed, but now saw the need of taking the matter up. He strongly defended football, remarking that it tended to elevate the tastes of the people. After which he advised them not to indulge in anything their con- science condemned. A very spirited discussion followed in which the following members took part i—Messrs. David Smith, James Moores, Ed. Rey, Jas. Bisbrown, Samuel M. Henderson, Ernest Hobson, Thomas Cooke. Daniel Bisbrown, Thomas Whyte, John Taylor, Geo. E. Button, John Barton, r, Clarke and Um Jones. There was a good attendance of members. A vote of thanks to Mr. Temperley closed the proceedings. EXTRAORDINARY CIIARGE OF HIGHWAY ROBBERY AT RIINOORN.</p>
        <p>ryuensdter irtdaanyda tmßru njacos .rHn aPnodlliecye. tCwoouryto uhnegf orme enMrn. aint:eod. Wm. Lightfoot (18), and Clement Pandlebury (20), were charged with having at an early hour on Sunday morn- ing the 12th inst. at Weston. feloniously stolen Bs. from the person of Frederick James Massey,of 6,Brunswick-st. —Mr. J. Burton defended.—The prosecutor stated that on Saturday night be went to Weston, and he left the house he had visited at 25 minutes to one. He walked along the highway. When nearing the hospital two men sprang out from the gorse bushes, caught hold of him and threw him to the ground. He recognised the men las the prisoners. Lightfoot held him by the shoulder,and Irendlebury seized his legs and lifted them up. Some money dropped from his pocket and Lightfoot picked It up. They then released him and ran away and he walked home..—Mr Burton asked if he would swear that Lightfoot picked up the money, and the prosecutor said "he fancied he did."—Wm. Whitby, black- smith, of Weston, said he was returning home along Weston - road between 11 and 12 o'clock, when he was overtaken by two men named Fraser and Limb and two others. He got into con- versation with them and they "jostled" him from one side of the road to the other.—P.C.' s Booth and Bentield proved the apprehension of the prisoners. When charged they both replied not guilty.—For the defence John Frazer, stonemason, at Weston works, whn lives at Rock House, was called, Be said he was with the prisoners from early in the evening on Saturday and later Robert Limb was with them. At eleven o'clock they met Reuben Blakelev near the Lion Hotel in Green- way-road. They walked towards Weston together. They saw Whitley at the " Slide " Whitley was drunk. They never jostled him about but they had some talk with him. Near Mr. Marshall's house they met Massey. It was then about half-past eleven, not a word was said to him, nor a hand laid upon him. He saw Lightfoot at bis house a few minutes before twelve o'clock. Pendle- bury stayed at his apartments until half-past one, and then went home down the wagon road. He had told Massey that he would sooner pay ten times eight shil- lings than think Lightfoot and Pendlebury has stolen that amount. —Reuben Blakeley and Robert Limb gave aizillar evidenee.—Mr.Burton was calling other witnesses when Mr. Brundrit stopped the case and said the magis- trates considered it was not at all proved. The defend- ants were discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>ST. PATRICK'S DAY. Yesterday was the anniversary of Ireland's patron saint, but there was little outward evidence that such was the case. Few of those green favours which were once RO common in Widnes and Runcorn were to be seen. At St. Edward's Catholic Church, Runcorn, a special service was held in the morning and a sermon preached by the Rev. Father Jerome, S.J. There was a large con- gregation. In the evening an entertainment was given in the Public Hall in aid of St. Edward's Free Schools, and Mr. Finegan, .x-M.P., delivered an address on "Ireland a nation." MARVELLOUS ESCAPE OF A RUNCORN LABOURER,</p>
        <p>Thos. Mullaney, of Windmill-street, had a marvellous escape from death on Monday afternoon. Ile was en- gaged in the work of pointing the Co-operative Stores in Church-street, and fell a distance of nearly 30 feet from a ladder on to the pavement below. His fall was emnewhat broken by the cornice of the boot shop. Ho was at once taken to Dr. McDougall's surgery, where it was found that he had escaped with a scalp wound, a scarred face, and a general shaking'. He was under the Influence of drink and had been told to leave off work before the accident happened. DEATH OF Mn. J. J. WATERWORTA OF RI:O.:CORN. After a Very brief illness Mr. J. J. Waterworth, land- lord of the Egerton Arms Hotel, Bridge-street, Runcorn, died yesterday morning at half past one. Mr. Water- ' worth was seized with an attack of inflamation of the bowels on Wednesday evening, and went to bed, and Dr. McDougall and Dr. Ormsby were in attendance, but their efforts were unavailing. The deceased was of genial temperament, and had built up a successful busi- ness as a licensed victualler, and was one of the best known men in the trade. It was that rare avis—a Radical publican, and was an intense admirer of Mr. Gladstone. Ide had only attained the age of 38 years, and leaves a widow and a family of young children to mourn his loss, and universal sympathy was expressed for then' in their sorrow. Jossen PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, Nigh- it.. and 4, Bridge at., Runcorn.—Adet. WIPE'S' WESLEYAN METHODIST CIRCUIT. The quarterly meeting of this circuit was held in the Lacey-st., ;school on Menday. The local preachers and stewards met in the afternoon and at five o'clock tea was provided by Mr. and Mrs. Edwin Wood, at whose invitation about 120 ladies and gentlemen, represeting all parts of the circuit, were present. At the busines.s meeting, in the absence through illness of the Rev. T. J. Kent, the chair was occupied by the Rev. P. J. Robert- son. The membership returns and school schedules were submitted, the former showing a slight decrease. An invitation was given to the Rev. T. J. Kent to remain another year as second minister. A vote of condolence was accorded to Mrs. Myles Evans upon the death of her husband. A unanimous recommendation was sent from the meeting to the Stafford circuit quarterly meet- ing in favour Of the Candidatere of Mr. li, M. Nield for the ministry.</p>
        <p>CHEh'S MA.TC7I: WARD,INGTON V. WIDNES. This match was played on Thursday evening last, at the Y.M.C.A. rooms, Friars-green. The follow- ing is the score :-- WARRINGTON. WIDNES. Mr. F. Dunne I Mr A. Midwood Mr. L. Chandler Mr. D. O'Sullivan Mr. E. L. Fleming Mr. G. If. Mason ~ , 1 Mr. W. Harrison 0 iMr. R. H. Dutton Mr. W. S. Jeffery 1 Mr. W. F. Marks Mr. P. Wilkinson Mr. J. S. Garland . 1 Mr. G. J. Warner Mr. H. Boni 0 Mr. A. Parkinson I .....</p>
        <p>Total</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TIIINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>TIIINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL ConnEuroNDENT. Mr. J. L. Muspratt, of th‘s Widnes firm of chemi- cal manufacturers, of that name, has been appointed chairman of Flint County Council, Mr. Maspratt is also the Mayor of the town of File,</p>
        <p>A tabular statement of the atteivEnce of the members of Chester County Council has just been issued and 1 find that Mr. Philip Speakman has tie best record of the whole council During the year ending this month, he had made 98 attendances out of a possible 115,a fact which shows a conscientious attention to the duties of the position he holcis. Next on the list comes Mr. W. Hodgson of Crewe, who has attended 90 meetings out of a possible 111. Major Hazlehurst for Runcorn North, was present at 11 meetings out of a possible 44 and the attend- ance of other councillors in Runcorn district were Alderman Rigby 70, out ot 91; Councillor W. R. , Earp 34, out of 93 ; Councillor T. Baxter 35, out of 67; Councillor J. F. L. Brunner 37, out of 53 ; Councillor H. Bratt 42, out of 55.</p>
        <p>It is a source of gratification that the Runcorn Free Library Committee have secured a course of Gilchrist lectures for next winter. The claims upon the Gilchrist Trust are Very nusasreus. Runcorn is indeed fortunate to have had a course allotted. The public will have an opportunity of listening to some of the scientific celebrities of the age upon subjects of deep interest, and as the Trust stipulate that with the exception of a few reserved seats. the charge for admission shalt be limited to Id., no one need hold aloof on the score of expense. The Free L.brary Committee have decided to form a Town's Committee to deal With the matter.</p>
        <p>If Widnes cannot boast of being a thing of beauty by any manner ot means, it has one recommenda- iion. It is a very healthy place, if we may take the death rate as a guide. Last month says the medical officer of health, it had the lowest death rate of any town in England. Personally I would prefer less gas and smoke and take my rink of swelling the death rate ; but one can't have every- thing in the world.</p>
        <p>There is not a more successfully managed under- taking in the three kingdoms than the gas and water works of Widnes,and the townspeople of that young borough derive great advantage from them being town's property. Gas at. is. 10d. nett per 1,000 cubic feet, is a surprising price, when it is considered that in the surrounding towns which are just as advantageously situated, the price is over 33. Water too is exceptionally cheap both for domestic and trade purposes, and the Corporation are now prepared to sell their surplus of 2i million gallons per day, at the rate of 2d. per 1,000 gallons. on a third of the price charged in Runcorn. Even at, that small price, Alderman Timmis informed the Council there would be a profit.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. Preston and Mr. James Pritchard, Chairman of Runcorn School Board, accompanied by the Rev. J. Hoare, of Derby, departed early in the week for a town in the Holy Land. The route to be traversed is London, Puri', Venice, Trieste, and oa to Jerusalem. The pilgrimage will be of great interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>, WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>, WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>TIIIIRSDAY.—Before His Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. JOMN DELEE v. TmE BURTON CYCLE COMPANY—The adjourned hearing of this case came on for the fourth time. The claim by the plaintiff, an independent gentleman from Halifax is £l3 Ss. 6d., consisting of £l2 paid by the plaintiff on account of the price of a bicycle which he gave a special order to the defendants to make; and £1 9s. 6d. paid for the carriage of the machine.—Mr. Davies appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. A. Browne for the defendants. A considerable amount of the time of the court was again occupied, but the result was a compromise between the parties to the effect that the case be adjourned br it month, the de- fendant to pay inte court £5 towards the costs, and make another machine according to the original agree- ment ; the machine to be examined by Mr. Temple, of Manchester, and his decision upon it to he taken. If the decision was favourable to both parties the case would be withdrawn, but if the decision was against the defendant the case would be proceeded with. His Honour was in favour of this procedure, and offered to try the case at a special court en Wednesday the 19th April, if necessary. He also mentioned that he would be pleased if the solicitors would mention it to hiss first, to give any long case a special court. TEE SUSPENDED DEBT COLLECTOR.—Wrn. Hull, debt collector, of Sale, whom the Judge suspended from con- ducting cases at the Court, a fortnight ago,had his cases ' conducted by Mr. 0. Leake, Manchester, but appeared as a witness himself. CLAIM FOR THE DETENTION OF A COAL WAGON.—Messrs. J. Oldfield and Co., coal merchants, Ethyl, claimed from Messrs. Rogers Bros. and Co.,breeze manufacturers, War- rington, £1 6s. 10d., for the detention of a wagon sent to Messrs. Monks, Hall, and Co., for breeze. Mr. Browne represented defendants. There was also a counter-claim for 68.2 d., balance of an account. Plaintiffs Tatted that they sent a postcard to Messrs. Rogers on the 6z,h Feb., stating that they had sent a wagon to Monks, Hall, and Co., to, be loaded. Immediately they received it they wrote back saying that they had left Monks, Hall, and Co. since November, and asking Messrs. Oldfield to send the wagon to the Widnes Alkali Co. Messrs. Oldfield never replied to this, but wrote ten days afterwards,and said they had not ordered the wagon to Widnes, and would charge for its detention until relieved.—Mr. Old- field informed his Honour that he paid the railway com- pany 203. a month for the hire of the wagon, and he made 30s.profit.—His flonour was of opinion that it was not so much a case of detention as of unlawfully taking away. He would, therefore, give judgment for plaintiff for Bs., as he could not take in consideration die amount of the hire. He would also give judgment for defendant on the counterclaim.</p>
        <p>RI) GBY FOOTBALL IN AN ASSOCIATION</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>ST RONGHOLD.</head>
        <p>ST RONGHOLD.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AT GOODISON PARK.</p>
        <p>(BY OUR OWN REPORTER.)</p>
        <p>An unique event in the "football world " took place on Wednesday, when those two famous ex- ponents of the Rugby game, Runcorn and Swinton gave an exhibition match, on the ground of the Everton Association Club, at Goodison Park, Liver- puol. Each club is the champion in its respective county, and their meeting in the stronghold of the rival code bad been a theme of conversation in football circles for some weeks. lam not an en- thusiast myself, but, I became infected with the general excitement,and having the necessary silver in my pocket to appease the usually rapacious rail- way company, who however, were willing on this occasion to take less than the ordinary fare. I wended my way to the railway station immediately 110 ad masticated my midday meal. There was a crowded platform and when the train put in an appearance that was quickly crowded too. The passengers entered first, second and third class compartments with the utmost impartiality, and the rule of "five on each aide "was more honoured in the breach than the observance as Shakespeare has it. While those who had no sitting accommod- ation had, perforce to stand. Each succeeding train discharged its Runcornians, Widnesians, and War- pngtonians into the seaport city and familar faces in Lime-street were almost as numerous as paving atones.</p>
        <p>I travelled to the ground in a tram car, with a man who, having friends in the district had corns from Bir- mingham to Be. the match, and he was more full of football than the average enthusiast, and he is bad enough. He had one redeeming feature. 11(9 was a great admirer of Runcorn, and he wished to lay three to on* that the Cheshire boys beat the " Swintiug Lion," but, as I had my doubts as to finding him when the battle was o'er, I resisted his tempting offer. Perhaps he found semeone who accommodated him and returned to Birmingham a poorer and a wiser man. Prophecy- leg about a football match ie as disappointing e game a.s forecasting the weather. Of course Goodison Park was reached at last, and the ground of the Evertna Club would seem to deserve all the enconiums that are passed upon it. It is the finest , football ground'l have ever seen. There is not a spot where the spectator cannot see the game, and there is accommodation under cover for 1e3,000 spectators. In fact there are large covered stands on three sides of the ground, and I Was informed that another covered stand was to bexrected on the fourth side. It is hiehly credi- table to the F.verton Executive that they should have given the exponents if a rival code the advantages of playing there and appealing to a much larger constitu- ency than they could have done at home on the little veined in Irevell-lane. No doubt tke formal thanks of the Runcorn Club will be forwarded in due course. At a quarter to five there were very few people on the ground and the stanele were practically empty, but from that time to the " kick off " people passed merrily through the turnstiles and it was computed that there were ten thousand spectators present. And there was room for another twenty thousand but unfortunately they were otherwise employing their nimble sixpences er saving them up for a Journey to Sheffield on the fol- lowing day to see their" pets" endeavour to vanquish Preston North End. However the" gate.' was worth £162 7s. 7.1., and after paying Hie expenses there would be a. most handsome corn to place in the empty exche- quer of the Runcorn Chib and to aid :he fund for the widow and family of the lamented " Tommy Davies " than whom a better footballer never donned a jersey.' P,uncorn team, led by Crompton were the first to step on the field, but many failed to receaeae them in the nufamiliar white jersey which they had donned instead of the myrtle green. §wlnten appeared a few moments later, led by the veteran Valentine. In their ranks ap- peared Bullon and Gould of Old Boys, Sudlow of St. Helens, and Hurst, of Oldham ; and Buncorn had the assistance of F. P. Jonee, captain of the New Brighton team. The teem' were as hi lows :—Runsorn : S. Houghtna, heck; F. P. Jones, J. H. Crompton, and E. F. Morris, three-quarter hacks; S. Abrams arm G. Robin- son, half nicks; W. Faulkner, P. Riley, J. Langley, Farmer, A. Taylor, S. Derbyshire, J. Davies, J. Stubbs, and H. Williams, ferwards. Swinton—Foulkes, back; fludlow, neerson, *Valentine, and Hurst, three-quarter backs; Buinbv and Brockbank, half backs; Lomax, Sharplee, Johnson, Hall, Hallam, Evans, Men, and Gould, forwards. It was expected from the records and reputations of the clubs that it would be a case of Greek meeting Greek, and the opening stages gave promise of an eve. and keen battle. Faulkner opened the play by kicking off and the first burst of cheering arose, when Jones made a splendid run to the Swinton line, alter receiving the ball from Crompton. For a few minutes Runcorn appeered likely te score but eventually Swin- ton removed ploy to the centre and afterwards until the half time whistle blew, they had the be-t of matters, and NT/dentine sad Sudlow each scored unimproved tries, as a rasa% of megnificient passing movements. The second portion of the game Was more even, but Swinton main- tained their superiority and Pearson scored a splendid try behind the posts and Valentine piloted the ball over the bar. Towards the finish Runcora pressed stronelv on several occasions but thee appeared slow to take advantage of their opportunities, and the game ended in a well deserved victory for Swinton by one goal, two tries to nil. Runcorn played much below their usual form. A new hall Was used and it appeared altogether too light and " frisky " for them, and they exhibited only occasional glimpses of that smart dribbling, picking up and pass- ing of which they are cat able. One reason perhaps for this inferior display as the fact that the halfbacks were out manceuvred by the epposing pair. Swinton played the four three-quarter system, and it itaswered edmirnbly. In every department they gave a better exposition of the game than their opponents, and their short quick passing was a treat to witness. This was in fact, about the only point the associationists , present, appeared to he able to appreciate, and the verdict of those in the vicinity of the Press box was that Rugby was a "rough and tumble" game compared with association. But a reliable verdict cannot be pro- nounced on witnessing one gains, and familiarity with the rules and the points of the game are needed for the )roper enjoyment of the rapidly varying phases of a lootball contest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>FIRE AT COCKHEDGE MILLS.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT COCKHEDGE MILLS.</p>
        <p>--- A fire broke out on Monday in the dust room of Cockhedge Mills. In this room, the Cotton is cleaned, and there is a lot of loose material lying about. The flames arose with tremendous rapidity to a height of 20 feet, but owing to the excellent precautions in vogue at the Mills, the tire was soon put out. The Cockliedge Mills are provided throughout with a very elaborate system of pipes to prevent fire from spreading. The pipes are carried along the roofs of the rooms,and at every few feet along them there is a small 'bulb of easy melting material. The pipes are always full of water, and as soon as any fire touches the bulbs, they are melted, and a stream of water pours down up- on the fire. These are termed sprinklers. On Monday, the i fire acted in this manner, and the bulbs being melted, a deluge of water was poured down upon the cotton. The rush of water through the pipes, caused by the melting of the bulbs, set off a fire alarm, with the result that the fire was extinguished, before it was generally known what was really the matter. Damage to the extent of £2O or £3O was, however,done in the short time that the fire prevailed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>._.e...____ WA It RINGTO N'S NEW BATHS.</head>
        <p>._.e...____ WA It RINGTO N'S NEW BATHS.</p>
        <p>APPROACHING COMPLETION OF THE WORK</p>
        <p>The work or completing the new baths, and the alterations to the old ones, is. now being pushed forward as rapidly as possible. Most of what novr remains to be done is joiner's work. The depart- ment in the old building that was called the two- penny bath has been altered to a ladies' bath, and about a dozen dressing boxes have been fitted up. The old sixpenny bath and the private baths, we under- stand, will remain as at present. A new entrance has been made at the end of the old baths in Legh- street, which will be the entrance to the new ones now in course of erection, The entrance will have a wood block floor and the walls will be plastered. The new bath is 75 feet long and 43 feet wide. It is much larger than the old one, there being a difference of almost 20 feet in length. The bed is composed of puddled clay and concrete, on the top of which glazed white bricks are laid eclgeways,interspersed with blue squares. The sides are lined with white glazed brick. Between sixty and seventy separate dressing boxes are now being fitted up, made of pitch pine wood. Over the dressing boxes a balcony with pitch pine railings is almost completely erected. It will be reached by three flights of stairs, and will be for the use of spectators at swimming galas, or on other special occasions. The floor round the new bath is made of concrete on iron joists, and underneath runs a subway in 'order to more easily get at the pipes when requiring repairs or altera- tions. The inside walls of the building have a dado of white glazed bricks to the height of the dressing boxes, the remainder being built with red pressed bricks. The roof is of iron principles and timber. The building is well lighted and ventilated from the roof. In addition to the boiler now standing,another has been ordered to be placed aloneside it,in order to supply the necessary steam. A new laundry will also be formed over the boilers for the purpose of drying the bathing clothes. The cost of the alterations will be £3,000, independent of heating pipes. The builders, Messrs. Gibson and Son,of Church- st., hope to have the work off their hands before the end of April. When the baths are completed, they will, no doubt, help to wipe out the reproach which has hither te been made against the Warrington Baths. Lovers of aquatic sport predict that durine the coming sum , great advance is likely to be made in town in the waymera of promoting" swimming fetes," and competitions.</p>
        <p>railings</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WA It RING TON.</p>
        <p>The Warrington branch of the Women's Liberal Association continues to display a great deal of activity. It intends, I understand, to hold a sale of work at the beginning of next month in aid of the Liberal Association. The sale of work will be opened by Mrs. E. Stewart Brown.</p>
        <p>The existence oi a peculiar club was revealed at the police court on Monday. It will no doubt be news to the general public that there should be such an institution as a swearing club in Warring- ton. The members of this society engage to pay a penny every time they swear in a public-house. It might be thought that having a lime hanging over their beads for swearing thatit would be a good check on foul language so frequently indulged in, in public-houses. This, however, does not appear to be the case, as one of the members has paid 9s. into the club, so that he had been going at it pretty heavily. The funds go towards paying for excur- sions into the country in waggonettes on Sundays in summer, and if it is this class of persons who do the " picnicing," we can very well understand the noise and clamour they make, disquieting places of worship, and making Sunday in some places a day of riot instead of a day of rest.</p>
        <p>The" protest" which wits made at the meeting on Thursday evening against the Welsh Suspensory Bill was not of an imposing character. The meet- ing was thinly attended, and Mr. John White very naturally under the circumstances deplored the " apathy "of the church people of the town. Can it be that there is anything in the threat of that London publican you referred tc last week, who said that if the Church didn't help the publicans the publicans would not help the Church ? It is just possible that the smallness of the meeting is accounted for by the fact that the publicans de- cline to " protest " against the Suspensory Bill till the Churchmen show tneir intention to" protest" against local control. They perhaps think it good tactics to hold back until the churchmen 6.eelz for An slliance, offeneive and defensive.</p>
        <p>Some of the speakers seemed inclined to make up for the smallness of the audience and the weakness of their arguments by the strength of their langu- age. Such loud-sounding words as "cowardly," "iniquitous," and" confiscation " were in plentiful request. By the way, Mr. Editor, to hear Mr. Wynne-Jones, of Wilderspool Brewery Company, talk about confiscation was amusing, was it not ? under the circumstances. 1 think Mr. Jones does not shine conspicuously on the logical side. He seemed to argue that the majority of people in Wales are opposed to Disestablishment, and then he said that Mr. Gladstone was promoting the Suspensory Bill in order to secure the " balance of power " in Wales in support of his Home, Rule Bill. But really, Mr. Jones, the " balance of power" lies with the majority, and if Mr. Gladstone is promot- ing a Bill which is obnoxious to the majority he cannot be accused of trying to secure the " balance ot power."</p>
        <p>As a matter of fact the assertion that the major- ity in Wales are against Disestablisbment is a hopeless and hollow fallacy. Out of the thirty-four members of Parliament for Wales, elected at the late election on a popular suffrage, thirty-one sup- port Disestablishment, and only three are against it. It is equally fallacious to say that the majority of Welshmen are Churchmen, because it is well known that everywhere the Non- conformists form the immense majority of the people. "Their chapels, as Archbishop Tait once said, are to be seen, in every village and on every hillside; and while these chapels very generally have overflowing congrega- tions, the churches in many parts are almost empty The lzte Mr. Matthew Arnold, although a supporter of the Establishment, yet declared that in some parts of Wales almost the whole population is out- side it ;' and he further said, There are districts of Cardiganshire and Carmartlienshire ' in which I suppose no one will deny, almost every soul is a dissenter."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TUE ARPLEY RIVER DEVIATIOIN.</head>
        <p>TUE ARPLEY RIVER DEVIATIOIN.</p>
        <p>The construction of the new road in coanectiorit with the Ship Canal works, Which is to run along- side the river deviation through Arpley nanadows,is in progress. The iron bridge con‘ eying it across the river just below Walt's Arches was opened last week, aud freight wagons with stone and other material for the new road are using it. The road will be 211. feet wide with two footpaths each six feet wide. The whole of this road, except where it crosses the river to the Wilderspool _crossings, is under the authority of the Warrington Highway Board, who received an intimation respecting the commencement of work from Mr. Lcaier Williams on Wednesday. The river deviation, which has been full of water for some months, will not be opened for six months yet. The water at pre- sent in it, some millions of gallons will have to be pumped out,and the bed deepened some six feet, accord- ing to the agreement. Two large pumping engines have already been erected. The whole of the work there, however, is likely to meet with delays until the con- struction of the docks has been definitely settled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON SCHOOLMISTRESS. -- EXTRAORDINARY ALLEGATION.</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON SCHOOLMISTRESS. -- EXTRAORDINARY ALLEGATION.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, on Thursday, before Alderman Chandley and Sutton, Charles Campbell, Bakehouse-yard, Latchford, was charged with neglect- ing to send his child regularly to school. Defendant did not appear, but was represented by his wife, who stated that the schoolmistress caned her boy every Mon- day morning when he went to school, because he did not go to confession on Saturday night. The boy, who was present, repeated the statement to the magistrates Alderman Chandley : I should like to make a memoran- dum of that. The boy's mother said it was all because he would not tell a lot of lies to Almighty God. Mr. Riley (school attendance officer) : It is an extraordinary and cruel thing to say, and I thing it would be well to adjourn the:case. Mrs. Campbell further alleged that it was not only her child, but every child in the school was punished for the same cause. Alderman Chandley: How do you know that? Mrs. Campbell : They have come to me with blisters on their hands and have told me. Alderman Cha ndley : I shall certainly adjourn this. case until next Thursday.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF CORPORATION OFFICIALS. A meeting of the General Purposes Committee of the Warrington Corporation was held on Monday afternoon, at the Town Hall, under the presidency of the Mayor, for the purpose of appointing officials of the Corporation. Mr. John Fairhurst, who has been for many years collector of rates,was appointed borough treasurer at a salary of £350 per annem, and Mr. W. H. Woodcock was appointed collector of rates and assistant treasurer at a salary of £2OO per annum, each of these two officers being required to devote the whole of his time to the duties of the office. The vacancy in Mr. Longdin's office, owing to the death of Mr. F. W. Walker. was also filled up by the appointment of Mr. John Sutcliffe to the post of assistant borough sur- veyor. Mr. Sutcliffe is 25 years of age, and he has been eight and a half years in borough surveyors' offices, hav- ing also baen for two and a half years assistant to the Borough Surveyor of Bolton. There were 115 candi- dates for the post. A special 'meeting of the Town Council will be held to-day for the purpose of confirming these appointments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>UNION CnußcH.</p>
        <p>A most comprehensive and enjoyable lecture was delivered in the Union Church, Frodsham Bridge, on Tuesday evening, by Mr. James Wilding, of Liverpool, the subject being " The Cathedrals of England," illus- trated by a number of lime-light views of some of the most interesting designs from the earliest period to the present century.</p>
      </div>
