      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>DO YOU ENJOY PERFECT HEALTH?</head>
        <p>DO YOU ENJOY PERFECT HEALTH?</p>
        <p>IF NOT WHY NOT ?</p>
        <p>Many persons suffer from ill-health and, it may be, are .afflicted for years, simply.because they have transgressed the Laws OF NATITRE.</p>
        <p>The irnplest manner for man to secure immunity from sense Is toobserte and follow these laws as far as practic- able, and if illness overtakes him to take as remedies only such preparations as are supplied by nature. The rally NATITIILL remedies are vegetable, and the most renowned and efficient vegetable remedy of the present age isGwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters.</p>
        <p>This preparation is now extensively taken throughout the country by pa- tients suffering from weakness, ner- vousness and indigestion, with their numerous and varied symptoms, and jts efficacy in all cases of the kind has been successfully established, and is attested by thousands of testimonials trot,. every district where it has been tried. Its claims have been tested and 'proved by the medical profes,ion snd others, and corroborated by the written testimony of eminent men. The Quinine Bitters contain not only a suitable quantity of quinine in each dose, but the active principles of the following well-known medicinal herbs: —Sarsaparilla, Burdock, Dandelion. Gentian, Lavender. and Saffron. The use of quinine is well known, but it was never satisfactorily combined with these preparations until the inventor of these Bitters, after many and long continued experiments, was able to secure a perfectly uniform combination of all the essential properties of the above plants in their greatest purity and concentration. It is a tonic: " Pick-me-up," scientifically pre- pared in happy proportions. Gwilym Evans' Bitters are ree:un- mended by itoctors, Analysts, and Chemists for Weakness, Indigestion. Nervousness, Depression of Spirits, and all Winter Ailments. It is invaluable in restoring strength to persons who are weak after a long illness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
