      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>BEFORE THE M.A.GISTRITES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE M.A.GISTRITES.</p>
        <p>AN INCORRIGIBLE WIFE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, -at tlie -13-O-rO4;1-1-1;olice-O-Ourt, before Mr. John Burgess (in the chair), and Mr. L. Voiaey, Annie Bond was charged with stealing at different dates two blankets and a woman's skirt, from Mrs. Ryder 8, Bax- ter-st. Prisoner pleaded guilty. From the evidence it appeared that the prisoner had pledged the two blankets at Mr. Briggs', pawnbroker, Powys-st., but he having his suspicions aroused refused to take the skirt in pledge, and he had also returned the blankets. The prisoner's husband would not appear, as he could do no good with her. She was sent to gaol for one month's hard labour on the charge of stealing the skirt, and 14 days for each of the other two charges, making two months in all.</p>
        <p>A SWEARING Cum. At the Warrington Police Court, on Monday, John Baguley, 6, Mill-at., was charged with assaulting Wm. Monaghan in Golden Square, on March &amp;h. P.C. Cor- coran gave evidence of the assault, but defendant said that Monaghan was the aggressor. He had two wit- nesses but they refused to come as they were in a swear- ing club with Monaghan. Defendant explained that a swearing club was a number of men who pined together each man agreeing to pay Id. every time he swore in a public-house. The Proceeds of the club go to pay for excursions in the country. One of the witnesses had paid 9s. into the club funds, and he said that he did not wish to,drop out now summer was coming. Defendant was bound over for six months, and ordered to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>PUBTACAN SUMMONED.</head>
        <p>A PUBTACAN SUMMONED.</p>
        <p>John Hollingworth, of the Roan Cow, St. James'-st., was charged with being drunk in that public-house on March 9th. Mr. Arthur Browne defended. P.S. Taylor said that on the above date defendant's wife came to him crying and made a complaint against her husband, who had struck her. Witness went to the Roan Cow, of which defendant is the publican, and found defend- ant drunk in the bar. Witness called him into the passage and told him that he would report him.—Mr. Browne hoped that the Bench would look over the matter this time as it was defendant's first appearance. He had been stabbed in the hand at Christmas, and had since suffered from rheumatic fever, so that it took very little drink to take effect upon him. The Bench ordered defendant to pay the Cogs. JOHN WARRINGTON BECOMES FASTIMOI736 On Wednesday at the Borough Court before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman), and Mr. John Geddes,John War- rington, was charged with sleeping on the premises of Edmund Mills, Tilley-st.' intending to commit 'a felony. The prisoner had made afire in one of the stoves, to the danger of about £l,OOO worth of goods. He was found crouched up within a few inches from the stove.—Mr. Taylor (P.C.M.), said he took him to the firewood fac- tory at Bootle on Tuesday last, where had he worked he would have been able to earn good wages. But after working a few hours he" chucked up" and made his way on foot to Warrington. In reply to the magistrates he said he only received fourpence for his labour, and thinking it not sufficient he thought he could do better elsewhere. Mr. Hunt (chief constable) asked him how long he worked, and in reply he said from 2.30 to 5.30. The magistrates sent him to gaol for one month with hard labour. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>" FUNNY " BUSINESS TRANSACTION..</head>
        <p>" FUNNY " BUSINESS TRANSACTION..</p>
        <p>- At the Warrington Borough Court on Friday, Edgar Pearce, Arplev-street, was charged with assaulting Frederick W. Wood on the 14th inst. Complainant said that on the above date he and a friend were going past Blawson's shop, at which defendant was employed. DeFendant asked them to go inside. Defendant lifted down some cloth on which was a ticket. Witness picked up the ticket, and putting it on his breast, asked defendant how he would look in the window with that on. Defendant using some foul language told him to put the ticket down. Witness in doing to, touched de- fendant on the head with the ticket. -Defendant upon this struck him in the side with his elbow, and then in the mouth, cutting it. A witness named Whitehouse corroborated complainant's statement.—Defendant said that he had been troubled with head ache previous to the occurrence, and asserted that complainant gave him a blow with the sharp edge of the card.—The magis- trates thought this was a funny way of transacting business, and fined defendant 10s. including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGT ON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>Mom]Ay.—Befors Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Alderman Francomb, Messrs. Wm. Pierpoint, J. W. Hayward,</p>
        <p>and S. Wright. ABSCONDING FROM THE WOREHOUSE.—WiIIiBIII Tom- linson was charged with absconding from the Work- house on March 4 with clothing belonging to the Guar- dians.—Mr. Ward, workhouse master, said that prisoner was admitted into the workhouse on September 4, 1892. and absconded on the above date. On Friday, witness received a telegram from Castle Donnington saying that prisoner was in custody at that place. Prisoner was sentenced to seven days without hard labour. ENLISTING UNDER A FALSE NAME.—For attempting to enlist in the East Lancashire Regiment under a false name, Joseph Dempsey was sentenced to a month hard labour. ALLEGED DESERTION.—George Maxwell was charged with being a deserter from the 2nd Battalion of the Cheshire Regiment stationed in Chester in 1888.—Pri- soner said that he had been punished for the desertion, and had since received his discharge from the army.l Prisoner was discharged. DRONE AND DISORDERLY.—For being drunk and dis- orderly in Horsemarket-st. on Saturday night, Emma Jones was discharge,' on promising to leave the town. —Ellen Johnson for being drunk and disorderly in Irlam- st. on Sunday night was also ordered to leave the town. —Harriet Edwards for the same offence in Ship Yard on March 8 was ordered to pay the costs, and Rose Kelly for this offence in Dial-at. was fined ss. including costs. DRUNIE.—For this offence in Irlam-st., en Monday morning, James Brown was ordered to leave the town. A ROGUE AND VAGABOND.—WiIIiam Foy WAS charged with being a rogue and vagabond and with sleeping in a closet of? Walsh's Yard on Monday morning.—P.C. Evans proved the case, and prisoner's mother said that her son would not work and that she wanted protection from him.—Prisoner was sentenced to one month hard labour.—John Smith was charged with committing the same offence in Golden Grove-st. on the previous Satur- day morning. The case had been adjourned from the previous Saturday owing to prisoner stating that he was reftlsed admittance into the workhouse by Mr. Curry on Friday med.—Mr. Curry, however, said that prisoner applied for the admittanJe on Friday night week, and was told to go to the relieving officer. He went away and had not returned to the workhouse since.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for 14 days bard labour. CHIMNEYS ON FlRE.—Michael Pinnett, 58 Dallam-lane, was fined Is. for having his dwelling-house chimney on fire on March 8, and William Quarmq, 31 New-road, was fined Is. for the same offence on the 9th inst. FOOTBALL IN THE STRICET.—Fotir boys, Thomas Cooke, 2 Selby-st. George Davies, 3trOaribaldi-st., Ben Tim- mins, 1 Collin-st.; and John Pritchard, 17 Cart wright-st., were each Charged with playing football in Cartwright- at. on Sunday the sth inst.—Mr. Arthur Browne ap- peared for the delendant Cooke.—P.C. Delahay stated that he caught the last three defendants who were play- ing football. They gave him the name of the first defendant, but witness could not swear to seeing him play. It afterwards appeared that the wrong name bad</p>
        <p>been given and Cooke was accordingly :discharged and the other three were fined with the costs.</p>
        <p>TIIRSDAY.—Before Mr. R,Garnett (chairman), Mr. E.</p>
        <p>J. Guest and Dr. Starke i Smith.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For this offence in Horse- market-st., on Monday, Rose Ann McDonald was dis- charged on promising to leave the town, and Ellen Gray alias Kelly, for being drunk and disorderly in Rigby-st., on Monday night, was fined ss. and costs or seven days imprisonment. Assattrif ON A WlFB.—James Varley was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-road, on Mon- day, and with assaulting his wife, Agnes Varley, on the 11th inst. D.S. Monaghan stated that prisoner's wife got a warrant against her husband on Monday, and on going to arrest him, he was found to be drunk, and gave them considerable trouble in getting him out of the house.—Agnes Varley said that she went to the Star beerhouse for her husband on the 11th inst., and he be- came very violent, and kicked her and hit her on the head, She applied for a separation.—Prisoner was sent Ito gaol for three months, and a separation order was granted, prisoner being ordered to pay 12s. per week to his wife's maintenance after he comes out of gaol. REFUSING TO QUlT.—Ellen Pearson was charg ed with being drunk and disorderly in Horsetnarket-street, on Monday, and refusing to quit the Hop Pole public-house, and also with breaking a plate glass window, doing damage to the extent of Bs. P.C. Cunliffe spoke to , arresting prisoner in Horsemarket-st. She was drunk and using filthy language. Her face and hands were covered with blood caused by breaking the Hop Pole window. Miss Coop, landlady of the Hop Pole, said the prisoner wanted some beer, and on being refused, she ' went out and broke the window. Pris.ner was fined ss. and costs or seven days imprisonment for being drunk, and was ordered to pay Bs. for the damage to the window or in default serve another seven days im- prisonment.</p>
        <p>W-EDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John_Fairclough (chairman)</p>
        <p>and Air. John Geddes.</p>
        <p>TILE SLEEPNO-OUT CASE.—The boy Hennigan who was caught sleeping in Bewsey Forge during the night of the Bth inst. and was remanded Ist week, was discharged on promising to go to Father Berry's Home, Liverpool, where Mr. Taylor, P.C.M., said there was a vacancy.</p>
        <p>THUBSDAY.—Before Alderman Chandley (Chairman),and</p>
        <p>Alderman Sutton.</p>
        <p>Scitoot. school cases were dealt with :—Thomas Fearnley, Scotland-rd., fined 2s. 6d. ; John Geulden, Howley-lane, 24. 6d.; Thomas Hughes, Bevoley-rd„ ss. ; Joseph Ireland, Hind-st., 2s. 6d. • John McDonald, Rose and Crown-st, dismissed; Charles Packer Howard-st., dismissed • Fred Roberts, Keen-st., dismissed; 'd; John Rooney,Cockhedge lane,2s.6d .Mr. George Riley, school attendance officer conducted the prosecu- tions.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Mr. John White (chairman), Aldermen Wright, and Councillor Roberts. ARREARS.—For failing to pay arrears Lr the mainten- ance of his son on the ship Clio, Edward Thompson, 52, Howard-st., was bound to appear in a month. QUARREL BETWEEN NEIGHBOURS.—James Brown, 19, Albert-terrace, and Harriet Rogers, 21, Albert-terrace, were charged with assaulting and beating Ellen Warbur- ton, 17, Albert-terrace, and Wm.Warburion, of the same address,on the 12th inst. Complainant said that Rogers had made a false accusation against her, and on asking her about it she took witness by the hair and pulled her to the floor. Witness's son came to her assistance, but Brown struck him. The two defendants were ulti- mately sent away by a lodger named John Burns. The magistrates dismissed the case, ordering each party to ay their own costs. ALLEGED THREATS TO A WlFE.—George was charged with using malicious threats to his wife, Annie Gill on the 13th inst. The Chief Constable said defendant had promised to keep sober and to sign the pledge that morning, and, as there had been nothing agait.st him before, he (Mr. Hunt) recommended that the case be withdrawn. Defendant was accordingly dismissed on paying the costs. A LEVANTING TENANT.—SamueI Weedall, was charged with unlawfully carrying off goods and chattels, forfeited to Mr. John Corradice, in lieu of two weeks rent for a house in West-st., occupied by defendint. A restraint had been levied on the goods. He was ordered to pay Bs. Bd. to the prosecutor, and pay the nests of the case.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WZDNESDAY.—Before_ Captain</p>
        <p>Withington (chairman),</p>
        <p>and Mr. W. Mortimer.</p>
        <p>DOG OWNERS WITHOUT LICENSE3.—The following persons were summoned for having dogs without licenses :—John Hollerhead, Burtonwood ; Thos. Hough- ton, Burtonwood ; John Williams, Burtonwood ; Joseph Mattress, Sankey Bridges; David Beddard, Sankey Bridges; Wm. Burgess, Sankey Bridges; Thomas Morgan, Sankey Bridges ; Win. Stuart, Sankey Bridges; George Bickers, Sankey Bridges ; Annie Bush, Penketh ; Samuel Hunt, Penketh ; William Harnpson, Sankey ; James Wright, Cuerdley ; Win. Scott,Cuerdley, and were all fined is. and costs: . .</p>
        <p>THErr OF Com...—William High= was charged with stealing 271bs. of coal,valued at 2d., the property of Mr. Wm. Griffiths (agent), at the new Ship Canal cutting, Arpley. P.C.Smith said that he met prisorer on the iron bridge, Arpley, with a bag of coat on his t.:ck. When asked what he had,prisoner dropped the bag alid ran off, but was caught in Wilderspool-road. Witness handed him to P.C. Dugdale,of the county police. Prisoner was fined ss. including costs. THEFT OF A Ducs.—Moses Twist and John Marsden, were each charged with stealing a duck, the property of Peter Dumbell, Burtonwood, on March 11. Peter Dum- bell identified the duck, and P.S. Devlin said that be met the two prisoners in Hood-lane on the 11th inst, at 2 a.m. The prisoner Twist was carrying a duck, and when hesaw witness he dropped it in a hedge. Witness arrested the two prisoners, suspecting that they had stolen it. He found that the duck's head had been cut off, and traced it to Dumbell's house,where it was found that the duck cote had been broken into. Prisoners were sent to gaol for one month. ASLEEP WHILE DRIVING.—For this offence, on the 3rd inst., at Itiaton, William Kenyon was fined 2s. 6d. and costs.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For this offence on January 30, in Sankey Bridges, Charles Maxwell was fined 2s. 6d. and costs or 14 days; and George Wood was fined 29.6 d. and costs for being drunk at Poulton, on the 10th inst.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Before Sir Richard M. Brooke and Major . .</p>
        <p>Hazlehurst.</p>
        <p>TEMPORARY AUTHOR—I-T-Y-: -L-i;Q-iUT Royle applied for a temporary transfer of the license of the Bull's Head, Halton-road, Runcorn, from Wm. Bramhall.—Granted. ALLEGED ASSAULT ON A MOTHER-IN-LAW.—James Cooke was summoned for assaulting his mother-in-law, Emma Woodtine, on the 7th inst. Mr. Burton represented de- fendant. The case was dismissed. __</p>
        <p>. NON-PAYMENT OF RATES.—There were 40 persons sum- moned for non-payment of their poor rates and ten tor non-payment of district rates. In the majority of cases a settlement had been effected, but in the others orders were made for payment within a week. DRUNKENNESS.—James McTurk was fined 10s. and Bs. 6d. costs for being drunk and disorderly in Hig h-st., on the 6th inst.—James Kelly was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Church-st. on Saturday night ; fined ss.</p>
        <p>WIDNES PETTY SESSIONS. THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. T. Sutton Timmis and • J. Brock. FINED FOR DRUNBENNESS.—Catherine Walsh, no Axed abode; Thomas Baxter, Cholmondeley-st. ; Bose Kelly, no fixed abode; George Johnson, Mersey-road; Thomas O'Toole, Nelson-st. ; William O'Toole, Moss Bank ; John Mitchell, Moss Bank; and Patrick McManus, Victoria- street.</p>
        <p>BREACHES OF THE PEACE.—William Fyldes and John Bouch, John-st.,were charged with committing a breach of the peace, and were hound over to be of good beha- viour for two inonths.—William Fyldes and Mary Fyldes, husband and wife, were charged with fighting together, and causing a disturbance. On promising not to repeat the offence, the defendants were dismissed on payment of costs.</p>
        <p>_ A QUARREL BETWEEN NEIGHBOURS.—Richard Clarke, William-St., appeared to answer a summons, charging him with having assaulted Mary E. Hall, a married woman,residing hiDaillfield-road. Mr.Kuowles appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. F. J. Poole defende.l. The defendant was bound over for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>CONFESSION OF MURDER AT HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>CONFESSION OF MURDER AT HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>TRIER AND SENTENCED TO DEATH IN FIVE MINUTES.</p>
        <p>At Leeds assizes, on Thursday afternoon, Arthur Shaw, Seventeen years of age, ship's cook, pleaded guilty to an indictment charging him with the wilful murder of Susan Toivnsend, an old woman, at Halifax, on May 24th, of last year. On the evening of that date Mrs. Townsend was found dead with her skull smashed in. Considerable mystery surrounded the affair, and was only cleared up when the prisoner about a month ago confessed to having committed the crime. By his own statement It appears that he went to deceased's house and asked for a penny so that he could get loigings. She refused to give him the money, and thereupon he dashed her brains out with a hammer. On Thursday no evidence whatever was offered, and Mr. Justice Bruce at once sentenced him to death, only five minutes elapsing between his appearance in the dock and the judge's sentence.—Shaw, when he confessed, was in custody charged with shamefully assaulting a little girl.</p>
        <p>The Committee of the Na Copal Conservative Temper ance Union are reported to have arrived at the decision hat the Local Veto Bill would not in operation promote he cause of temperance. The Royal Laundress es say Critchley's "STARCH GLess s unequalled for impartitur a beautiful finish to starched ac., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wall, says l—" My wife will not finish our linen without your " Starch Gloss.' From Mrs. —, Bradford "Dear sir,— I am pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at Id., 3d., and 6.1., by mi St starch selle.:s. If not in stock will procure it. 'X. • CEITCHLET, BlAckburn. P.oprietor.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>I i</head>
        <p>I i</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1893. TO ENGLAND A.ND BACK. SOME SABBATH ASPECTS OP VISITING THE OLD COUNTRY. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>(Concluded.) (No. 4.)</p>
        <p>The writer of the following account of a journey "To England and Back in the Winter Season," resides in Seattle, Washington, U.S.A., but is the on of a gentleman much respected in Gateacre, and the sou-in-law of a well-known Warringtonlan.</p>
        <p>A general rush is made to the Library to secure a table in order to pen a few lines to triends, or perhaps a cable or telegram noting safe arrival to the expectant ones, so that they should know what time to meet the steamer. All such messages are handed in to the saloon steward, and transferred at Queenstown for despatch upon the steamer's arrival. On account of the great con- sumptiln of coal and nearing the end of our journey. the steamer was pretty high out of the water and therefore rolled a great deal, so that writing letters under such trying circumstan,es is far from easy or pleasant, for holding on to a table with one hand, paper and pen with the other is inclined to make one think that something Is going decidedly wrong_ Chairs rushing hither and thither as though wound up by clockwork, and war- ranted to go in any position. Taking all these incon- veniences into account, don't be surprised if your "Don't forget to write" is not always heeded.</p>
        <p>Who has not when writing a letter raised his head and looked around for may be a subject, or perhaps to aid one as to an idea. So I look around and think that perhaps a short account of the beautiful room may prove interesting. Placed on the promenade deck over the saloon it is in a central position, well lighted and ventilated, just the place one could picture as an ideal room. The walls are panelled in oak, with handsome carvings relieved by 6eautiftil designs produced by burning into the wood, the ground work being then gilded. This is apparently a novel yet very attractive style of ornamentation. The furnishing is excellent, the chairs and lounges being exceedingly comfortable. Nor can we forget the handsome writing tables and book- cases, the latter containing nearly 1,000 volumes of well selected works. At night the port holes are covered by coloured glass slides, giving the room a cozy appearance. Needless to say such an apartment was very popular with all on board. But to remain at a table too long would be to deprive some anxious one from writing a letter, so having completed a few hasty lines I vacate my chair which is soon taken. The postal box is placed in the saloon companionway. As letters are dropped therein how can we help thinking what a wonderful agency the postal union is—hourly the bearer of good and bad news. Even to stand for a few minutes before any ordivary pillar box and look into the faces of the bearers of the little missives. Here the buainess man drops in a handful of letters, &amp;c., with a look of satisfaction; then perhaps the office or errand boy with an indifferent," what-do-I-care " sort of a look, struggles to crush a whole handful through the opening; then comes along the blushing maiden taking a farewell look ' to see if the stamp is exactly in the rignt place, the ad- dress written in faultless style, hesitatingly she drops it in—yet ere it has reached the bottom of the box she remembers, that she forgot to add a second postscript and would like to have it back, but the recipient being ignorant of her wishes is content, and ere long is just revelling in the latest—whether it be sister, brother, cousin, or well we will leave to the imagination theotber one. Then cornes,perhaps,Paul Pry, anxious to mail his friends letters, yet reading the contents of any postal cards as he comes along, closely followed by the profes- sional debt collector with his" pay-up-and-100k-pleasant" countenance. Ah ! here's the actor probably accepting some offer, even in the posting of a letter a good deal of stage action is noticeable; nor must we forget Paddy of Cork—how he, after carefully Writing his letter to his friend, placed the same in a clean unaddressed envelope proceeded to the pillar box dropped in his letter with a cry down the .aperture, 'Hurry this on to Timothy O'Rafferty of Tipperary, here's a penny for the stamp and twopence for yourself for hurrying."</p>
        <p>As noon approached upon looking over the ship's run it read : December 17, strong S. W. wind moderating, ends fine, overcast hazy S. W. sea, 435 miles. To many this was a great disappointment, for regardless of the distance, they had counted upon entering Queenstown on Tuesday morning. The uncompleted run to that port was 210 miles, therefore all hope of entering the harbour by daylight died away. Not even a vessel appeared in sight the whole day. Yet /Shortly after lunch a mist arose gradually, turning to a thick fog, the loud whistle sounded at intervals, and general uneasiness reigned amongst the passengers, for who cares to be near land in a fog. There was nothing for it but to wait patiently until it lifted and think we would be doing well if we reached Liverpool any time on Wednesday,instead of,as at first expected, Tuesday night or early next morning. Moral: Nerrer count on uncertainties.</p>
        <p>Never air your nautical knowledge among seafaring men, or you will be quickly convinced how much you know.</p>
        <p>Never hang a celluloid collar on the electric light bracket unless you have more than one; fire in the state- rooms, is not allowed.</p>
        <p>Never eat simply because you want to get your money's worth of every feature of the voyage: everybody has to make sacrifices in life.</p>
        <p>Never forget the location of your stateroom. Never, no never, volunteer to hold a baby whilst mamma goes on deck for a breath of fresh air, or you'll regret it. Never imagine yourself in a hotel, and throw your boots out into the corridor at 2 a.m., with noise loud enough to waken the dead and drive the living crazy. Never wear a silk hat or it will be ruiaed and only fit for a St. Patrick's Day parade. Never go to meals if you doubt your ability to remain at table ; this lack of consideration for others has often disastrous results.</p>
        <p>Never ask for your trunk,when an you want to get out of it is a paper of pins or a nail brush. Never omit to contribute to the Seamen's Fund box ; you owe it as a thank offering for a safe voyage to the haven where you fain would go.</p>
        <p>Next morning we were told we had passed Queens- town—the weather would not permit of our entering the harbour—and that Liverpool would be reached that night. What welcome news, and what a glorious day. True, it was rather cold, but as we passed up the Channel, what did we think of it. On the left, a view of the Irish Coast, whilst to the right the mountains of Wales were discernible. It was no uncommon sight now to see a steamer or sailing vessel. As the day advanced the coast line of Wales became very distinct, the Cambrian range rising high into the heavens with proud Snowdon towering above all the snow capped summits. In the distance Holyhead could be seen and from here our arrival was to be announced all over the world, through the a gency of Lloyd's shipping bureau. What a welcome sight it was for us to see those welcome signal colours run up and answered from the Mainland. One by one the lighthouses along the coast were passed, each one now flashing its warning signal. Just off Point Lynas the S. S. " Adriatic " White Star line, outward bound for New York, signalled us. Just after this we descended to take our last meal, thank and reward the stewards for their kindness and attention during the trip.</p>
        <p>And here I would say that very few have any idea the amount of work these good hearted fellows have to do in order to cater to the wants of such an army of passengers. Of course we could not think of leaving without interviewing our bedroom steward, who had by this time arranged all our several packages, leaving us little to do. All this takes time, and when once on deck again the Bar Lightship was in view. Oh ! what a wel- come and familiar sight, and there in the far off distance a dazzling glare from countless lamps lighting up the Heavens under which peacefully lay the city of Liver- pool, on the banks of the river Mersey. As we crossed the Bar and steamed slowly up the river waiting for the various tenders to come alongside, try to imagine the excitement and expectancy within many hearts. The North Fort and New Brighton Battery are passed —but no tender has yet arrived, so a signal of green fire is noticed on the bow of our steamer. Shortly afterwards it is answered by the little tug boats, which had by some means got away astern of our mighty steamer. Once alongside it was not difficult to pick out familiar faces, and a hearty welcome awaited us to old England's shores. Bidding adieu to the captain and many passengers and almost with a feeling of regret—so at- tached had we become to our ocean home—we left for shore. Three hearty cheers for captain and crew were given, the gangway drawn in, the ropes cast off, and our little tender soon landed us alongside the Liverpool landing stage. But not so our baggage, for by some means the whole has become mixed, the sorting up of the packages belonging to nearly 1,300 souls was neither an easy nor short task.</p>
        <p>Midnight was just striking as I bid farewell to my fellow voyagers, they deciding to stay in the city, whilst I drove home, there to receive a real good English wel- come, and no incentive was necessary to feel that "There is no place like home." I will not trouble any as to our movements in dear old England—the greater portion of the time we spent together visiting various places of interest, though in the case of two of us but for short periods; home having fargreatet attractions than Loodon, Paris, Edinboro, etc., 'could offer. Ana here I will conclude by taking an extract from Charles Lever, which goes a long way to express my position upon walking my native streets. " And one by one each familiar object caught my eye, I could almost have fancied the long intervals since I had been there before like a mere dream. National physiognomy too has a strange effect on him who has been so long absent from his country. Each face one meets seems well known. The traits of features to which the eyes were so well ac- customed awake a memory of individuals,and tis some- times a most difficult task to distinguish between the acquaintance and the passing stranger."</p>
        <p>"SAVEZ."</p>
        <p>CONCERNING SOME YOUNG MEN-</p>
        <p>BY LIMB BLUNT.</p>
        <p>If the reader discovers any allusion in this article which he thinks bears a relation to any innocent act of his, I hope he will not take it as a covered thrust at him. To prevent him feeling theslightest annoyance I will make this honest confession—in secrecy of course—the allusion is not meant for him, but the other fellow. I have been in Warring- ton some time and found it a very interesting place; it has nearly charmed me to death, and it abounds in a refulgence of interesting phases vvhicn would enable a rational man to do away with the dissipa- tion ot novel reading.</p>
        <p>Sundays I find the most interesting days. One can get such a study of the youth of the town then; when it comes out in all its winning sim- plicity, and good nature, and best clothes. Summer Sundays especially are the best days. You can then see youth, free from the garb of ail restraint, fling himself into a sea of innocent pleasure which should infuse his intellect with an ecstatic intoxi- cation, i.e. when he has any intellect. Some of them are so heedless, " don'tcher know," that their "papa" and "maters" won't let them out with such a light, transient, and easily lost thing as an intellect. So they come out without, but let on to have it with them, Is hict is the next best thing. The park is where you will find these sprouting sprigs of Warrington's glory, growing like poppies in the warmth of their own sunshine. You find them strolling about in groups, with hats tilted over their eyes,and consumptive looking moustaches growing on their upper lips, and pipes in their mouths. They don't indulge in small talk—smart sayings are what they use, and their jokes are just like desert ; they are chestnuts. You get into the park on a Sunday afternoon, and maybe turn down a quiet path for a walk. Occupying the whole of a form you will probably find one of ttese inter- esting groups; and if you are young lady unlimited joy plainly walks into their eyes upon 'observing you. Every eye is upon you ; the group exchange joyous w„inks, grins, nudges, and other mute though expressive symptoms of elation. Something makes you appre- hensive, and you don't seem to be able to walk straight or look at anything in any way but as though you were found in the act of going to steal it. Then you get opposite, and something like this greets you :—" Poor Tottie. Oh, don't she 'pear lonely ?" "Poor little thing Won't Charlie come —ain't he finished cigars and wine yet?" "Shall I come and show you the birds ?" "My dear, your velvety cheek is suffused with a seraphic blush. It ain't Chelmon's paint, 'eh e Then they all laugh in their simple way; but, your blushes are frizzling your cheeks; your senses are slushing around like hot butter, and confusion won't describe your state. It is no use looking bra seat. They are all occu- pied by the same happy young sparks; they put you through the same ordeal, and inside 20 minutes you feel crushed, and silly, and scattered, and mad, and flushed, and you want to cry, and then rush off home by the side-streets, and spank your baby- brother. You probably know one of those young men, and when you meet him in Sankey-et. (luting the week, does he call after you? Not quite. He spies you 20 yards away, and despite the fact that his pipe is alight, he will turn to the wall with his back to you, and light his pipe again • light it until you have gone by, and then walk off. like a domes- ticated kangaroo. It dawns upon you that he is going to be a man some day, or something brave— probably a volunteer. He reminds you ot the wolf that is brave with the pack, but a kid by itself. Which he is.</p>
        <p>But suppose you happen to be an elderly matron instead of a young lady. Then the remarks are made about your comelinees. One of the youths in a tenor voice will sing " 0-n-only a fa-ding blossom." Another of them in a burlesque tone will pretend to be overcome with emotion and re- mark" Sweet 17 I Look at them nose and those mouth, and they eyes. An't she a daisy." You don't feel these remarks at first, like the younger person ; experience has taught you to have a better grip on your temper. But the eighth group does it. One of its number unfortunately remarks that he is glad you are going to give your feet a chance to spread by wearing your husband's blutcher's. Off goes the steam valve; you are plunging at them with your thnbrella, making wild assertions about having them horsewhipped. A crowd collects and then you go, bursting with passion, which takes a long time to subside and calm down.</p>
        <p>Perhaps you are not a woman at all, but a man. Well, those jovial, witty, and humorous young fellows are just the same, every time. The fun bubbles in everything they say. Suppose nature has equipped you with a pair of lengthy legs. One of the group will say " Wait a bit chappies. There are two of my old girls props walking away." Some one will call " shanks," and another "spindles." Maybe you are sporting a brown hat. Then the penalty is " Where did you get that hat?" " Did it cost eighteen pence or one and ninepence ?" And if you have a silk hat, then look out for some- thing more brilliant. They suggest tJt the hat has been stolen ; that it is the one you wore when you buried your mother, and subsequently adorned you when you married your wife, and stood duty at the christening of your first born. All this is very funny ; and yet if you are an effeminate man you carn't seem to laugh any more than a lock- jawed clam. Neither do you laugh if you are a strong man; but swear—to yourself—and wish that killing people wasn't looked upon with dis- approval.</p>
        <p>However, when a band is in the park, the young people don't enjoy themselves so much in this man- ner. The young fellows step into their tanned leather boots, and don their straw hats and low waistcoats and things. They walk slowly round and round, preserving a stolid, majestic dignity, which somewhat irrelevantly makes you think of frozen mutton. It must be the refrigerated sheepishness about the aspect. They look askance at the girls from the sides of their eyes, and feel highly disappointed if they don't turn back and show wonder and affection for either the young mashers or their hats ; they ambitiously believe that they have a claim prior to that of their hats. The girls are just in the same condition,every time. They bring out their smartest dresses and hats, and are pleased when the grass is damp that they may be compelled to raise the train of their dresses and unintentionally show their pretty ankles. They generally carry small prayer books. They march past the young men,apparently ignorant of the fact that they exist ; then they will turn their heads round and look after them, and perhaps the young men see thew. And then—well, the male gender will come along back, and with a voice full of lob- sidedness and had grammar, he will ask: "Will you 'eve an 'umbug, Miss, I-forget-your- name ?"</p>
        <p>And he knows he didn't forget it, because he never knew it. Then she'll probably say: "I'm so fond of tbis piece of music. What is it ?"</p>
        <p>" S'my fond of it. Believe it's Gooernod's—er— what yer call it—oh " Triumphant March."</p>
        <p>Then more humbugs follow; he tells her about "Gooernods's" other works; she listens and says she knew him well; then he says how hot it is; she tells him about a sunstroke she nearly caught whilst mooning about in the park before, and they conclude, for mutual protection from the sun to go and sit on a sheltered seat in a quiet corner. Which they do. And when the band has stopped discours- ing " Gooernod" you'll find those couples still seated, drinking in the music of each other's voices and eating each other's humbugs. It is just so, every time.</p>
        <p>Sunday evenings are good times too. MN; of the young men go to places of worship then. When I tirst came to Warrington from the country my cousin used to be one of those young men in the Park. And when we went to divine worship in the evening he just froze up my young blood. When the venerable minister had launched upon his ser- mon, my cousin took from his pocket one of those slangy, vulgar, sporting papers. I shot a look Of reproach at him which ought to have knocked him down; but he came up with a smile and said "Ire's belly slow—the old 'un—aint he. Its bully to get something to read. I'll swap you for "Witty cuts" when I'm thro' with this. You aint got one Sin) They all have 'em here." And after the service that cousin of mine took me by the arm and said I was thirsty; which I wasn't, and he lead me up an entry into a room full of smoke. lie called it a snug. There were about 14 other young men in- side—they were all just like the mashers I'd seen pictures of in comic papers; but never alive. I was going to ask Cousin George if they were really liv-</p>
        <p>ing, until I saw one take up a tumbler of stout and go "smack," and then put the empty glass down. Well, I won't say what those, young men talked about; but it often brought crimson blushes to my cheeks. They all had such a winning unaffected way of saying " yas " instead of" yes" and "awe, r.-ally" instead of " ah, really." They were Mostly young clerks at about XI per week, but they talked about putting " fivers "en horses like millionaires, and they said everything was either" demn fine" or "toe bad."</p>
        <p>They talked meetly about 'football and horses, except when the waitress came around, and then they couldn't say anything except" Well, my prettav little creat-cha. Don't forget the smile with the bittah." Most everyone of them said that; it didn't seem to occur to them to say anything else. As the evening galloped along one of them was- called upon to sing., He was the vocalist of the "set" but he never sang until he was "&amp;-hem, Jest a little fresh." He elected to sing "The future Mrs. Hawkins." For the guidance of his friends he called his voice tenor ; but it was a pcor, lame, shadow of a voice, and used to duck under a high note in a sickly kind of way instead of climb- ing it. _He evidently didn't know the coster dia- lect for this is how he murdered Cheveliers style : " Awe, Leza, sweete Leza If yoo dey an old ma-AID Youl ewnly 'eve yoorself to bla—a—m—e."</p>
        <p>But they applauded him as though he had been Sims Beeves. They did what. they called "paying roundrand it seems there was one among the flock whom it was necessary to "calk." My cousin said that when they asked him what he would have he always said" brandy and soda" ed.; but when it came his turn to pay he said" draught stout," two- pence.. By and by it came his turn to pay, and he said" What'll.you have A—?" "Brandy and soda." "Er-what did you say yours was B—? Bitter? ph Brandy and soda." It went all round, "Brandy and soda," and that young man began to look like a pantomime fairy was htd—the colour was emigrat- ing from his face. But be tried to smile, and when the "drinks " were being ordered I saw him count eighteenpence, quietly; and then he rose calmly and said " You'l excuse me a minute chappies—l'll be back in a giffy." He walked slowly out; the "drinks" came in; three minutes, four minutes, five minutes, and ten minutes passed, and that young man wasn't back. The company got ner- vous, and when a quarter of an hour had gone, someone said he'd probably quite forgotton about the thing, and so the company paid for their own "brandy and sodas." But they didn't laugh, as they ought to have dune, after, doing such a smart thing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>TYIE. NEW LOANS.</p>
        <p>The Manchester Ship Canal (Additional Capital) Bill .was before the Select Committee of the House of Lords odMonday. There were also on the list bills promoted by the Corporation of Manchester and by the Corporations of Salford and Oldham. The first of these proposed to lend the Ship Canal Company the whole of the addi- tional capital required, £2,000,000. The -bill of the Salford Corporation proposed to contribute £1,000,000 and that of Oldham £250,000. Mr. Pember, Q.C., pointed out that two years ago the Corporation of Man- chester lent the Ship Canal Cempany £3,000,000; and it was thought that the sum would enable them to com- plete the undertaking and leave a surplus of £300,000; but last year it was found that £1,489,282 was still needed before the canal could be opened for traffic' and the Corporation were asked to lend the Ship Canal Cow- pony another £2,000,000. rhe question was whether the Canal Company should borrow the money from the Manchester Corporation or obtain part of it from the two other municipal bodies. He explained that they had agreed to accept the loan from the Manchester Corpora- tion on the following terms—that the chairman should be elected by the shareholders' directors; that the deputy chairman should be elected by the Corporation directors till the loans were paid off; that while the £3,000,000 loan remained unpaid the directorate should consist of 21 members, 11 to be appointed by the Corporation, but as soon as it was paid off the directorate should be re- duced to 17 members, seven being appointed by the Cor- poration; and that when the whole of the loan, £5,000,000, was paid off, the directorate should number 15, only two being appointed by the Corporation. Mr. Pember tendered, on behalf of the Ship Canal Company, an expression of regret to the Salford and Oldham Cor- porations that he could not accept their loans. The Mayor of Manchester (Mr. Alderman Marshall) was ex- amined, and said the Corporation would have declined to find the money unless they could have obtained favourable and equitable terms, especially considering what they had already done for the Canal. Counsel ap- pearing for Salford and Oldham held that the Man- chester Corporation had been guilty of a breach of faith.</p>
        <p>The Select Committee of the House of Lords on the bills relating to the Manchester Ship Canal met again on Wednesday, Earl Cadogan presiding. At the outset of the proceedings the Chairman invited the opinion of counsel representing the various promoters as to the possibility of some arrangement which would relieve the Committee from having to decide on the claims of the several Corporations to the " honour of lending money to the Company." Mr. Moulton, on behalf of the Corporation of Manchester, said his clients would not seek power to prevent the Company getting money from other friends, but the Corporation insisted that they should have the majority on the Board. Sir John Harwood was called, and he stated that the main object of Manchester had been that under all circumstances until their £3,000,000 was paid off or reduced they must have the control: They had no negotiations with the Corporations of Salford or Oldha:n, and he did not re- gard the agreement they had come to with the Ship Canal Company as any breach of faith with those two Corporations. It was his opinion that the bill of the Manchester Corporation would be withdrawn unless the arrangement come to was sanctioned. Asked by the Chairman" Are we to understand that if these condi- tions, which are now in the bill of the Manchester Cor- poration, are not complied with, the Corporation' of Manchester will not advance any more money ?" The witness answered " That is so."</p>
        <p>The Select Committee of the House of Lords on the Manchester Ship Canal Bills met again on Wednesday. The Chairman (Earl Cadog,an) stated that the Committee , had come to the conclusion that Salford and Oldham, in view of the arrangement between Manchester and the Company, had no locus standi against the preambles of the bills of the Manchester Corporation and the Ship Canal ampany, and that the Committee found the preambles of those bills proved. Replying to some observations of counsel, his Lordship said that in the opinion of the Committee Manchester stood in a different position from other towns, in face of the loan of three millions and the agreement made with the Canal Com- pany. After the decision of the Committee the repre- sentatives of Salford and Oldham decided that the bills promoted by the Corporations of those boroughs should not be proceeded with.</p>
        <p>The Select Committee of the House of Lords consider- ing the bills relLting to the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany met again on Thursday. Lord Cadogan presided. Mr. Bidder, Q.C., representing Salford, said that Corpor- ation felt a little hurt that the arrangement had been made between the Ship Canal Company and the Manchester Corporation before they had presented their own case. It would, he contended, be impossible for Manchester, when the question arose of converting the canal undertaking into a public trust, to found their claim for sole control on the ground that they had lent the five millions. On behalf of Salford he then withdrew the bill. Mr.Littler,Q.C.,took a similar course with regard to Oldham. The clause which had been in dispute, as finally settled, providAs that when half the total loan of five millions has been paid off the representation of Manchester shall be reduced from eleven to seven, the number ot shareholders' directors to remain unchanged. Meanwhile there will be 21 directors, 11 representing the Corporation and 10 the shareholders. No portion of the loan can be repaid before January 1, 1897, with out the consent of the Corporation. The bills were ordered for third reading.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC NOTICE.—Not one but lIUNDRIMS of people of both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constitutions are yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson. 3 and 4, Fitcallan Square, Sheffield. Write to thorn for advice free when Buffering from weakness, loss of flesh, appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches, indiges- tion, pallid skin, nervousness, and general debilty. Posi- tive cure„for all Blood and Skin Diseases, acquiriii or consti- tutional. Sixty years' experiencc. One address (as above) for over 30 years. The " A 1 Mixture," 4s. 9d. the case. The Magic Mirror in envelope, bar stamps. Sufferers &amp;sap- pointed with Blsctilc Belts should write to us at once.—Advt</p>
        <p>An Ir:shman named Mich ael Splann was on Wednes- day fined £lOO and costs by the Cardiff magistrates, in default three months' imprisonment, for illegally selling beer in his house on Sunday, the sth inst.</p>
        <p>Sentence was passed at Melbourne, on Wednesday, on the directors and officials of the Anglo-Australian Bank, who had been found guilty of issuing a false report and balance sheet. Mr. C. R. Staples was sentenced to five years' penal servitude, Mr. J. E. Norwood to two years, and Mr. J. flaraldson, accountant, to six months' im- prisonment. VALUABLE DISCOVERY NOR THE HAIR.—It your hair IS turning grey or white, or. falling. off, use "Tug MEXICA,I HAIR low" for it ?oil./ poritrvely restore in every ease Grey or White h original to its oginal colour, Without leaving the disagreeal.le smell of most "Restorers," It makes the hair eiarmingly beautiful, as well as promoting the growth of the on bald snots, where the glands are not decayed. Price 3s. 6d.—For an Oil to mate the Hair soft, glossy, arid ruxu- riant., ask for •• CaRTEuS COLOONE OIL," Price le. of ale dealers. Wholesale depot, 13.3, learringdon-road, London,—</p>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENr.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>At Saturday's sitting of the House of Commons, Mr. Ml:minima informed Mr. Chamberlain that in connection with the new labour bureau three new</p>
        <p>LABOUR CORRESPONDENTS had been appointed, one of them being a lady ; and that before the appointment of these officers he made careful inquiries into their fitness for the discharge of the duties entrusted to them, but the inquiries did not extend to their political opinions, though he had heard it stated that one of them is a life-long Conservative.—The CHAN- maxima of the EXCHEQUER, In auswer to Sir Richard Temple, said be was in communication with the Trea- sury with the object of making financial provision for the superannuation of teachers in public elementary schools as recommended by the committee. MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons' on Monday, Mr. MIIND_ informed Colonel HowArd Vincent that the paym made to the Labour correspondents of the Board _ _ . _ .</p>
        <p>Trade would be a small annual one. The appointmen bad been wholly uninfluenced by political considerations. Sir W. HARCOURT intimated that, in consequqnce of the delay in the progress of Supply, the debate on the Home Rule Bill would not be taken before Easter, but would be the first business after the Easter vacation.</p>
        <p>iELLIM lent of LtS•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>At the morning sitting of,the House of Ccmmons • on Tuesday a conversation took place on allegations made that some Irish landlords are using pressure to in- duce their tenants to sign petitions against the Home Rule Bill, and a debate on the subject was hinted at.— Colonel VINCENT moved the reduction of the Stationary Office vote by £l,OOO as a protest against the use of foreign-made paper in the Government offices, but at the suggestion of Mr. J. CiwangerAlN he withdrew the motion. On the vote for law charges Mr. BANBURY&amp; drew attention to the remuneration of the law officers of the Crown, and the matter was discussed till progress was reported. The House was couutectout shortly after the evening sitting had begun, while Mr. BROOKFIELD. was drawing attention to the recommendations of the Select Committee on the Hop Industry..</p>
        <p>In the House cf Lords the Earl of KIMBERLET, reply- ing to,Lord Nowrox, said that the new Education Code was laid on the table on the 27th ult, and he would see whether its circulation would be expedited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Cotnmons on Wednesday Mr., E. R. JONES moved the second reading of• the Welsh Local Veto Bill,which empowers two-thirds of the ratepayers in a given area to prohibit the sale of intoxicating liquors in their, district, and a bare majority to reduce the number of licensed houses Or to say that ncinew licenses be granted. Mr.Enwenns seconded the motion. Sir W. IVLlantorr moved the rejection of the bill on the ground that it contained no provision for compensation. Mr.J H.JoarisTonn seconded the amendment. Mr.Lt.oxn, Crzonoz,Mr.J.H.Roberts,Sir G.O.Morgan,Mr. W.Ctosfield , and Sir W.Harcourt spoke in support of the bill,and Baron F. de Rothschild,Lord Carmarthen,Mr.Heneage,Mr. Mat- thews, and Mr. Long against it. On a division the amendment was defeated by 281 to 246, and the second reading was agreed to. Before the House rose the Rail- way Servants (Hours of Labour) Bill was withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons, on Thursday, the Manches- ter Ship Canal Bill was read a third time ivithout dis- cussion. The President of the Board of Agriculture'in- formed Mr. Long that it was intended to hold a system- atic examination of Canadian cattle landed at the slaughter port, in order to judge if the slaughter order could safely be revoked.Mr.Asquith,replying to Mr.Bous- field, said the only power to prevent unqualified persons practising as medical men, apothecaries,. OT assistants was vested in the Society of Apothecaries, and that only so far as apothecaries were concerned. He was not pre- pared to propose legislation on the. subject. Mr. Campbell-Bannerman informed Baron IL. de Worms that the naval and military authonties were now of the same opinion as they had previously expressed regard- ing the Channel Tunnel, namely,.against it.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords, ,a bill introduced by the Bishop of Salisbury to provide for greater freedom of instruction in Board Schools was read &amp;first time. A bill to consoli- date thelaw relating to copyhold tenure was laid upon the table by the Lord Chancellor, and read a first time. The Archbishop of Canterbury moved the second read- ing of the Church Patronage Bill, the intention of which he said was not to make a heroic resolution in the matter of patronage, but only tio remedy abuses against which there was a general and a just outcry. 'Me bill was read a second time. Lord CossLow moved the second reading of a bill for the marking of imported meat, but on the promise of Lord Ribblesdale that a Select Committee of their lordships' House should be appointed to look into the whole question, including the marking of cheese and fruit, he withdrew his motion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>GOVERNMENT PREPARATIONS FOR THE ULSTER RISING.</head>
        <p>GOVERNMENT PREPARATIONS FOR THE ULSTER RISING.</p>
        <p>On Thursday it was announced that Colonel Dawson, commanding the Belfast Regimental District, Colonel Parker, Royal Engineers, Major Cameron, Royal Engineers, Major Irving, Army Pay Department, and Major Menzies 3rd Battalion Royal Irish Rifles, all stationed at Belfast, have been appointed to the tempor- ary commission of the peace by the Lord-Lieutenant. Other appointments are impending.</p>
      </div>
