        <head>lettrro to tin Cbitor.</head>
        <p>lettrro to tin Cbitor.</p>
        <p>BUDDHISM AND CHRISTIANITY.</p>
        <p>- - - Sir,—Driven from the main point of contentioa in this discussion," Harry Esmond" now finds it convenient to disown any connexion with the more open and straightforward utterances of his friend Eclectic.", He seems to be quite unconscious that his own quotatien from Clodd, involved him very suspiciously at least in the insinuation that there had been some " borrowing I of the younger religion from the older," in fact the whole tone of his first letter had that appearance and nothing else, and it is difficult to comprehend what all the dis- cussion has been about, or what he can possibly mean when he tells us in this last communication of his that " either the statements advanced by me are correct or they are not—that if not correct they ought to be easily disproved—and they have not been so far—but if cor- rect why not honestly and candidly admit the fact." The mystification of this passage, and its succeeding apostrophe is only metalled by its effrontery. He shall not, however, so easily escape from the responsibility of his first utterances. Ha cannot get away from his own words that " the most remarkable points of similarity between SU ddhism and Christianity are those relating to the birth and life of Chrishna, a Buddhist god (sic) and Christ the founder of Christianity," and after detailing various particulars, further says, that "they agree almost word for word with the account given by St. Matthew of Christ." Now this is mu, of the statements I presume which " Harry Esmond " tells us "if not correct ought to be easily disproved, but has not been so far," etc. The worthlessness of such a statement I think will have appeared sufficiently, on two grounds : 1. That when the incidents are compared with the earliest legends there is nothing but the most shadowy and insignificant points of resemblance existing, and that any " word for word agreement with St. Matthew's gospel" is absolutely false. 2. I quoted the authority of the most eminent Sanscrit scholars, such as Sir Monier Williams and Davies (and many others might be quoted), who affirm that the Hindu Scriptures containing those very legends of Chrishna the most like to anything in the life of Christ date no earlier than the sth or 6th century of our era. Now, for " Harry Esmond" to pretend in his vague style, that in his reading of books dealing with the Hindu Scriptures, " He has never read in one of them that their date is anything so recent," not only exhibits his very superficial acquaintance with the sub- ject he writes about, but in the face of authorities whom I have quoted savours of a sell sufficiency ona could hardly have iillagilll2ll. I speak especially of his vague style, for he writes simply anconscious that the Hindu Scriptures date from a thousand years before Christ to more than a thousand years after, and we are left to surmise as best we may, whether his readings refer to the Chrishna legends or to something else of a far earlier age. Such then is the upshop of these his " most remark- able points of similarity between Buddhism an 1 Chris- tianity," and this the net result of statements which he says " have not been disproved BO far," but which are showia to be utterly worthless in their character, and at the best are nothing more than a bald anachronism. But "Harry Esmond "is determined to die hard. Whilst lie dares not openly assert that our gospels are copies of Hindu legends, any straw which, like a drowning man, he can grasp at, does not esc ipe him. An unfortunate reference was made to the Bhagavad- Oita in the first letters of this correspondence. This poem according to the latest opinion, is not earlier than the 2nd or 3rd, and most probably the 4th century after Christ. The mention of the 2nd century seemed to afford "Harry Esmond" a last, expiring chance for a stroke. I must be aware, he says, " that the date of our Gospels is a very debateable subject, and that a great many of the finest scholars of our day fix them for the end of the 2nd century," to which I reply that I am aware of no such absurd opinion ; but on the contrary, that the consensus of all scholars of any reputation fixes -the date of St. Matthew's and St. Luke's Gospel (the only gospels which have aught to do with this subject between the middle and third quarter of the Ist century, a fact admitted by so unfavourable a witness for the truth as Ernest Ronan. But I have called the reference to this famous poem the Bhagavad-Gita, an unfortunate one, for having lately read a translation of it, I find that from beginning to end there is not a use in it bearing upon any incidents at all in the life of Christina which compares with aught in our Gospel narratives. The poem is a theistic, moral, and philosophical one only, with here and there a remarkably elevated and Christian sentiment, parallel in thought and expression even to New Testament passages—so much so, that prominent scholars,both here and in America,and on the continent, have eoncluded that it is pervaded largely with an in- fluence derived from Christian sources, and for this the date of the poem afforded ample opportunity. I do not hesitate,therefore, to dismiss in the "lightest and airiest"manner possible anything that such writers as Peebles can have to say upon this subject, as set against the latest testimony of our best Sanscrit scnolars. And let messy that the further " Harry Esmond " extends his researches into the history of " the lovely shepherd god" the more eertainly will he find that my statements -" fit in " too correct-is with what is known ef the licen- -1,1T13 DrAreate an his sharislesA MMUS with his bgi.l- tul Let me apprise him of a late histo- rical incident. When Jhansi was captured in the times of the great Indian mutiny, English officers were dis- gusted to see the walls of the Queen's palace covered with what they described as grossly obscene pictures, representations of the acts of Christina, which to the Hindu queen were simply regarded as religious pictures, and what is saddening to think of, such features enter into the worship of millions of our fellow-subjects at this hour in India, east are defended by the Brah-</p>
        <p>mans. A closing remark upon what " Harry Esmond" calls ny "garbled account" of Max Muller's review of M. Jacolliot's book. Jam simply amused at the two points which strike me in that paragraph : the denseness of the apprehensien which could not discover the quiet satire which underlies the whole criticism of the book ; and in the next place, the cool assumption that Max Muller's judgment upon a purely professional question of Sansciit scholarship should not commend itself as of sufficient weight to the judgment of " Harry Esmond." I need merely add that the only meaning conveyed by Max Muller's decision, upon the merits of Jacolliot's worthless production is—thot it is a mere repetition of the trumpery Brahmanic frauds which deceived Colonel Wilford and Sir Wm. Jones.—Yours, &amp;c., JOHN DIGNUM. Pa—Another paper upon Christian Missions will be all that is needed in this correspondence—so far as I am concerned. Penketh, 18th March, 1893.</p>
        <p>-- BUDDHISM AND CHRISTIANITY.—SOSIE POINTS OF RESEMBLANCE. Sir.—Mr. Dignum is still intent on fighting a bogey— and a bogey of his own creation. He still persists in attributing to me statements which I have never made, and he further makes statements of his own, which are unsupported by any authorities. As I stated in my last letter I never said that the Christian Scripture* were eopied from the Hindu or Buddhist ones. but Mr. Dig_ num makes the statement that the Hindu or Buddhist books were cr,pied from the Christian ones. But what does Max Muller say ? In lecture IV. in his Introduc- tion to the science of Religion he says :—" In no religion are we so constantly reminded of our own religion as in Buddhism. Between the language of Buddha and his disciples, and of Christ and his Apostles, there are some strange coincidences. Even some of the Buddhist legends and parables sound as if taken from the New Testament, though we know that many of them existed befere the beginning of the " Christian Era." This, be it observed, is from Mr. Dignum's own authority. In a previous letter of mine I referred to an address at Rochdale by Archdeacon Wilson, in which he said that the morality of the Hindu scriptures was on a very lofty level, and that even in practice it was in many respects universally beyond anything that obtained in Christian countries. Since then, the Rev. Archdeacon has given another address on Buddhism in which he says that" the story of Buddha's life is curiously parallel, in some particulars, with that of our Lord." And so with Buddhist teachings and its rites and ceremoni es, and monastic and other institutions. I think it is hardly worth while bandying words with Mr. Dignum. I will leave it to your readers whether he is climbing down or not, when, after saying that "The string of ordinary insignificant parallels cited by your correspondents (I refer here to facts or incidents) should merely excite one's derision or contempt. The fleetire, features of most of the coincidents noted vanish the moment you come to trace and compare them " he, in another letter says" I am most heartily ready to admit that there are wonderful points of comparison between the, moral teachings and conduct of Buddha and the character and teachings of Christ." If this is not eating his own words I don't know what is to say nothing of climbing down. Mr. Dignum says that "Buddhism is essentially Atheistic, and is no religion at all." Well, it is a most remarkable fact that Buddhism does ignore the, existence of God. Mine, it iimores it, hut does not deny it. But Mr. Dignum says it i He says it religion naotr easlia.UL:etpuesn dependence cwehoant Max Muller says. a ligher power and in this respect is the oppodta pole se. Christianity. But what is religion? The late Rev. M. Robertsen said religion is goodness—and that Chris- tianity is love of God and love of our fellow-men. All *lse, he said, is only husk and shell. Now Buodhism is religion, but it is not Christianity, although in many respects singularly like it. Shall we turn to Max Muller again? In his science of religion he. says, " There is no religion—or if there is. Ido not know it—which does not say, Do good, avoid evil. There is none which does not contain what Rabbi Hillel called the quintess- ence of all religions, the simple warning, " Be good, my lacy." "Be good, my boy may seem a very short</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>3'"</p>
        <p>catechism, but let us add to it, " Be good, my boy, for God's sake," and we have in it very nearly the whole of the law and the prophets. After giving a number of Most beautiful extracts from Buddhist and other sacred books Max Muller goes on to say, "1 wish we could ex- plore together in this spirit the ancient religions of man- kind, for I feel convinced that the more we know of them, the more we shall See that there is not one that is entirely false ; nay,that in one sense every religion was a true religion, being the only religion that was possible et the time, which was eompatible with the language, the thoughts, and the sentiments of each generation, which was appropriate to the age of the world. I know the objections that will be made to this. Was the wor- ship of Moloch, it will be said, a true religion when they burnt their eons and their daughters in the fire to their Gods? Was the worship of Mylitta, or is the worship of Kaq a true religion, when within the sanctuaries of their temples they committed abominations that must be nameless? Wail the teaching of Buddha a true reli- gion, when men were asked to believe that the highest reward of virtue and meditation consisted in a complete annihilation of the soul ?" "Such arguments may tell in party warfare, though even there they have provoked fearful retaliation. Can that be a true religion, it has been asked, which consigned men of holy innocence to the flames, because they held that the Son was like unto the Father, but not the same as the Father, or because they ceuld not worship the Virgin and the saints? Can that be a true religion which screened the same nameless crimes behind the sacred walls of monas- teries? Can that be a true religion which taught the eternity of punishment without any hope of pardon or salvation for the sinner not penitent in proper time ?" Mr. Dienum's own religien has been guilty of all these crimes. 'Scarcely any horror or atrocity perpetrated in the name of religion amongst barbarous tribes but can be snatched by deeds of equal horror and atrocity done in the name of Christianity. Christianity has, again and again, deluged the world with blood, tossed helpless babes on the points of pikes and bayonets,and laid waste the fairest provinces of God's earth. For trifling matters of creed and dogma men have been imprisoned, and cruelly tortured, and burned at the stake. Let Mr. Dignum deny this if he can. And yet, I believe, he will say that Christianity is a religion. Yes, and a nation that prides itself upon being a Christian nation wages petty war upon weak and defenceless tribes all over the i world. She sends these tribes missionaries to confer ! upon them the blessings of Christianity ; and she also ; sends them rum and whiskey, which demoralize and decimate them snore than all her Getting and Maxim guns. At the muzzles of her guns and the points of her bayonets, she forces the infarnme opium drug upon the weak millions of China—deaf alike to their indiguarit protests and their abject entreaties. At home, in the sacred streets of her pious cities vice stalks with unblushing front in the broad light of open I day. Drunkenness, licentiousness, brutality,aboundo on every hand. Within the shadows of her cnurch spires, and within sound of their bells, every abomination known to humanity can easily be found by any one who will take the trouble to look. In her greatest city—the richest city in the world—a city abounding with churchee—anon Farrar says, that her harlots exceed in number the entire population of the city of Exeter— almost double the entire population of Warrington. It would be possible to fill a volume in this strain—every word of which should be absolutely true. Of course I know that this is only one side of the shield, and that a good deal of quite another character can be said on the other side. Still, it is self-evident to every ca:ndid mind that there are many foul and fatal ' blots on mcdern Christianity. And yet in a large sense lam myself a Christian. I am a Deist —a believer in God—though lam utterly unable to demonstrate. His existence. But I am no orthodox christian, and I believe it is rank blespneiny on the part of any one to suppose " that all the nations of the earth, before the rise of Christianity, were mere outcasts, forsaken and forgotten of their father in Heaven, without a knowledge of God, without a hope of salvation." Well might Max Muller call such a supposition a Godless heresy. I had intended to use the testimony of Gerald Massey to show not only the indebtedness of Christianity to pre- existing religions, but also to show that almost every saying recorded of Christ, every moral truth inculcated in the Sermon on the Mount had been previously re- corded of other teachers, and inculcated by ether religions. But I think I have said enough on this point. Mr. Di„enum says that Buddhism is meiely a moral sys- tem, blending with a wretched, false philosophy. With your permission sir, I will deal next week with both Buddhism and Christianity from a scientific and philo- sophical aspect. From the first I have had no intention of saying anything to the detriment of Christianity, and it is only owing to the attitude of Mr. Dignum that I have now been constrained to do so. But I shall be perfectly fair in all I say, and will give almost exclu- sively as my authorities eminent ministers ot the churches. .</p>
        <p>HARRY ESMOND.</p>
        <p>THE ANNUAL ACCOUNTS OF PUBLIC - CHARITIES.</p>
        <p>Sir.—About three years ago you were god enough to I insert a letter from me,drawing attention to toe .53 stenta- tic neglect of the law by the Trustees of i.ablic charities, or those having their control or management,in not sub- mitting, a statement of the annual income and expendi- ture for the information of the public at the annual vestry meetings, and seeing that such statement was not Ontered in the vestry minute book.</p>
        <p>As the annual vestry meetings will be held within the course of the next two or three weeks, I venture again to draw the attention of the public to this very import- ant question. /- Since the date of my last letter a supplementary digest of the accounts of charities for the county of Cheshire has been printed and published by Parliament. This digest or return only contains the accounts of those charities of which no returns had been previously made to the Charity Commissioners, or which returns had been previously imperfect. The digest, however, in- creases the cash total of the earlier one by nearly fifty per cent., the former one showing an annual cash total of a little over £15,000, the latter one an addition of over £7,000. These additional charities number 288 ; SO of which consist of the possession of land or buildings which are otherwise unproductive of income ; 47 give seine particulars of income and expenditure,the amounts of which in the former return were eithernot stated or im- perfect; and adding the totals of the two returns together we find that there are now within the knowledge of the Charity Commissioners over eleven hundred charities , possessing an annual cash income of over 122,000,exc1u-1 sive of a considerable prorrtiou stated as cnoistiag of land and buildings but yielding no cash income. The report of Lord Brougliam's Coin mission contained the names of •many charities which were reported as lost or untraceable, and as some result of the wide at- tention given to this subject, two very useful Acts of Parliament have been passed within the last two years, via. :—The Charitable Trusts Recovery Act, 1891, and another enabling County Councils to institute local in- quiries in their respective counties. It is to be hoped that neither of these Acts will be allowed to remain a dead letter. I have before me a copy of the circular officially issued by the Charity Commissisioners from their office at Whitehall and in the hope that its publication may be again useful in quickening the interests of the public at this opportune moment of time, I quote it at length. "The Charitable Trusts Acts require that the trustees or persons acting in the administration of every charity shall, in books to be kept by them for that purpose, regularly enter or cause to be entered full and true accounts of all money received and paid respectively on account of such charity, and shall also, on or before the 25th day of March in every year, or such other day as may be fixed by the Board of Charity Commissioners, prepare and make out the following accounts in relation thereto (that is to say) : 1. An account of the gross income arising from the endowment, or which ought to have arisen therefrom during the year ending on the 31st day of December then last, or on such other day as may have been appointed for this purpose by the Board. 2. An account of all balances in hand at the com- mencement of the rear, and of all moneys received dur- ing the same year on account of the charity. 3. An account of the same period of all payments. 4. An account of all moneys owing to or from the</p>
        <p>charity. 80 tar as conveniently may be, such accounts shall be certified under the hand of one or more of the trustees or administrators, and shall be audited by the auditor .sf the charity, if any ; and that the said trustees or admin- istrators shall, within fourteen days after the day appointed for making out such acconnts, deliver or transmit a copy thereof to the Commissioners at their office in London, and, in the case of parochial charities, shall deliver another copy thereof to the churchwarden or churchwardens of the parish or parishes with which the objects of such charities are identified, who shall present the same at the nextt, general meeting of the vestry of such parishes, and insert a copy thereof in the minutes of the vestry book ; and that every such copy shall be open to the inspection of all -persons at all seasonable hours, subject to such regulations as to the said Board may seem sit; and that any person may re- quire a copy of every such account,or of any part thereof on paying therefor after the rate ot twopence for every seventy-two words or figures."—Faithfully yours, W. HANDLEY. •</p>
        <p>Runcorn, March 16th, 1893. ESMOND AND DIGNUM : SOME ODIOUS COMPARIS NS. "How happy could I be with either, Were Vother dear charmer away !" am one of those unstable individuals whose mental attitude is one of poise. I rejoice in the posses- sion of a balanced mind, and am sufficiently impartial to lean always to the argument last given—swayed alter- nately by opposing opinion. Hence I generally coincide in opinion with the man who has had the last word, and, between-whi'es—in the intervals—l find myself holding</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>EXAMINER, S WITTMAN. MARCH 18. 1893. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, S WITTMAN. MARCH 18. 1893. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>the most diametrically opposed opinions ; for example— with Mr. Dignurn that Christianity owes nothing to earlier religions, and with Mr. Esmond that it has borrowed some of its essential teatimes from Buddhism. This being the case, just imagine' my state of mental mixture whea I find both these doughty champions hammering away, side by side, on the same page, and I have to swallow the bane and the•autidote together! How can I applaud the one in presence of the other— Well done, Dignum ! Bravo, Esmond ! all in a breath ? No ! the unction of bible-worship and the water of critical inquiry are incompatibles, ancf will not mix, and, with, me, they are unassimilable—altogether indi- gestible. This may be the responsible cause of the uncontrollable feeling I now experience to turn upon my instructors and rend them. lam impelled to ask— what is all this pother about ? Though not essential in this discussion,,l should be delighted to find both dis- putants deffating Christianity. We should then know better what they were quarrelling about—at present it is open to question if they do so themselves. If Mr. Esmond's first letter was "not an attack upon Christian- ity" what on earth was it ? Where would revealed Christianity be if his contentions were established and ' its main feature a mere copy from some earlier and, to the Christian, earthlier, religion ? Mr. Esmond's Chris- ' tianity might not be affected, Mr. Dignum's would be annihilated. Some peAple hold it to be " bad form " to , show your neighbour the unreasonableness of his devo- • tion to his particular form of Mumbo-Jumbo—let him bow down to it as he likes—to long as this affects only ' himself. But if he tries to induce you to become a devotee and kew-tow to his wooden-headed deity, then I indeed he loses claim to this immunity. Rut if these comparisons are to go on where shall we end ?' I expect i to see somemie writing to you on " Paganism and Christianity—some points of resemblance "—and instancing the fable of Jupiter's descent from heaven in I form of a bull to enchant Europa. Both the Rhadaman- I thean and the Christian stories are alike in, this, they I both contain bulls, upon whose horns their believers are impaled. We often see the question put " What shall I do to be saved ?" 're this I always reply, hibernically, " From what ?" From what consequences am Ibe saved and how did they arise? To myself, and doubtless to many others, it would be deeply interesting to have an opinion ' upon so important a" point" from either, or both, your learned contributors.—l am yours, &amp;c., 'SOS.</p>
        <p>THE WELSA SUSPENSORY BILL. A PROTEST MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>public meeting to protest against the Welsh Saspensory Bill, was held in thwßankey-st., Co- operative Hall, on Thursday evening. There was not a large attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. Wynne-Jones, and there werwpresent on the platform Mr. Byron Reed, the R:vr.- F. W. Willis, Canon P,enrhyn, Bev. J. J. bangliatm Mr. John White, Mr. C. S. Burgess, and MV t 11, Woods. Amongst the audience were the Mayor (TAlderman B. Greenall) and the Mayoress,CchincilloeSmethurst and ether prominent churchmen., Letteroof apology were read from the Rev. T. Whitley,.Duresbury, Rev. 14orley Stevenson, Rev. E. S. Jackson. Rev. B. B. Proacott, Captain Withington, and Mr. Wm. Job non.</p>
        <p>The'Calainstart said they were not mit there that evening as party politicians, but as Chureh politicians. They were assembled there as Churchmen and Church- womer4.and he thought there were those present who were supporters of the Church outside its coinesonion ; and their object in so assembling was to record their protest against that infamous bill knowdas the Welsh Suspensery Bill. (Applause.) When last- hertiad the honour of presiding over a Church meeting in Warring- ton there-was only a vague rumour that the Catrch of England, would be attacked at some period not far removed: if it was necessary at that time to, show concern at the actions of the Liberationints;, bow much more necessary was it now thot the Suspensory Bill had passed its first reading is the House+ of Commons, after being introduced by the present ministry. If they permitted that measumta be gassed and the four Welsh Dioceses of the Church of England to, lie swept away, then they would nest have to look out for the safety of the Church in England. The Welsh Suspensory Bill was simply a malignant and cruel, and'dispicable attempt to injure the Church.„ to mutilate her before making an attempt at her execution. It was weakening her and reducing her vitality. It was , as cruel to the Church--the taking away of those four dioceses—as would be the cutting off of a man's &amp;Az, fingers before they killed him. They might feel assured that when the Liberationists had done their workt• of pillage in the churches of • Wales they would turn to the churches in, England. Ile believed thas the Bill was merely iatended_for party purposes, and merely to serve. . . .</p>
        <p>certath Noncomforniiste .wrho'allow.ed politimito go be- fore their religion. (Applause.) It was an attempt upon the part of the Liberationists to plunder and des- troy and despoil the church, and what they in- tended ultimately to do with the plunder, they had only to read Mr. Gee's little scheme to see. He• believed that the Nonconformists were jealous of the growing, energy of the Cisurch of Wales. (Hear, hear.) Be could safely say that at no time. had the Welsh . . _</p>
        <p>Church been ktiOyon'to be respQcted and beloved by the Welsh peopke as it was at the present moment. (Ap- plause.) When the Church in Wales was disorganized, and when its places of worship were well nigh empty, and when its. clergy were either absentees or lazy, there was no attartk.made upon it ;..there was no attempt to . .</p>
        <p>'introduce a Eili for its-disestablishinent. And what was the cause ?' Simply that the Nonconformists knew fell well that the Church's weakness was their strength., But now that the clergy were active and the Church strong, the Nonconformists, who could not cope with it spiritually,, tried to bring about its downfall. (" Shamer"). The speaker then went on to state that there . was no doubt the Church-supporters were in a majority in Wales. As an instance of this, he said that recently 'a petition was signed by 4,501 adults in Carnarvon out of a population of 8,000. A similar petition wail signed in Anglimeas. and it was a well-knows fact that those who signed were of all denominations—Churchinso, Wesleyans, Calvinists, Baptists, &amp;c. Be concluded by, stating that the Bill was a political dodge, that Mr Glad- stone might secure the balance of power in Wales for his Home Rule Bill ; it was a cowardly measure, and if passed would be confiscation and pillage. Mr. C.. 3. BURGESS moved a resolution that the " meet- ing eraphadvally and strongly" opposed the Welsh Sus- pensory Rill now before Parliament. In doing so he said the Chairman had said that meeting was not one of politician', and he really felt that he was in a prediaa- meat sate what his politics were He was an earnest Liberal, who liked to keep up with the times, and he would support any measure, no matter which party brought it in, if it seemed a just measure and one for the benefit of the masses of the people. (Applause.) But beyond that he was a Churchman and would support that church even though he had to sink his politics. No consideration of party would induce him to vote for any measure which would be to the detriment or the injury of the Church. (Applause.) He condemned the Bill in every sense of the word, and he con- sidered it a base and cowardly attack maim the mother Church ; it was an attempt te weaken her, an attempt to take away four af her limbs, for the four dioceses of Wales had no separate existence from the .7.lg- lish Church. The Liberationists were striking Waugh Mr. Gladstone ; they were striking at their establish- ment, trying to paralyse the life of their Church,and Mr. Gladstone knew it as well as they did. It was an iniquiS tious Bill—aad when he saw the meanness of its cowar- dice, the "squaring" of 28 Welsh votes, then it made his blood boil. He concluded by expressing the hopes that every loyal Churchman would do his best to 'defeat the Bill. The Rev. F. W. WILLIS condemned Mr. Gladstone' attempt to pass a Bill which he described as both mater- ially and spiritually unjust to the Welsh Churchmen. Be said that Mr. Gladstone had expressed the opinion that the Welsh Church wail iaseparable from the Eriglish,and how he desired to divide them. During the last half century £2,218,723 had been spent by the Welsh Church- men in buildings, and they had obtained four Communi- cants against three in England, according to the popu- lations. That was a plain expression of popular feel- ing. He seconded the resolution proposed by Mr. Burgess. Mr. Brutes RIND said that be would ,liko to adopt the most excellent tone and temper of the mover and seconder of the proposition + The cause of their church was not the cause merely of the clergy, but forrthe laity as well. Ha would proceed to answer the question of the mover of the proposition as to what the welsh Sus- pensory Bill was. Unfortunately the Bill did not in „it- salf convey the reply. It said that persons appointed atter the passing of this Bill would only hold their appointments during the pleasure of Parliament. He (the speaker), had taken some pains to ascertain what was meant by the pleasure of Parliament, but he had not been able to get any very strong legal definition ; but it was generally held that it meant this, that any bishop, dean, canon, or parson appointed to any see or parochial benefice in Wales after.the passing of this measure woald not be entitled to compensation noon the disendowment of the church. There is another interpretation to the effect that any person appoieted after the passing of this measure would become the parliamentary trustee, and if disendowment were defer red for five years. the unfortunate parson would hay* to give back to national purposes the whole five years salary. He wanted to know what clergyman would undertake the responsibility of having office, when hanging over his head was the probability of being called upon, at an early date, to pay back every shilling he received from the preferment. The result of this bill would be, that when a preferment became vacant, it would not be filled and there would be no clergy to minister to the people. He had never known a more wicked, a more criminally wicked bill to b. conceived than this present one. The speaker went on to protest against the theory that a moral question should be carried by counting heads, as a question was good or bad notwithstanding the nume ben s for and against it. But he also denied that it was , proved that the church was in a minority in Wales, and said that the Welsh members had defeated the proposal to have a religious census during the last census, when it , could have been proved. He also warned the Noneon-- I formists that if they supported the disestablishment of the church, their turn would come next. Speaking. of. the Places of Worahip Ilofranchisement Bill, he said that it was a bill to establish chapels which was rather- a strange thing to do by the party who wanted to dis- establish the church. Mr. Reed concluded his speech,by urging his hearers to do their utmost to defeat the bill, The proposition was passed unanimously. The Rev. E.G.E.Canterox in moving a vote of thanks to Mr. Reed said that he did not think the ohurch had much need to fear as it had passed through troubles successfully before. Mr. J. WHITS seconded, and wished that there had been more present, but the church party in Warrington was very apathetic. He hoped that 'they would rouse themselves. Mr. REED briefly replied, and Mr. Wood announced that a petition was drawn up to be signed in that room, and would be sent to various churches of the town. He hoped to have at least 1,000 signatures. A. rote of thanks to the chairman closed the meeting.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Foo7DALL.</head>
        <p>Foo7DALL.</p>
        <p>Da aloes seem funny; it appears to be humourous, some- how. It is humouroos, and 012 f football team must be all hwmorists. Here we have•them on Saturday proving that they can play, by beating: St. Helens Recs. and then on 3/mnlay they go and swap, round, and lie down while Whines lick them. They must do it because it is funny, I'm seta.</p>
        <p>St.. Helens Recs., whom they defeatedi by two goals and a try, have until last week held the third seat in the Lancashire Cup competition coach,. whilst on the other liand,Widnes have been hiding their talent during almost the whole of the season under the bushel—or a bushel— of quite minor teams. Widnes,. undoubtedly played a game superior to that of our team. Some of our for- wards clidn't play anything like up to the mark- Some- times, 04 course, it does not do to..ost the Widnes public in a bao temper, so I conclude that Warrington were putting them in a good humour by pretend!rag they couldn't'play much.</p>
        <p>But let us return to Saturday and find, ourselves beaming :n the beautiful rays whieMbenevolent. old Sol shied at us for a change. In every respect it seemed to be the match of the season. Nothing was paltry about it ; nothing seemed to strike you .as shabby (e3cept it was the manner the fellow next door drank: the pop without even looking at you). Both teams appeared to have new rig-outs; the gate was the largest this.season ; and altogether the match was carried out witn every success. The great majority of thoseinclinedtto. bet, placed their " spondulieks " on St. Helens Rem,. and then they waited; waited with apprehensions which grew to doubts ; waited with deabts which grew to fears ; waited with fears till they saw Warrington. win; and then didn't wait any more, noteven in some cases for the man they had made the bet, with: Ot:r crest- fallen P.U4 spirits grew so high that we had to lasso him from, the top of a telegraph post where he waa sing- ing " Oh !'What a surprise ;" and other saered.aim</p>
        <p>At any rate the result did come-as a surpriseand it is questionable whether there was ever much quicker scoring than the notching of two goals in the first-seven minutes. It was truly said that the game was won in the first quarter cf an hour. Warrington men all reund,appeared to knead their various qualities together (anti they needed to) until they were found playing with a, unison, a compactness, and vigour, tempered with judicious dis- cretion, which probably they have never displayed be- fore this,seeson. In the first half of the game they gave the visitors no quarter whatever ; they did not remain contented 4 with defensive tactics then, but kept up a spirited attack.</p>
        <p>In the secQnd half they fell off slightly,and the wonder is that the Recs., aided by a strong breeze, did,not get a, single point. Their defeat was a more complete one, than dui', hive met with before during the wholeof their' quest for,the grail—l mean the cup. It was a defeat which will be noticed and wondered at, all over Lanca- shire.</p>
        <p>The home forwards particularly were to be•congratu.- fated. Boscow played rather an average game bun Evans, , I-leesom, and James Bate were capitandefenders. Ileesorn.in his new place at the wing made a. praise- worthy.debut. Webster with his tine running,:and tack, ling was a surprise for the Recs. which they had not been prepared for. JAley came out well, and Ashton,. Fair. Turner, Frank Turner, Nevins, and Thornily,. contributed very materially to the victory.</p>
        <p>The resalts of the competition stand thus until nemt Saturday :</p>
        <p>ran Agst.. rid. Won. Lost. Drr. G.T. G.T. rts. Swinton l3 . 9 . 2 . 2 ...1528,.. 3 5 -.20 Slaked Tyldesley St. Helens Recreation 14,... 5 ... 6 ... 3 _1112...10 9 ...13 Warrington Oldham l3 . 4 .. 6 . 3 , 7 14... 9 9 —ll Wigan lO, . 4 . 5 . 1 . 3 4 ... 58; ... 9 lircuighton Rangers lt iehdale Hornets [Broughton.</p>
        <p>I I notice that Latchford and the Rangers collided with each other on Saturday, to continue what is being called the one-horse struggle," over the Junior Leigue cham- pionship. Neither team scored a tangible point. It ap- pears to me that the enthusiasm in this competitionisn't ' becoming very sultry. Nobody seems particularly anxious to arrive at a decision, and I'm thinking that unless A little bit of "hustling" is done we shell have our junior teams courting sunstr2kes by playing in the middle of summer.</p>
        <p>The results stand thus at present : For Agst. Plyd. Won. Lost, Drn. U.T U.T. Pts. , Parish Church 5 ... 3 ... 2 ... 0 ... 3 S, ... 2 3 ... 6 Stockton Heath 5 ... 3 ... 2 ... 0 ... 3 3.... 2 3 ••• 6 St. Mary's Latchford Walkers' lk:ewery 4 ... 0 ... 4 ... 0 ... 0 0 ... 3 7 ...</p>
        <p>Arthur J. Gould must feel prouder now than at any other time in his life, for he has led Wales to victory in each of her International matches. kle is 28 years old, and has figured prominently in first-clasa football for the past ten years. Although so matured and ex- perienced in the game, the present season has been to him a marvellous one—he has scored over 40 tries, a record any three players collectively might envy. He isone of the very fasteat runners amongst our Rugby footballers. Although this is his most prominent quality he is one of the most unselfish of exponents of Rugby. His passes are perfection, and at the same time effective and as a wing helper he has no superior. As a member of the famous Newport Club, he is the mainstay of their grand combination, and in the midst of running, dodg- ing, and kicking, his judgment is clearand sound.</p>
        <p>A ssocwrios. The first tie in the Warrington and District Associa- tion competition will be played off to-day, Saturday, be- tween the Warrington Cricket A.F.C.,, and Padgate In- stitute, .n the ground of the former. The following will represent the Cricket Club.:!—Groal, J. Forest • full backs, .1. S. Francomb and W. IL Hannah ; half backs, T. Chidlaw, F. Williamson, and J. Dunn ; forwards, W. Reid, R. Fennelly. E. Connor, A. It Hoare, and J. Isher- wood. Referee, Colour-Sergeant .1. Walsh, Orford Barracks. Kick oft at three o'clock..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>MiscELLsszous.</head>
        <p>MiscELLsszous.</p>
        <p>The Orford Harriers indulged in their seven miles cross-country handicap on Saturday, and haven't sent word if they enjoyed it. Messrs. L. Scott and E. Hackett laid the trail through Orford and Winwick. Mr. L. Scott started the runners out the pace being very quick,H. GroUnds (sminutes), winning by 20 yards from H. Sinclair (10 mine, limit). P. Sinclair (8 minutes) be- ing third.</p>
        <p>Warrington cyclists move about with censiderable rapidity, and they have commented this season well by becoming omnipresent. That is, according to BORIS of our contemporaries. They stated that the W.C.C. and the W. Wheelers were present at the Cycle Parade in Liverpool on Tuesday. As a matter of fact they were 11 all in Warrington and didn't take part in the prods- [lion.</p>
        <p>Turner has been giving the audiences at the Theatre Royal displays of his strength during tlag weak. On Wednw.day he wrestled with Ross the famous Eng- HAI wrestler who was beliten by Cannon last week; bus he last the combat. On'Thursday he made up for it by beating J. C. Daly, the Irish champion.</p>
        <p>A little sixponny handbook and diary, which an be conveniently stowed away in the waistcoat pocket, has been issued by Messrs. George G. liussey and Co., of Peckham, London, and it will be of great utility to cricketers. It contains information relating to the summer game of wonderful variety and completeness, such as the result of county matches, battisg and bowl- ing averages; principal fixtures for 1893, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WOTtICHOUSE OFFICIALS AND THEIR</head>
        <p>WOTtICHOUSE OFFICIALS AND THEIR</p>
        <p>TEMPERS.</p>
        <p>At the Winchester Assizes, on Thursday, Mr. Justice Mathew heard a curious case of manslaughter against Robert John Lane, a porter of Lymington Union. It was proved that a boy, aged eleven' who had run away from the workhouse, was captured, and on being re- turned to the institution a disinfecting bath was ordered, as the boy was in a dirty conaion. It was alleged that the porter hada spite against tke boy, and poured the carbolic acid over him while in the bath. The boy died from the effects of the burns. After hear- ing the evidence, the Judge said that people in unions must be taught to control their tempers, and sentenced , him to twelve months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Karon MINEItS AND THE EIGHT HOURS BILL. . .</head>
        <p>Karon MINEItS AND THE EIGHT HOURS BILL. . .</p>
        <p>--- - At a national conference of Scotch miners at Hamilton On Thursday it was decided to have a series of demon- aerations in Ayrshire, Lanarkshire, and Stirlingshire, and a resolution was carried recommending the holding of a national miners' holiday for a week or a fortnight, to be followed afterwards by certain restric- tions as to time of working. A further resolution was carried eNpressing dissatisfaction with Mr. Gladstone's attitude towards the Miners' Eight Hours Bill, and re- peating the conviction that nothing less than the bill will be acceptable. It was stated that Mr. Gladstone's staunclicsr friends amongst the miners were dissatisfied because, while applyiug the numerical test to the dis, establishment questions he ignored, such prieciple as regards the Eight Hours Bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>AT OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>AT OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>TIIE REJTOR ON THE QUAKEItS,</p>
        <p>The Rector gave an address on Sunday night at the Parish Church on the Quakers. He said that this sect separated from the Church because they were against all ritual. He would ask his hearers to notice how the pendulum swung backwarde.and forwards in the matter of religion. We have the Independents or the Congregationalists in favour of a laxer discipline, and then the Romauists in favour a stricter discipline. Then we have the Baptists who be- lieved in an extreme ritual and the Quakers who were against all ritual ,and lastly we have Unitariani who took tha intellectual sine, and the Wesleyans who took more notice of tho feelings. In this respect, history repeats itself, as in the early times the pendulum frequently swung too far backwarde and forwards. Thus in the fourth and fifth centuries, there were the Arians who said that Jesus Christ was not God ; and the Apoolin- arians who said that Jesus was not man. The different forms of dissent in England arose owing to a failure of the Churchto fully recognise some great truth, and the church learnt something from each of the different sects. In reviewing the Quakers, or Friends,he would ant give a sketch of their history, secondly give some of their chief doctrines, and lastly, their virtues. The Quakers was the name by which the world called them, but they called themselves Friends. The name Quaker was given them„ owing to their founder speaking of their quaking in the presence of the Lord. Their founder was a young man named George Fox, horn about the time of Charles L ascended the throne of England. He came of humble parents, and was deeply in earnest, always anxious to know the truth and do the right, a religious enthusiast without much education. It was just at the time when Charles had been defeated at Naseby in 1848, that George Fox was wandering about the lanes near Coventry with the Bible in his hand and the Holy Spirit in hie heart. It was just at the time when it seemed as if the church would have been overthrown by the Presbyterians, Independents, and Baptists, when this man caused a diversion in its favour by the attempt to produce a greater spirituality. England was sick of Rome, the Puritans, and even of Laudism, with its strict enforcement of minutim. The creed of Fox was " I believe in the Holy Ghost." This Society of Friends became crystallised into a seca, and among its support- ers were Bailey and Penn. Terrible were the persecu- tions that the Quakers endured both in this country and in America for their refusal to take oaths, their refusal to pay tithes, for not showing outward respect for the magistrates, and of being suspected of what men called Fifth Monarchy principles. He would now consider their doctrine. First they taught that the revelation of God with Jesus Christ is by the Spirit,and that the Holy Scriptures are only secondary. Secondly, and we may be thankful to them for it, they taught as a protest against Calvinism, the universal redemption of man- kind by Jesus Christ. Thirdly, they taught the import- ance of a holy life. Fourthly, they taught that a uni- versity degree and career could uot make a minister, and they were quite right. A university career is very valuable, and he, (the rector) always wished to have clergy with him who had had a university education. But the Quakers were right in not considering this as necessary, but they were wrong when they taught that there was no need of a human commission, and that any man could be a minister. The teaching of the church was very different,as it taught the absolute necessity of ordination. No matter how able or holy a man may be, his ability and holiness do not make him a minister un- less he is appointed by those who are authorised to give the outward form. They were also opposed to sacra- ments and other forms to which the church attached great importance. Coming to their virtues he would first mention their philanthropy, their love of their fellow creatures, of which he could speak from personal know- ledge. Then there was their abhorrence of slavery. They took an active part in freeing the slaves. Lastly, there was their hatred of war, and their,love of peace, and their opposition to a standing army. The subject next week will be the Unitarians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>MAYOR AND CORPORATION AT CHURCH.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND CORPORATION AT CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Commation and officials of Warring- ton attended divine service at the Parish Church on Sunday Morning. The members of. the Corporation met at the Town Hall about ten o'clock, and, headed by a large number of police, under the direction of the Chief Conatable, followed by the firemen directed by Superin- tendent Turner, marched through Sankey-st., Butter- market-st., and Church-at., to St. Elphin's. To the right of the Mayor walked Alderman Francomb (deputy- mayor), and on his left the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), and there were also in the procession Aldermen Wright, and Blackhurst, Councillors .1. Smethurst, J. Muir, R. T. Fairclough, James Fairclou-h, W. .1. Shaw, Robert Heaton, J. Hill, J. Ilepherd, Shaw Green, 'I. Hutchinson; John Fairhurst, (borough treasurer), T. Longden (borough surveyor), George P. F. White (deputy magistrates' clerk), Charles Madeley (librarian and curator), %V. S. Haddock (gas engineer), J. P. Leigh (cemetery registrar), E. Aukland, H. Tort, Alfred Jolley and William Lawless. Arrived at the church gates a halt was made to await the arrival°, the churchwardens and sidesmen' who headed the procession to the church door. Here the Rector and the assistant clergy were in attendance, and they preceded the civic body into the church. Mere was a large congregation and the ser- mon was preached by the Rev. C. E. Jackson. THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE. On Sunday afternoon the second weeting of the P.S.A., in connection with Wycliffe Church, was held ! under the presidency of Mr. W. Owen, J.P. There was a large attendance of men. The Chairman said it Wag the first opportunity he had obtained to address a num- ber of men on religions matters, as a layman of the Established Church had few opportunities of doing so. He afterwards delivered a brief addzess to the men.Coun- cillor A. Parks J.P. (chairman of the Ashton-under-Lyne P.S.A.) gave a short history of the working or the P.S.A. in that town, and he said the attendance was never less than twelve ot thirteen hundred men. He said it was the movement which would iell most truly upon this century. Most of the great social questions would not be settled between poitical parties but would be settled by the moral stamina of the workingmen. Some critics had remarked that it would draw scholars from the Sunday 'Moots but his experience proved to the con- trary. He looked upon the moveim-nt as a centre of activity. They had a saving bank in connection with the movement, and the men were saving at the rate of £5OO a month. They had also a labour bureau, lectures, concerts, , and ambulance classes. He thought they tended to the moral and spiritu well-being of the men. Their members attended at the average of 95 per cent. During the afternoon Mrs. R. H. Pearson sang in a most excellent manner." Come unto me" (Coe- nen) and also the solo " I heard the voice of Jesus saying." A nutnbor of members were enrolled at the close Of the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FAA:ME NT OF ?EMBERS OF PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>THE FAA:ME NT OF ?EMBERS OF PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>STATEMENT BY MR. GLADSTONE.</p>
        <p>In the HOUR:, of Commons yesterday, Mr, Rober Wallace asked the First Lord of the Treasury whether he could state if the Government intended by legislation Budget arrangements, or otherwise to etve practical effect to the principle of the payment or members of Parliament. Mr. Gladstone replied that he had no hesitation in saying that in his opinion legislation was the only method by which a question of this magnitude could be disposed of. With r(ard to this question, he had al- ready communicated With two depittattoos of members of Parliament, who felt strongly with respect to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>YEAR'S BREWING.</head>
        <p>A YEAR'S BREWING.</p>
        <p>It appears from a return issued on Wednesday that the malt and corn used in the manufacture of beer last year in the United Kingdom amounted to 55.852,570 bushels, the sugar used in brewing to 2,096.720 cwt.,and the" licence duty paid, beer duty charged" amounted to £10,079,678. It also appears that London is the largest brewing centre, consuming 10,956,363 bushels of malt and corn. and haying 139 breweries ; and Burton comes next with a consumption of 6,228,906 bushels.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>COLONEL WARBURTON AND HIS TENANTS*</head>
        <p>COLONEL WARBURTON AND HIS TENANTS*</p>
        <p>Mr. Jordan will on Mondays in the Rouse of Commons ask the Secretary of State for War if he has seen the report of an interview with Colonel Warbut ton, in which it is allegel he said he would require in future that any new tenant coming to his estate must be a Churchman and that he must also join his yeomanry. Considering that Per Majesty's forces are recruited by volunteer enlistment, is it comnetent for Colonel Warburton to I'o press men into his troop,and will he inquire further into this CNC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>'ED E RATION AND THE 4LOCAL VETO.</head>
        <p>'ED E RATION AND THE 4LOCAL VETO.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON TEMPERANCE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>MLL.</head>
        <p>MLL.</p>
        <p>A spPcia/ meeting of the Warrington Temperance Federation was held yesterday afternoon, at the Horsemarket Cocoa Booms, under the presidcsey of the Rev. Johp Yonge.. There was a very large and representative attendance, embracing all the temperance organisatione of the town and immedi- ate district. The followingresolution waS adoptectl:: —" That this meeting empress its great satisfactiOn at theintroduction by Her Majesty's Government, into th'e Rouse of Commons of a measure embody+. ing in tr. considerable degree the principles of the populerveto in the issue and renewal of licensetn- and pledgesthome present to use their utmost en,- deavours.to aid the government in carrying thei bill through Phrlitament." It was further resolved that copies of the foregoing resolution be sent to Mr. Gladetune,.Sir Wm, Harcourt, Mr. Asquith, Sir Wilffid. Lawson and the member for the borough.</p>
      </div>
