      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>"-BEFOGGED " PRISONER.</head>
        <p>A "-BEFOGGED " PRISONER.</p>
        <p>A "SCENE "'' AT TIIR BOROUGH COURT.</p>
        <p>At the Borough •Court, yesterday,. Wm. McMutrie, 55, Hamilton-st., was charged with assaulting his wife Ellen McMittrie, on the 51sh inst. Prisoner, who appeared in a.very °melted Condition, said, as soon as he was put in the dock, "your Worships, my counsel hasreftsed, to, Dleadi for sae, but I will plead myself with your permission,' , On being asked if was his wife he said :--" Well, I took her to the altar." When ths testament Was given-to his•wife foster to be sworn , 14cMutrie cried out excitedly and bringing his bands together with emphasis; and, his eyes turned upwards," Ellen ! Mien Prionif kis&amp; that book ! You know you are going.to felines." The wife briefit,, stated the facts of the case, saying that she wished for s, separation. Defend- ant continued shouting and interrupting and on being asked 'by. Mt. Hunt to be quiet, be said, " Well, Mr: hunt have you over been in sach a pre- dicament as I anak, in. nowa The mother-in-law, whose tiaras ia•Nirs.T6gg,.was then called, prisoner saying that it was a pity he had got amongst foggs. Prisoner was so noisy and unmanageable that the magistrates considered him unfit to be tried then, find ordered himdo be kept locked up all night to appear to-day (Saturday). Defendant's mother was in court, and after her son Was taken away, she begged of Mn Hunt "not to abuse. her son."</p>
        <p>SALE OFISHIitE HORSES AT SUTTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon a large company assembled at the Stud Farm, Sutton Bridge,. on the occasion of the disposal of the stud %:;.f shire horses belonging to Mr. S.B. Chadwick, of Crofton Lodge, Higher Runcorn, by Mr. Sexton, of the firer of,Mensrs. Sexton and Gritnwade, of Colchester.. Although v the whole of the animals were sold without reserve,.it is interesting to know that Mr. Chadwick, who has already greatly encouraged the breeding of animals.of- a. high clear in the county, does not intend tn abandon the laudable pursuit ; but rather provide more accommodation in aceordance with modern requirements. The catalogue, included thirty-five animals, some of which were splendidly selected mares and yearlings and two-year olds, which had been gathered together with extreme- care. The arrangements for the sale-were all that could be desired. A4large marquee had been. erectliil by Mr. Cooper, of Runcorn,,and beside the ring was a commod- ious grand-stand. 'rhese had been carried out by Mr Walter Taylor (bailiftat the farw4, and Mr. William Ball (stud groom),:Onder the supervislon of Councillor Shaw- Green, of Warrington. An excellent luncheon was partaken of by abeub 500`gentIhmen, the caterers for which were Messrs. Galt an&amp; Capper, of Lord-street, Liverpool. The chair was occupied by the Rev, John B. Hughes, of Citeswardine Vicarage, Market Drayton, and various toasts were proposed„ including "The hGnt Mr. Chadwick. Afterwards the sale proceeded briskly and the,prices-realised were considered good.- The amount secured was 2;467 guineas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>,GOODWILL OF A WELSH HOTEL</head>
        <p>THE ,GOODWILL OF A WELSH HOTEL</p>
        <p>MR. JUSTICE CAVE ON BREWERS AND CONFISCATION.</p>
        <p>At the Cheater assizes on Wednesday, Rushford Thom- win, ,wine and spirit dealer, Greenhill, Holywell, Flint- shire, sued Henry Clough, the present tenant oftthe Wynnstay Arms Hotel, Ithvl„ for £144 7s. 6d. money due. Mr. R. Bankes. was for the plaintiff and Mt F. Marshall for the defndant. Baldwin became tenant of the hotel mr the 13th February, 1892, under Mossrs. Greenall,, the- Warrington brewers. As the house had not been doing good business, his agreement was for a yearly tenancy at the rate of £lO a year, the landlords agreeing to,pa,y the rates and taxes for the, first year. , Baldwin succeeded in working up a good conzaiction, and in Jely the takings had increased over £5O a month. At the end of July, wishing to retire from the wholesale business,,the plaintiff disposed of the lease to Clough, who signed an agreement to pay £lOO for goodwill, and to take the furniture and stock at a valuation. The action was brought to recover £144 whick remained unpaid.. The defence was that the defendant had been induced to enter into thecontract by false repretTentat ions to 7,hemTect (1) that the rent was £lO free from taxes, rates,,and gas, (2) that the takings of the house averaged £29a week, aud (3) that any money defendant paid for , goodwill could he recovered from Messrs. Greerall on ) his leaving. Defendant further stated that three or four weeka after entering the house he discovered that the. spirit bottles in the windows were filled wits water,and a stock of packets of tobacco with sawdast.—Joseph. Hartley, the North Wales agent for the Wilderspoor Brewery, said when Clough signed the aiseement with, Messrs. Greenall he was distinctly told he-would have to pay the rates, and that he could not get back the £loa goodwill. Plaintiff ought not to bave had the money for the goodwill.—The Judge : When the tenant gets up the value of a house do you confiscate, the additional value of the property ?—Witness: Wedb.—The Judge it is about the coolest thing I have heard in nay life. Ido not wonder landlords are not popular in i Wales if this is the way they conduct themselves. Are you sure you are authorised to say this for your pritici- : pals P—Witmess : Yes; there is no such thing as good- will recognised in our firm.—The Judge: Then I repeat :it is a most disgraceful thing to confiscate a man's labour and put the money into your own pocket.—Wit- ness: Wo should never take the house from him so tong as he behaved himself. He could keep the house for-ever. His Lordship, in summing up, sae it was outrageous and monstrous for brewers to confiscate the value of a tenant's labour, especially as the brewers themselves were at present going up and down the country com- plaining about their own property being confiscated. If there was any confiscation at all, he would sooner see their property confiscated by the Government than their tenant's property confiscated by them. Confiscation which benefited the public was manifestly very much better than that which benefited the brewer.—A verdict was given for the plaintiff for the full amount claimed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>COUNTY MAGISTRATES 1N LAN CASHIRE. •</head>
        <p>COUNTY MAGISTRATES 1N LAN CASHIRE. •</p>
        <p>STRONG ACTION BY THE GOVERNMENT. In the House of Commons, on Thursday, Mr. Leer asked the Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster if her was now prepared to make a statement with reference to his action regarding the nomination of the couaty magistrates in Lancashire, and if he woudet lay upon the table of the House his correspondence with the Lord- Lieutenant upon the subject, as well as the memoran— dum of 1870. Mr. Bryce: Haying received many complaints re'" gutting the composition of the County Bench of Lau- cashire,and the great disparity in the representation of political parties thereon, and having natisfied myself that they are well founded, I called the attention of the Lord Lieutenant to this disparity,.. intimating that 31 was unwilling, in spite of numerous' sippeals, to depart from existing practice, , though Of recent origin, and suggesting to bim,,,,the pre- priety of taking steps to, diminish the disparity. I :laid before him the names of certain gentlemen to be recere"' mended to me for appointment as magistrates. De" 'however' absolutely declined to entertain the suggestion. ,Lhave, therefore, felt myself obliged to revoke the' memorandum of 1870, by which the right of recele" inendation was entrusted to the Lord-Lieutenant--- (31inisterial eimers)—and have decided to revert to ,the practice previously in force, under which the Chancellor ofthe Duchy received recommendations from various ' cireirterarand not from the Lord-Lieutenant only. There Nyilidae no objection to producing the memorandum ' referred +to and the one I have had issued at once if the bon: member' will move for them. In reply to a further question by Mr. Legh, alti.Brvesexpressed his anxiety to havn, the subject discussed in the House. He stated in reply to oiher members that the papers containing the- names sub- mitted would be regarded as confidential, but those he, wasprepared to submit to the House weadd be quite smKeient.</p>
        <p>AVollier named Alfred Stockton was ,remanded at Pontypool:ea Wednesday on a char,e of causing the deathwf another collier named Lewis, with whom he had fought me the subject of Welsh disestriblisisment.,</p>
